\par\par \b \uc2 BIS-Sukhdave Singh: Global RMB - challenges and implications\b0\par\par\uc2 1,715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2014\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Release date - 21082014\par \par \uc2 Keynote speech by \uc2 Dr Sukhdave Singh\uc2 , Deputy Governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malaysia (\uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2 ), at the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Forum: China Globalising: RMB Rising (Malaysia) - \u8216\'20\'18Global RMB: Challenges and Implications\u8217\'20\'19, Kuala Lumpur, 12 August 2014.\par \par \uc2 It is an honour and pleasure to be here with you this morning to talk about what is likely to be the most important development affecting the global monetary system since the introduction of euro. Should the RMB become a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will transform the global financial landscape. In fact, the RMB could potentially define this century, in the same manner that the US dollar dominated the previous century.\par \par \uc2 In my remarks today, I would like to share some thoughts on the potential for the RMB to achieve the status of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Where it to do so, what will the potential impact be on the global monetary system? Finally, I will touch on the implications for Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 The world is ready, is China ready?\par \par \uc2 From where we stand today, the world is clearly ready for a global RMB. The question is whether China itself is ready.\par \par \uc2 There is no question that the RMB would become an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The only question is how big of an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Will the RMB\u8217\'20\'19s role in the international trade and finance reflect the status of China as the second largest, and soon to be the largest, economy in the world?\par \par \uc2 To be an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not take much. You just need to lower the barriers and allow for it to be traded outside your borders. The Malaysian ringgit was an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before we de-internationalised it during the Asian Financial Crisis. At its peak, the ringgit accounted for about 18% of Malaysia\u8217\'20\'19s trade settlements, which was significant given the high trade orientation of the Malaysian economy. However, what we learnt from the crisis in 1997 was that for our small open economy, the internationalisation of the ringgit was inconsistent with maintaining macroeconomic and financial stability, and the benefits were not commensurate with the risks. China is not a small economy but like Malaysia it is a developing economy. Therefore, it is not immune to the types of vulnerabilities an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the accompanying open capital account can create in its immature economy with its immature financial system.\par \par \uc2 The rapid momentum of the current RMB internationalisation reflects China\u8217\'20\'19s strong position in global trade. However, it also reflects a safe phase of RMB internationalisation. It is comparable to wading in shallow water, where you can see the bottom of the sea, anticipate what hazards may lie on the sea floor, and therefore are able to walk rapidly. The real difficult part is when you get into deeper waters, where the risks are less visible, the waves are higher, the resistance of the water stronger. Progress can then be expected to be slower and more cautious.\par \par \uc2 As the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a developing economy, the road to internationalisation of the RMB will have many potholes, and it would be the skills and resilience of the policymakers in China that will determine whether the RMB manages to navigate these hazards and travels far on the road to internationalisation. After all, we can think of other more developed countries, including another at one time second-largest economy, which upon reaching the threshold on which China stands today, decided that the costs that come with having their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  playing a big international role were something that they did not want to bear. For lack of official support, these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  consequently never achieved their full global potential.\par \par \uc2 Would the reality be different for China? Only time will tell. It is the case that the RMB will sail more smoothly and speedily in international waters if its sails are filled by the strong winds of robust Chinese economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Five key pre-conditions to being a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 So allow me to look at some of the key fundamentals that I believe would support a sustainable progress of the RMB towards being a major global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Studying the history of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the British pound and the US dollar provides us some indication of the possible path of RMB internationalisation. Let me be clear, this does not mean that I am implying that the RMB must necessarily follow the path of other major global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Nevertheless, such reflection does provide an opportunity for a realistic assessment of the prospects and challenges that lie ahead for the RMB. There are at least 5 key factors. Let me list them without going into the details.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that rise to dominance are often of countries that are dominant in global trade.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  often become internationalised because the country is a key global exporter of capital.\par \par \uc2 Every successful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had official support.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only rise to international prominence when backed by liberalised and developed markets.\par \par \uc2 There needs to be confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economy.\par \par \uc2 How does China fare against these signposts? I would say that it is three out of five. As far as trade dominance is concerned, China surpassed the US economy as the largest goods trading nation in 2013.\par \par \uc2 The internationalisation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite its cautious nature, is certainly getting a lot of official support - not just from Chinese policymakers but also from policymakers in other countries.\par \par \uc2 As for confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economy, there would not be so much enthusiasm for RMB internationalisation were such confidence missing. The fact that countries are queuing up to be able to trade RMB within their borders, or to gain access to China\u8217\'20\'19s domestic financial markets, shows a strong desire on the part of these countries to participate in what they see as an economy with a potential for continued strong growth. There is a deep hunger internationally for Chinese consumers, Chinese exports, Chinese assets and of course, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While the USD was at one time supported by its gold backing, the RMB today is backed by China\u8217\'20\'19s huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is further enhanced by expectations of conditions in China remaining supportive of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The main precondition on which China does not do well is in the stage of development and readiness of its financial system and policy frameworks.\par \par \uc2 These challenges are not insurmountable but they are big enough to make Chinese policymakers adopt a gradual and sequenced approach to RMB internationalisation. They are big enough that premature liberalisation could create significant risks to macroeconomic and financial stability. The challenge for Chinese policymakers is to develop the domestic financial system to a level where integration with the global financial system would not lead to significant vulnerabilities. Therefore, the state of the financial system will be a key determinant of the extent to which policymakers are willing to liberalise and open their system, and consequently, will also be a key determinant of the extent and pace of RMB internationalisation. The global status of the RMB is closely linked to the global status of the Chinese financial system. Ambitious reforms could expedite RMB internationalisation.\par \par \uc2 On the issue of being a global exporter of capital, China is certainly that. However, a large part of these outflows have been official and they have not been in RMB. This in fact touches at the heart of what is known as the Triffin Dilemma. China has been sending USDs abroad because that is what it has been receiving through its current account surpluses and FDI inflows. Sometime in the future, China may develop a structural current account deficit, but I do not see that happening in the foreseeable future. Opening up the capital account and domestic financial system to non-resident investors could lead to even more foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  capital inflows. On both accounts, China will not only continue to export USD capital but could find itself confronted by persistent appreciating pressure on its exchange rate due to the high demand for RMB.\par \par \uc2 Promoting RMB for trade settlement will only go so far in resolving this. If more of China\u8217\'20\'19s trade is settled in RMB, less USD will flow in, and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less and would have less USD to send abroad. But the question remains, with more RMB flowing in through the current and capital account, how do you get more RMB out of China in the first place?\par \par \uc2 There are in fact numerous possibilities for China to create a significant pool of RMB liquidity abroad without necessarily running a current account deficit. This include RMB outflows through official aid, international loans by banks in China, foreign issuance of debt in Chinese markets, direct investment abroad, inter-company loans, repatriation of corporate profits, resident spending abroad and so on. Some of these are already happening. Offshore RMB balances in Hong Kong, Chinese Taipei and Singapore for example rose almost 40% between late 2013 and early 2014 to RMB1.4 trillion.\par \par \uc2 These numbers are indeed encouraging, except for one thing. When the RMB is abroad, it gets homesick, terribly homesick. It yearns to return home to the land of its birth. When the legal avenues for it to do so are limited, it desperately seeks other ways to do so. And as the size of overseas RMB pool increases, the pressure of the homeward bound flows will also increase. Will the Qualified Investor Schemes, RMB clearing banks and the eventual introduction of the \uc2 China International\uc2  Payment System alleviate this situation of the offshore RMB? Yes, it would, but not entirely.\par \par \uc2 In the current situation, despite the sharp increase in RMB related trade and investment transactions, the data I have seen suggests that the use of RMB remains rather lopsided and favours the flow of RMB into China rather than out of China. A significantly greater proportion of China\u8217\'20\'19s exports were paid for in RMB, then its imports. Similarly, while 63% of the FDI into China was in RMB, only 16% of the direct investment out of China was in RMB.\par \par \uc2 What all these lead us to is that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be a true\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020140822ea8m000km\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
