\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: As U.S. Calls In Air Strikes in Iraq, Markets Slump -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 1,054 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2014\par 13:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 WRAP: Stocks markets around the world have slumped even further, and safe-haven plays such as German bunds and the yen have gained again, as news of U.S. air strikes in Iraq added to overall concerns about global conflict and signs of soft growth in Europe and China. After months of pretty much shrugging its shoulders in the face of escalating geopolitical tensions in Russia/Ukraine, Israel/West Bank and Iraq itself, the fact that the advances of Islamist militants in Iraq could drag the U.S. so quickly back into that country's problems is disturbing enough to push investors over the edge. But if it weren't for the backdrop of concern that central banks such as the Federal Reserve and the Bank of England are preparing possible rate increases at a moment when the global economy still seems fragile, perhaps investors would be more resilient in response to this news. In the full context, however, it makes for an unsettling environment in which to try to put money to work in markets whose valuations were, until recently, looking stretched in some segments. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S. President Obama authorized targeted airstrikes and emergency assistance missions in northern Iraq in a bid to help Iraqi and Kurdish troops contain advances by Islamist militants.\par \par \uc2 The alarming advances by the Islamist group ISIS, including reports that it had seized the Mosul Dam--a vital source of both water and hydropower for large sections of Iraq--have comes as a shock to the world. The concern now is whether U.S. forces will be dragged deeper into a conflict that it was supposed to have withdrawn from some years ago. It is as if that withdrawal was never really possible in the first place. And with ISIS holding designs over Syria as well, the scope of American involvement could be even greater. This comes at a time of widening geopolitical concerns across the world, too, alongside the hot spots of Ukraine and Israel. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: The politically sensitive trade surplus widened to a record $47.3 billion in July from $31.6 billion in June, well above the $27.7 billion economists projected, as exports to the U.S., Europe and Southeast Asia surged while imports slumped. Exports were up 14.5% year-over-year, nearly double the 8% growth expected, whereas imports fell 1.6% following a 5.5% on-year expansion in June. The WSJ survey had predicted a 3% rise.\par \par \uc2 Such a glaring shift in the trade balance threatens to reignite trade tensions with the U.S. The fact that it comes after a period in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had actively moved to weaken the yuan or contain its ascent won't help in that regard. However, the shift could simply reflect the fact that there has been a rebound in the rest of the world's economy while China's remains sluggish. And the latter isn't good news for the global economy in any case.(MC)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN; The current-account balance fell into a deficit of Yen399.1 billion ($3.91 billion) in June, the first negative reading in five months as robust income from overseas investments wasn't enough to make up for ongoing trade deficits.\par \par \uc2 Although the data are from a different period, they stand in stark contrast to China's and offer a reminder that outside demand really isn't that strong for Asian exporters, which in turn could enhance the thesis that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surging exports stem from its exchange-rate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than anything else. In the case of the yen, this could eventually bring downward pressure on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as current-account deficits put Japan's giant debt-servicing needs at risk. For now, though, geopolitical concerns and global market weakness are likely to maintain demand for the yen, given its traditional role as a safe haven. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.K.: The trade deficit widened to GBP2.5 billion from GBP2.4 billion in May, as goods exports declined more rapidly than imports and were insufficient to offset a widening surplus in services.\par \par \uc2 The data confirm the picture of the U.K. economy as being out of synch with much of the rest of the world. Domestic demand has recovered spectacularly in Britain, but with the pound strong, its exporters continue to struggle. (MC)\par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIA: The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  lowered its calendar 2014 forecast for growth to 2.5% from 2.75% previously and now sees inflation at 2.25% instead of 2.5% for the same period.\par \par \uc2 Although the quarterly statement foresees ongoing "stability" in interest rates, the downgraded assessment of the economic outlook will inevitably raise speculation that the RBA is contemplating another cut in its benchmark rate, which is now at a record low 2.5%. The report also reflects how significant the mining sector slowdown has been on the back an easing in demand from China. (MC)\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL: 8 a.m. EDT. (9 a.m., Sao Paulo) July consumer price index. [CPI was +6.52% on-year in June.]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's bugaboo is its inflation rate. And at 6.52% last month, it was still above the top end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target band despite the country's interest rates being among the highest in the world. What Brazil needs is structural reform to bring imbedded inflation down, not tighter monetary policy, which is hurting growth. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: 9:30 p.m. EDT (9:30 a.m. Saturday, Beijing) July producer price index. [PPI previously -1.1% on-year.]\par \par \uc2 While China's vast mountain of bank debts and dubious investments clearly fuels nervousness at the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can at least breathe easy when it comes to inflation. Both producer and consumer prices are far from being inflationary, which means the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has some room to deliver monetary stimulus and encourage targeted credit delivery so long as the latter doesn't get into the hands of heavily overleveraged state-owned enterprises. (MC) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 08, 2014 07:26 ET (11:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140808ea880022w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
