\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China posts record trade surplus, but domestic economy may lag\b0\par\par\uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2014\par 08:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * July exports +14.5 pct y/y vs +7.5 pct forecast               \par \par \uc2 * July imports -1.6 pct y/y vs +3.0 pct forecast               \par \par \uc2 * Trade surplus $47.3 bln vs $27 bln forecast               \par \par \uc2 * Policy support seen needed to bolster domestic demand   (Adds details, official comment)               \par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao and Xiaoyi Shao               \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Aug 8 (Reuters) - China's buoyant exports pushed its trade surplus to a record in July, fuelling optimism global demand will help counter pressure on the domestic economy from a weakening property sector.               \par \par \uc2 While manufacturing appears to have picked up in the world's second-largest economy, unexpected weakness in the services sector this week has renewed concerns about the growth outlook. The weak housing market remains China's biggest risk, posing a drag on the broader economy and investor confidence.               \par \par \uc2 Recovering global demand may not be enough to bolster a weak internal economy weighed by a cooling property sector and Beijing's anti-corruption drive, suggesting policy support will likely continue to keep economic growth on track, analysts say.               \par \par \uc2 Exports in July jumped 14.5 percent from a year earlier - the fastest pace in 15 months, the General Administration of Customs said on Friday, doubling from 7.2 percent in June and roundly beating market expectations. Exports were stronger than expected even after pricing in inflated export data in early 2013, when firms falsified invoices to skirt capital curbs.               \par \par \uc2 Some analysts attributed the export spurt to delayed shipments caused by recent volatility in the yuan which may not sustain. Meanwhile, imports fell 1.6 percent versus a rise of 5.5 percent in June, leaving the country with a record trade surplus of $47.3 billion for the month.               \par \par \uc2 "The (export) data indicates very strong demand externally and less need for a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB in Hong Kong.               \par \par \uc2 "However, imports contracted 1.6 percent year-on-year, indicating soft domestic demand and a downward pressure on growth. Policymakers are likely to do more to support the domestic economy."               \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll had predicted a 7.5 percent rise in exports, a 3 percent increase in imports and a trade surplus of $27 billion.               \par \par \uc2 Financial markets firmed on the data with the Shanghai Composite Index rebounding. It rose 0.25 percent by midday from its intraday low, when it was down 0.32 percent.                              \par \par \uc2 STRONG EXPORTS, WEAK IMPORTS               \par \par \uc2 After a weak start this year, China's exports have shown signs of improvement helped by stronger global growth as well as supportive domestic policies and the effects of a weaker yuan.               \par \par \uc2 Exports to the United States, China's top export destination, rose 12.3 percent in June, quickening from a rise of 7.5 percent in June, while those to the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the second-biggest market, grew 17 percent, compared with 13.1 percent in June.               \par \par \uc2 Exports to \uc2 ASEAN\uc2  countries rose 11.9 percent in July, accelerating from 9.7 percent in June, the customs data showed.               \par \par \uc2 Customs spokesman Zheng Yuesheng told state television that China exports are likely to stay strong in the coming months.               \par \par \uc2 "The external demand is improving as a recovery in major  developed countries underpins the global economy," he said.               \par \par \uc2 Imports from China's largest source for resources, Australia, dropped 5.7 percent, partly due to softer commodity prices but the fall adds to questions over the health of domestic demand.               \par \par \uc2 Some analysts attributed weak imports to the crackdown on  commodity financing following the Qingdao port fraud probe.               \par \par \uc2 Combined exports and imports grew 2 percent in the first seven months from a year earlier, trailing far behind the government's full-year target of 7.5 percent.               \par \par \uc2 The record trade surplus and pressure from capital inflows were seen renewing pressure on the yuan. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised for its third consecutive day of gains as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled it was comfortable with stronger levels as the economy was improving.               \par \par \uc2 "With Chinese economic activity improving, we believe that there is scope for RMB to further appreciate, driven by conversion of onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits and increased foreign portfolio inflows," analysts at ANZ said in a note.               \par \par \uc2 That could create a fresh headache for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year when it punished speculators betting on one-way yuan appreciation.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese leaders have pledged to maintain pro-growth policies to help achieve the annual growth target of 7.5 percent.               \par \par \uc2 The government unveiled a burst of "targeted" policy stimulus since April, including cutting reserve requirements for some banks, hastening construction of railways and public housing and allowing local governments to loosen property curbs.               \par \par \uc2 The Politburo, a top decision-making body of the ruling \uc2 Communist Party\uc2 , said last month that China must maintain a "certain speed" in its development over the long term to help resolve problems in the economy               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-8T07:55:12.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140808ea88000yw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
