\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CHINA \highlight\uc2  MONEY-PBOC cuts back on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , but not totally hands off\b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2014\par 10:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Firms retain more dollars on hand after yuan fall               \par \par \uc2 * Focus shifts to corporate dollar demand from policy factors               \par \par \uc2 * PBOC cuts frequency of large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * But traders expect continuing daily guidance on yuan               \par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Pete Sweeney               \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, July 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reduced heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, removing a thorny issue in relations among the world's top two economies, but traders say the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 isn't about to take its hands off the wheel.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese firms, including banks, have increasingly held on to dollars as capital inflows into China slow as a result of slowing economic growth and particularly after the PBOC let the yuan surprisingly fall in the first four months of 2014.               \par \par \uc2 The rough balance of dollar supply and demand in the market as a result of these developments will enable the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce the frequency of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the yuan's exchange rate in future.               \par \par \uc2 But the PBOC -- with a trading room of its own in the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System and often acting via state banks -- is likely to keep quoting the yuan every day and conducting routine trading through which it can guide its movements and prevent its value from running out of control, traders said.               \par \par \uc2 "All data points to evidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has indeed reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into trading to influence the value of the yuan in recent months," said Liu Dongliang, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shanghai.               \par \par \uc2 "Market anticipation of the yuan's future value has changed a lot after the yuan's unexpected depreciation earlier this year, with firms more reluctant to sell dollars they earn.               \par \par \uc2 "Still, the PBOC's daily activity will last at least for another couple of years as the market learns to operate without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's guidance, among other reasons."               \par \par \uc2 PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan said this month during the annual U.S.-China Strategic and Economic Dialogue that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "significantly" reduce its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when some prerequisites are met. U.S. officials say \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  deliberately holds down the yuan to boost its exports, an accusation China denies.                              \par \par \uc2 WHAT DOES DATA SHOW?               \par \par \uc2 The PBOC engineered a fall in the yuan earlier this year, guiding it down by as much as 3.4 percent by the end of April, to dampen speculative bets on one-way yuan appreciation.               \par \par \uc2 Since May, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shifted to holding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady, suggesting that its drive to guide the yuan lower was largely over, although the yuan is still down 2.5 percent so far this year.               \par \par \uc2 China's trade surplus widened sharply to a five-year high of $35.9 billion in May from April's $18.5 billion, but Chinese banks posted a surplus of only $6.8 billion in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements for their clients in May -- the smallest in 10 months and the fourth straight month of falls.               \par \par \uc2 That indicates that Chinese corporates are retaining much of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, partly reflecting changed market views of the yuan's future value, traders said.               \par \par \uc2 "Reflecting the new trend, the market has now shifted its attention more toward real dollar demand from corporate orders and away from policy factors," said a dealer at a European bank in Shanghai.               \par \par \uc2 As a result, the PBOC and commercial banks together bought just 38.7 billion yuan ($6.22 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a net basis in May, down from 116.9 billion yuan in April, the lowest level since last September. The dip reflects less capital inflows into China, traders said.               \par \par \uc2 More surprisingly, the PBOC's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets only edged up 361 million yuan in May, down from an already thin 84.6 billion yuan rise seen in April, the lowest growth since July last year. The increase and decrease in its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets reflect its dollar purchases and sales in the yuan market.               \par \par \uc2 Compared with overall banks' dollar purchases, the virtually flat PBOC \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets show that commercial banks have also tended to keep more dollars on hand.               \par \par \uc2 "These data points are supported by others," said a trader at a major Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.               \par \par \uc2 "Banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits and loans have also showed corporates increasingly retaining dollars as assets while denominating liabilities in yuan."               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-7-18T09:45:07.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140718ea7i007y8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
