\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-UPDATE 2-Home prices not China c.bank's main priority -Zhou\b0\par\par\uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2014\par 09:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Zhou: China c.bank watches GDP, inflation more closely than home prices\par \par \uc2 * Says China c.bank "not very experienced" in dealing with home prices\par \par \uc2 * Believes China can free interest rates market in two years (Repeats to fix formatting issue)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, July 10 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pays more attention to inflation and economic growth outlooks than developments in the property market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Managing China's housing market has been a challenge for policymakers in the world's second-biggest economy this year.\par \par \uc2 While the government would like the market to cool, so as to temper record home prices, it fears a property slowdown may get out of hand and hurt the wider economy.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on the sidelines of high-level talks between the United States and China, Zhou said the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 lacked experience in dealing with the housing market and should therefore exercise caution.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  takes macro-prudential measures to manage demand in the market property and guide financing for property developers," Zhou told reporters at a briefing. "But the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pays more attention to the trend in the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 "We are not very experienced in dealing with the property market - we haven't experienced too much cyclical volatility.\par \par \uc2 "So I think we should be more cautious in making judgment on the situation. For example, the judgment on whether supply has significantly exceeded demand. We should be prudent and need some time to watch," Zhou added.\par \par \uc2 Official data showed that China's housing prices fell for the first time in two years in May.\par \par \uc2 Zhou, who said that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  also planned to significantly reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when conditions were ripe, also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believed it could free up the country's interest rates market within two years.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , as the main government agency on rates reform, we should have an internal work timetable," he said.\par \par \uc2 "But the fulfillment of the timetable depends on external conditions - both the domestic and international economy. We believe that we will be able to achieve the goal within two years."\par \par \uc2 To protect banks' profits, China caps deposit rates at 110 percent of a benchmark level set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But critics say the ceiling distorts the price of credit, and helps to fuel wasteful investment funded by artificially cheap loans that has led to a massive buildup in debt. (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Writing by Koh Gui Qing; Editing by Clarence Fernandez) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-7-14T08:32:31.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140714ea7e003sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
