\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Says It Won a Victory in China  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Battle. Did It? -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2014\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 <FONT FACE="Arial" class="NormalMinus1">\par \par \uc2 DJ U.S. Says It Won a Victory in China \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Battle. Did It? -- WSJ Blog\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury Secretary and his top aides left Beijing last night thinking they had a won big battle in their effort to get China to strengthen the value of the yuan. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had agreed to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to "what are really extraordinary circumstances," Treasury Secretary Jack Lew told reporters in a briefing at the end of the two-day Strategic and Economic Dialogue before he jetted home.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. side credits what it sees as a turnaround in China's policy, in particular, to \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  governor \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , who is clearly a leader of the reformist faction and who has regularly said he would like the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reflect market forces. It's true that Mr. Zhou's influence is on the rise. But it's also clear that he doesn't set monetary policy in China. As Mr. Zhou is quick to acknowledge, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  isn't independent and he is one voice among many senior leaders.\par \par \uc2 A decision to bar \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and let the yuan rise and fall with the market would be one of the most significant in recent Chinese economic history and would certainly need the approval of the government's top decision-making body, the \uc2 State Council\uc2 , and perhaps China's real rulers --- the seven-member Politburo Standing Committee.\par \par \uc2 It's become vogue in Beijing to talk of reform. Even the \uc2 Communist Party\uc2  has committed to the market playing a "decisive" role in economic decision-making. Yet economic reform has moved achingly slowly over the past year, particularly when compared to the vigor and sweep of the Party's anti-corruption campaign.\par \par \uc2 Advisers to the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  say it's tough to push reform and that there are many factions stacked against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if the opponents don't make their case in public --- and especially not to visiting Americans. Given the politics, Mr. Zhou has had to act carefully. For instance, when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  earlier this year widened the band in which the yuan could trade (a favorite goal of reformers), it then proceeded to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to push down the value of the yuan (something opponents of reform were happy to see). China watchers say one reason for this pattern is that Mr. Zhou had to show that reform wasn't incompatible with the goal of boosting the economy in the short run.\par \par \uc2 During the Strategic and Economic Dialogue, senior Chinese officials took pains to say they weren't giving up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "When conditions permit, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will gradually reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Zhou said at a briefing. That formulation made it into the final statement of the two nations: \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as conditions permit," the statement said. Trouble is, there was no definition of the conditions, meaning they could be either political or economic conditions, or whatever the Chinese deemed them to be. The loophole is about as large as the Pacific Ocean.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Lou Jiwei\uc2 , who is taking on a Joe Biden, tell-it-like-it-is kind of role in Chinese politics, was blunter. "It is very difficult for us to completely refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said in a Wednesday briefing.\par \par \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2 , a former \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  China hand who now teaches economic at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 , explained the state of play of the U.S.-China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars. "The statement is essentially a declaration of a truce on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue in a manner that is seen as acceptable to domestic political constituencies on both sides," he says.\par \par \uc2 All this isn't to say China won't some day let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise and fall along with market forces, similar to what the U.S. does. But when that day comes, it will be because of an agreement among China's most senior leaders, not because of anything the U.S. manages to persuade officials to say.\par \par \uc2 -- Bob Davis\par \par \uc2 More at The Wall Street Journal's China Real Time Report blog, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime/}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 11, 2014 00:33 ET (04:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 11-07-14 0647GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020140711ea7b0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
