\par\par \b \uc2 Kerry Discusses Cybersecurity With Chinese Counterparts - Update -4-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley and Bob Davis   \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2014\par 02:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--U.S. officials said they had eased relations with China during two days of talks on issues ranging from cybersecurity to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they forged few major deals, suggesting wide gaps remain between the world's two biggest economies on issues of global significance.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State John Kerry said the annual talks, known as the Strategic & Economic Dialogue, showed "the U.S. and China are committed to a new model of relations based on practical cooperation, but also constructive management of differences." That was a theme echoed by Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  and by Chinese officials.\par \par \uc2 The good vibes even included Mr. Kerry picking up a guitar during one lunch and playing the Beatles song "Yesterday," according to China's vice education minister, Hao Ping. Chinese officials also performed during the lunch, he said, which focused on educational issues.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kerry highlighted several areas where the two governments are working together, including fighting wildlife trafficking and promoting energy efficiency.\par \par \uc2 But there were no agreements on how to resolve maritime border disputes, deal with industrial cyberespionage or what strategy to take to thwart North Korea's nuclear-weapons program or to enhance human rights.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Mr. Kerry said he had "a frank exchange" about China's cyberespionage with Chinese State Councilor Yang Jiechi, urging the government to restart a special working group on the issue that China canceled after U.S. prosecutors indicted five Chinese military officers in May for cybertheft. At a Chinese news conference, the cybersecurity issues weren't raised.\par \par \uc2 When it came to proliferation of nuclear weapons, Mr. Kerry said the two sides created a new working group on the subject.\par \par \uc2 On economic issues, Mr. Lew, the Treasury secretary, said the U.S. had convinced \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to "what are really extraordinary circumstances." That would be a big win for the U.S., which has complained that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly since the beginning of the year to drive down the value of the yuan against the dollar to help its export sector.\par \par \uc2 But a fact sheet issued bythe U.S. and China didn't show that level of commitment on the part of China. Rather, it said only that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as conditions permit"--without defining the conditions. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Zhou Xiaochuan, used the same formulation as the fact sheet during a press briefing.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Finance Minister \uc2 Lou Jiwei\uc2  was more blunt. "It is very difficult for us to completely refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 Cornell University\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  scholar \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2  said the two sides had hit upon a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  formulation that "leaves enough wiggle room for both countries to claim progress on cooperation while continuing to implement monetary and exchange rate policies that each side sees as in its own best interest."\par \par \uc2 In last year's talks, the biggest news was the restart of negotiations on what is called a bilateral investment treaty, which would reduce barriers to investment in both nations. At the time, China agreed to revamp its approach to investment approvals. Rather than requiring each investment to get a government OK, investments could proceed unless they occurred in areas specifically deemed off-limits by Beijing--a so-called negative list. This was seen as significant because it would potentially reduce the power of China's bureaucracy to block deals and give market forces greater sway.\par \par \uc2 But since then, negotiators say, the talks have moved very slowly. On Thursday, China agreed to begin negotiations on the negative list next year. Meanwhile, negotiators are working on the treaty's language. Mr. Lew asked for patience.\par \par \uc2 It is "always difficult and complicated" to complete such deals, he said.\par \par \uc2 Zhu Guangyao, China's vice minister of finance, said the two sides had made progress on the investment deal "but there will be great difficulties because the China and the U.S. have very different national conditions and levels of development." China wants to make sure that it isn't unfairly penalized by the U.S. review of deals on national security grounds, he said.\par \par \uc2 As for China, Mr. Zhu said, "the challenges are even bigger. We need a fundamental shift in our investment system."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Chamber of Commerce\uc2  in China said in a statement that it "applauds the commitment' to an investment treaty which could "provide better market access and investment protections to both U.S. and Chinese investors.\par \par \uc2 One area the two sides did see eye-to-eye on is energy efficiency. The two nations agreed to adopt more stringent vehicle fuel-efficiency and greenhouse- gas emissions standards--though the report didn't include any specific numbers. Both nations also said they would improve fuel quality to reduce pollution and would speed development of systems that capture carbon dioxide emissions from coal-fired power plants and other energy sources and stores the emissions.\par \par \uc2 The "China-U.S. partnership on [carbon-capture technology] has in many respects now left the theoretical feasibility realm and entered the 'steel in the ground' phase," wrote Sarah Forbes, a senior associate at the \uc2 World Resources Institute\uc2 , an environmental think tank in Washington.\par \par \uc2 On other issues, though, there was no meeting of the minds. The U.S. had hoped to convince China to agree to allow more high-tech goods to be imported duty-free, including advanced semiconductor material. China's reluctance to do so has held back global efforts to strengthen a 70-nation trade pact, called the Information Technology Agreement. But China hasn't made the concessions sought by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The two sides held "constructive discussions" on the ITA, according to the fact sheet. Mr. Zhu had a different take: "Although ITA expansion is currently at a standstill," he said, "the responsibility is not with the Chinese side."\par \par \uc2 Richard Silk contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ian Talley at ian.talley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 11-07-14 0008GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140711ea7b00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
