\par\par \b \uc2 China Has Wiggle Room in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Transparency Plans -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2014\par 16:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:15 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  May have committed to reducing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is preparing to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  more transparent, but the fine print means the promises aren't exactly a major step forward. Beijing left itself a major loophole on cutting \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying they would only do it as conditions permit, and gave no timeline for transparency. "We believe that given the current economic situation and that the economic recovery has not been fully put in place and the capital flow is still not yet for normal, it is very difficult for us to completely refrain from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister Lou Jiwei. (ian.talley@wsj.com; @iantalley)\par \par \uc2 10:08 EDT - Inventories at US wholesalers rise 0.5% in May, matching economists' expectations. Excluding autos, inventories were up 0.3%. Following these figures has taken on increasing importance for GDP estimates because inventories have caused swings over the last year. GDP for the first three months this year contracted by 2.9%, and part of that decline was from businesses sharply drawing down inventories after building them up late last year. \uc2 Macroeconomic Advisers\uc2  is forecasting GDP will expand at a 2.7% rate in the April-to-June period. Commerce is set to release the first read of its 2Q GDP report on July 30. (sarah.portlock@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 EDT - Economic activity picked up in the week ended June 28, says the \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2 , and store sales were a big reason why. The BTMU's weekly business barometer increased 0.2% for that week, lifted by a 1% increase in real chain store sales. The shopping rise is the third weekly increase in a row. Coupled with positive same-store sales reported today by major retailers, the uptrend suggests an increase in the Commerce Department's tally of June retail sales. That report comes out July 15. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:56 EDT - When Bank of Canada Gov. Stephen Poloz discussed the exports outlook early this year, he likened the-then weaker Canadian dollar to "icing" on the "cake"--i.e. demand from the U.S., which takes up some 75% of Canadian shipments. "Exports still await U.S. demand so there isn't any cake yet and with the Canadian dollar having strengthened since mid-March, there isn't much icing either," according to HSBC chief economist David Watt. The shift in market sentiment on CAD is one reason for BOC to keep a "decidedly cautious tone" in its July 16 rate decision, Watt says. (nirmala.menoN@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 1354 GMT The yield on offer for buying Greece's new three-year bond is not enough to compensate for the country's political risks, says Eliezer Ben Zimra, a fund manager at Edmond de Rothschild Asset Management in Paris. Greece paid a yield of 3.5% for the bonds, raising EUR1.5 billion ($2 billion). Mr. Ben Zimra says Greece's political instability means the yield isn't chunky enough relative to the risk the bonds won't be paid back in full. "That's why we didn't buy," he says. (ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd)\par \par \uc2 09:39 EDT - Median listing prices for US homes in June rose 8.1% from a year earlier and stood 2.6% higher than three months earlier, according to \uc2 Trulia\uc2 . But several Western markets that witnessed some of the biggest price rebounds over the past year have seen those gains come to an end. Asking prices in Las Vegas were flat in 2Q from the prior quarter, and down 0.3% in Phoenix. Sacramento saw asking prices rise just 0.3% in 2Q, from a 5.6% gain in 1Q. The price slowdown isn't a total surprise to anyone that's been tracking sales out West: Sales dropped last year after sustained price increases, a jump in mortgage rates and low supplies of homes for sale. (nick.timiraos@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:39 EDT - Flight-to-safety has sent Treasury yields tumbling, making traders cautious about today's $13B sale of 30-year bonds. The to-be-issued debt recently yielded 3.338% in the secondary market, the lowest since May 2013, and lower yields dented demand for yesterday's $21B 10-year sale. "The demand wild card remains if any structural buyers show up" today, notes Nomura. "We think supply concession will be needed." One supporting factor: risk-off could bolster haven demand, and the long bond still offers the highest yield in the \uc2 US government\uc2 -debt market, nevermind the gap between it and the likes of Germany and Japan. (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:37 EDT - \uc2 GMP Securities\uc2  reminds clients that \uc2 Bank of Portugal\uc2  has been working for a while to ring-fence \uc2 Banco Espirito Santo\uc2 , a part of the troubled Espirito Santo conglomerate that's dragging down global markets this morning. It's been doing so "in an effort to convince the market that this situation is indeed a one off situation...and not symptomatic of problems across the Portuguese banking system." But clearly, it's not working this morning. "At this point the market is shooting first and asking questions later," the firm notes. In part, they say, this is because the \uc2 European Union\uc2  "has failed to fully implement EU banking reforms that would clarify the process of dealing with such a banking problem." In the absence of a roadmap, all roads appear to lead to market turbulence. (erik.holm@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:28 EDT - House Republicans are holding a hearing this morning on changing the governance of the Fed. The legislation would have a number of unwelcome provisions for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : requiring the Fed to use a policy rule that limits its flexibility, adding extra steps to international negotiations the Fed conducts with its foreign counterparts, and requiring disclosure of high-earning Fed staff. The Fed's regional banks are not subject to the federal payscale and would likely have hundreds of employees whose salaries would be disclosed. But they probably don't need to worry for now: the Fed's congressional opponents haven't gotten anything against the Fed into legislation since the 2010 Dodd-Frank Act and are unlikely to muster the momentum for reform this time either. (josh.zumbrun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:23 EDT - Economists are being cautious about the latest drop in jobless claims. A big reason: the timing of annual retooling shutdowns at auto makers may be distorting the seasonally adjusted data. As Ray Stone at Stone McCarthy Research writes, "we suspect the dip in claims may be an artifact of fewer than typical claims stemming from seasonal automotive shutdowns." Ian Shepherdson of Pantheon Macroeconomics calculates the 62% dropoff in auto output last week should mean a 60K jump in unadjusted jobless claims, but they instead rose just 17K. He cautions next week's claims number could see a big increase as temporarily laid-off auto workers begin to file for benefits. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:14 EDT - There's a rising tide of risk aversion rolling over global asset markets and three risk-sensitive majors are vulnerable, Scotiabank says. It sees mounting downside risk for the Canadian dollar, as oil prices come under pressure and worries about global growth escalate. USD/CAD, last at 1.0674, faces resistance at 1.0688 and support at 1.0645, it says. The euro is struggling on weak industrial production in France and Italy. EUR/USD, last at 1.3608, and Scotia warns a test of 1.35. The Australian dollar is weak after details of employment data disappoint. AUD/USD at 0.9371, and a break of 0.9347 opens up a test down to 0.9200. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 9:13 EDT - Mortgage-backed securities have risen today with Treasurys as investors seek safe havens, but they continue to lag the government debt--as they have most of the month. MBS have softened as QE tapering will soon make even light issuance of them a heavier lift for other investors. What's more, prepayments--the bugaboo for MBS trading at premiums--crept higher than expected last month, further taking the shine off bonds which are widely seen as "rich." (albert.yoon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 10, 2014 10:15 ET (14:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140710ea7a002b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
