\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Says China to Boost  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-Exchange \highlight\uc2  Transparency\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley   \par \uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2014\par 14:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2  on Thursday said after high-level talks here that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was committed to reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was preparing to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  more transparent, a step that would make the yuan's value more market-determined.\par \par \uc2 "China is making preparations to adopt greater transparency including on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will accelerate the move to a more market-based exchange rate," Mr. Lew said at the end of two days of strategic and economic talks with officials.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have talked about making China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  more transparent in the past. If enacted, such a move would help bring greater visibility to the way China controls its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market--a step toward gradually relaxing controls and giving the market a greater say in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2 Beijing left itself a major loophole on cutting \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it would make such a move only as conditions permit and offering no timeline for transparency.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that given the current economic situation and that the economic recovery has not been fully put in place and the capital flow is still not yet for normal, it is very difficult for us to completely refrain from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, \uc2 Lou Jiwei\uc2 , said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Lew said the commitments "will assist China in its reforms and will help level the playing field."\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the talks, Mr. Lew said he would press China to continue to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, to appreciate. After years of steady appreciation in the value of the yuan, some economists question whether China is done lifting the exchange rate, especially as the country's economic growth rate slows. By preventing the yuan from appreciating under market pressures, China can keep its exports internationally competitive and spur growth.\par \par \uc2 Beijing argues the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value is now determined by the market, an assessment the U.S. challenges. The Treasury Department in April took Beijing to task over its exchange-rate policy. It stopped short of calling the country a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator," a term that would potentially trigger sanctions against China, but it called the yuan's weakening in value versus the U.S. dollar in recent months "unprecedented."\par \par \uc2 Bob Davis contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ian Talley at ian.talley@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-07-14 1216GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140710ea7a000a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
