\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Down As Exports Weaker Than Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2014\par 11:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.1443               6.1565 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.2028    +0.95%     6.1999 \par             High         6.2054    +0.99% \par             Low          6.1940    +0.81% \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan failed to extend gains Thursday despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's attempt to push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stronger via a daily reference rate, as the country's exports grew at a lower-than-expected rate in June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The yuan ended at 6.2028 to a dollar Thursday, weakening from 6.1999 at Wednesday' close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded between 6.1940 and 6.2054.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.1443, much lower than Wednesday's 6.1565, as it guided the yuan higher while senior officials from the U.S. and China meet in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 But traders didn't follow the lead as exports from China only rose 7.2% year-over-year in June. Growth was slightly faster than May's 7.0% increase, but below a median forecast of 10.0% growth in a poll of 21 economists by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "Finance Minister \uc2 Lou Jiwei\uc2  yesterday confirmed our interpretation of China's current policy stance, as part of the overall macro policy stance: the government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] market to prevent the [yuan's] appreciation until its worries about overall economic growth have receded," RBS said in a report Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC governor \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  also said Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't ready to give up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , echoing remarks made Wednesday by the country's finance minister. The U.S. has pressed China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade according to market forces. The Chinese government loosely controls the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and earlier this year \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sharply weaken it against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou said China wanted to eventually move to market-determined exchange rates, but that money flows were still too unstable to allow that. "When conditions permit, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will gradually reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, without specifying the conditions.\par \par \uc2 The yuan ended its steady appreciation in February and lost up to 3.4% against the dollar after policy makers engineered a decline to squeeze out speculators betting on yuan gains, while investors also soured on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the economy slowed.\par \par \uc2 As of Thursday, the yuan has lost about 2.4% against the U.S. unit this year.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.2510/6.2540 from 6.2465/6.2515 late Wednesday, implying a 0.8% fall by the yuan over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.2015, higher than CNY6.2000 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Wynne Wang at wynne.wang@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-07-14 0937GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140710ea7a0008q\par }\page 