\par\par \b \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's Zhou: Still Committed to Interest-Rate Liberalization in Two Years\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis   \par \uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2014\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , on Thursday reiterated the intention to liberalize interest rates within two years but said the exact timing would depend on "good conditions" in the domestic and global economy.\par \par \uc2 "We'd like to finish in two years," he told reporters during the annual meeting of senior U.S. and Chinese officials called the Strategic & Economic Dialogue. "This goal has not changed."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Mr. Zhou laid out the liberalization goal, but since then reform has moved slowly. For instance, China hasn't yet approved bank deposit insurance, which is widely seen as a crucial step needed before allowing banks to compete on deposit rates. Without such insurance, depositors could take big hits if banks sharply raised rates to attract depositors and then went broke.\par \par \uc2 China has been debating deposit insurance for two decades. A final decision has been before the \uc2 State Council\uc2  for months, Chinese officials say, but the council hasn't acted. The \uc2 State Council\uc2  is the Chinese government's top decision-making body.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou suggested that other parts of the Chinese government may have slower reform timetables. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  isn't independent.\par \par \uc2 Senior leaders say "we should have a sense of urgency when implementing reform," Mr. Zhou said, which was a reason for setting an ambitious goal.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, Mr. Zhou said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't ready to give up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , echoing remarks made Wednesday by the country's finance minister. The U.S. has pressed China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade according to market forces. The Chinese government loosely controls the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and earlier this year \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sharply weaken it against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou said China wanted to eventually move to market-determined exchange rates, but that money flows were still too unstable to allow that. "When conditions permit, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will gradually reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, without specifying the conditions.\par \par \uc2 He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was developing new instruments to manage monetary policy, particularly instruments aimed at affecting long-term rates. He didn't provide details.\par \par \uc2 Write to \uc2 Bob Davis\uc2  at bob.davis@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-07-14 0647GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140710ea7a0006n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
