\par\par \b \uc2 Economists React: China's Exports Rise, But Not Enough -- WSJ Blog -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2014\par 08:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 For months, analysts have been predicting a rebound in global trade as economies around the world get stronger. But thanks to a terrible winter in the U.S. that dampened growth, wobbles in big emerging economies like Brazil and India and continued sclerosis in Europe, that recovery has been slow to arrive. China's latest exports figures show a 7.2% year-over-year rise in June, ahead of May's 7% but still short of forecasts of 10% growth. Meanwhile imports were up a lackluster 5.5%, suggesting that China itself is not adding much to global demand, either. With exports continuing to outstrip imports, the trade surplus was a whopping $31.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 Economists weigh in with their views (edited for style):\par \par \uc2 Based on recent trends, we have tempered somewhat our expectations for the recovery of growth in global demand and China's exports. The most recent data available -- for April -- shows that global import growth has so far this year not accelerated decisively... This is in part because global economic growth is not accelerating anymore. Global industrial production growth seems to have plateaued through April as the recovery in developed countries is losing steam again. It is possible that global import momentum has improved in May-June, for instance fed by the stronger U.S. growth that recent indicators suggest. But the disappointments on global imports in recent years call for caution. -- \uc2 Louis Kuijs\uc2 , RBS\par \par \uc2 Improving conditions in developed markets mean that we expect the export growth to remain healthy going forward, despite today's disappointing data. In contrast, we expect import growth to drop further, given our view that cooling investment growth will weigh on commodity imports over the next couple of years. As a result, China is likely to continue to post large trade surpluses. -- Julian Evans-Pritchard, Capital Economics\par \par \uc2 We still remain confident for China's exports, as we believe recovery of demand from developed countries will support China's export industry in the medium term...China's exports will continuously benefit from better economic conditions in developed economies and a softened renminbi. In the near term, we believe reference rate of the renminbi will stabilize at 6.15 to 6.16 per U.S. dollar as foreign capital inflows have already moderated in recent months... In the longer term, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to step back to an appreciation path, but at a milder pace compared with previous years. -- Fan Zhang, CIMB\par \par \uc2 The trade surplus exceeded $30 billion again in June, and it continues to add pressures on yuan appreciation in the foreseeable future. A large trade surplus amid the Sino-U.S. Strategic and Economic Dialogue has again put the yuan in the spotlight, facilitating renewed appreciation. More importantly, the net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased by the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commercial banks declined sharply in the past few months, indicating that the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market...The reducing intensity of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also allow the RMB to appreciate further. -- Li-Gang Liu and Hao Zhou, \uc2 ANZ Bank\par \par \uc2 - Compiled by Richard Silk\par \par \uc2 More at The Wall Street Journal's China Real Time Report blog, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime/}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-07-14 0644GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140710ea7a0006f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
