\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Companies Step Up Business Conducted in Yuan -5-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Hong and Lingling Wei   \par \uc2 1,282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2014\par 02:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 American companies are conducting a record amount of business in Chinese yuan, looking to benefit from cost advantages over dollar transactions.\par \par \uc2 Payments made in yuan by U.S. companies ranging from \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 . to small clothing importers quadrupled over the past year to a record 2.6% of the global yuan total, according to the \uc2 Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication\uc2 , a financial-services firm that monitors international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. recently passed Taiwan to become the fourth-largest hub for trade in the yuan outside mainland China, after Hong Kong, Singapore and the U.K., according to Swift.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is making inroads with U.S. firms now because it is becoming more cost-efficient to pay in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . American importers can often negotiate better prices with Chinese suppliers if they agree to use yuan to make a purchase. Chinese companies tend to increase prices when they settle trades in dollars as a way to offset potential exchange-rate fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 Transactions in yuan still represent a tiny portion of the more than $500 billion in annual trade between the U.S. and China. But companies and banks in both countries say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi, is starting to gain a foothold in America for the first time since China began loosening restrictions on trading the yuan outside China in 2009.\par \par \uc2 "More and more of our payments will migrate to the renminbi now that it's easier to do so," said Ryan Hershberger, Ford's treasurer of Asia-Pacific in Shanghai. "We think the benefits will accrue over time."\par \par \uc2 At the start of a two-day summit in Beijing Wednesday, Treasury Secretary Jacob Lew pressed China to further free up the yuan. U.S. officials have accused China of keeping the yuan weak to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 Widespread use of the renminbi by U.S. companies would be a milestone in China's push to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more accessible world-wide because the U.S. is the world's largest economy and China's biggest trade partner. The yuan's liberalization is part of the government's long-term goal to allow free-market forces to play a bigger role in the economy, with the aim of shifting the world's financial center of gravity from the U.S. to China.\par \par \uc2 "If China in the long run is interested in having the renminbi challenge the dollar as a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the size of the U.S. economy, U.S. firms will have to get on board," said Mark Wu, a former \uc2 World Bank\uc2  economist who teaches at Harvard Law School.\par \par \uc2 U.S. companies need to use the yuan for a variety of reasons, including paying for imports from China or protecting against exchange-rate fluctuations. They also need to make yuan transactions if they establish an office in China to pay for things like salaries and office supplies there.\par \par \uc2 Beijing began loosening its grip on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2005 when it dropped the yuan's peg to the dollar, though the yuan still trades in a tight range set daily by the government. Four years later, China launched a pilot program to allow some Chinese companies to settle their trades in renminbi. That program has since expanded to include almost all companies in mainland China. The Chinese government has also simplified the approval process for yuan settlement.\par \par \uc2 Usage of the yuan has taken off globally. More than 18% of China's total trade is paid for in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from less than 1% five years ago, according to \uc2 Bank of China Ltd\uc2 . The \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  reported last year that the yuan is now the ninth most actively traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world.\par \par \uc2 U.S. companies have been much slower than firms in Europe and Asia to adopt the yuan. The dollar is ubiquitous in global trade, so U.S. companies have less incentive to switch to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when dealing with foreign suppliers and customers.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday in New York, the yuan was trading at 6.2 to the dollar, near a three-month high. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 2.4% this year after rising since 2010.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials acknowledge that the yuan is a long way from competing against the dollar as a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  used by governments around the world as a store of value for their rainy-day funds. A "lot of homework" is yet to be done before the yuan becomes a truly international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Zhou Xiaochuan, said at a press conference in March.\par \par \uc2 The government hasn't put a timeline for when that would occur. On Wednesday, Chinese Vice Premier \uc2 Wang Yang\uc2  said China needs to find "balance" in the pace of economic reforms.\par \par \uc2 "If we move too fast, we will be tripped by the demons of details," he said.\par \par \uc2 Payments out of the U.S. account for 2.6% of total renminbi payments world-wide, compared with 5.9% in London and 6.8% in Singapore, according to Swift data. Hong Kong commands the biggest share, with 71%.\par \par \uc2 "The increase in the use of renminbi in the U.S. shows that renminbi has reached a new level," said Li Jianjun, an analyst at \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  in Beijing. "At the same time, we should keep a cool head. The increase is off a low base."\par \par \uc2 Ford said it started paying in renminbi for auto parts from Chinese suppliers 19 months ago, after the Chinese government passed legislation in 2012 that removed a lot of the paperwork and documentation required for a foreign company to make renminbi payments.\par \par \uc2 Although the switch hasn't resulted in any significant cost savings yet for Ford, the company expects to use renminbi payments as one way to negotiate lower prices with Chinese suppliers in the future, Mr. Hershberger, the company's Asia-Pacific treasurer, said.\par \par \uc2 Victoria Classics, a textile importer based in Edison, N.J., started paying its Chinese suppliers in renminbi in the middle of last year after importing from China for more than two decades. The company's factories in China had been unwilling to accept yuan payments until last year, when the Chinese government simplified the process for settling yuan trades. Joe Cohen, chief executive of Victoria Classics, said the company has saved approximately $200,000 since it switched to renminbi. "If U.S. companies see the benefit, they will expand their usage of renminbi," Mr. Cohen said. "You don't know until you try it."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks have heightened this year for exporters in China. For the past four years, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadily and consistently appreciated against the dollar, making it easy for Chinese companies to anticipate and protect against exchange-rate fluctuations. However, since the start of this year, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has widened the yuan's trading band and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to guide the yuan lower in a bid to shake out speculators who had one-way bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. The heightened volatility in the yuan's exchange rate has made Chinese companies more willing to stick to their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and avoid the conversion to dollars.\par \par \uc2 "I'm used to accepting dollars, but renminbi settlement is getting more attractive because it reduces my \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks," said Du Hanbing, who runs a business in Shenzhen that makes embossing machines and sells them in the U.S. and Canada. Mr. Du said he hadn't considered getting paid in yuan until recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Bob Davis contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 10-07-14 0019GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140710ea7a0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
