\par\par \b \uc2 Juggling Act for China as Inflows Slow -- WSJ Blog -3-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shen Hong   \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2014\par 05:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 As fresh signs of slowing capital inflows emerge, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces an opportunity to focus its monetary policy more on the domestic economy while ensuring it supplies the right amount of cash.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  bought a net 361 million yuan ($58 million) of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May, down sharply from 846 million yuan in April and marking the lowest in almost a year, official figures released on Monday showed.\par \par \uc2 The data suggest that the magnitude of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - buying U.S. dollars with yuan to prevent the latter from appreciating, essentially stalled in May as a result of decreasing capital inflows, economists say.\par \par \uc2 "This means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer forced to print money to offset the inflationary impact of hot money, but it also offers challenges in terms of maintaining an appropriate level of liquidity in the financial system," said Guan Qingyou, vice president of Minsheng Securities' research institute."\par \par \uc2 In a bid to stop the yuan from strengthening too much, Beijing has\par \par \uc2 in recent years bought billions of dollars with fresh yuan notes through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The move has led to a rapid buildup of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, while the extra money supply of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  added unwelcome inflationary pressures on the economy.\par \par \uc2 At the end of March, China had $3.95 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, up from $3.82 trillion at the end of 2013.\par \par \uc2 "The fast growth of such \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases in the past deprived the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  of its power and initiative in setting its monetary policy, making it hard for policies to better serve domestic economic needs," said Mr. Guan. "The latest data showed the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is regaining its power and using more targeted policies to help stabilize economic growth and push through structural reforms."\par \par \uc2 Under President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , China's new leaders have embarked on a painful journey to cut the economy's unhealthy reliance on investment-led growth and reset it with a consumption-led growth model. This effort requires an end to years of easy credit supply and using relatively higher borrowing costs to remove industrial overcapcity and nurture more innovative businesses.\par \par \uc2 The latest \uc2 PBOC\uc2  data echoes a broader indicator released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, which showed the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  and Chinese financial institutions bought a net 38.66 billion yuan of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May.\par \par \uc2 The figure, which is widely viewed a proxy for capital inflows or outflows, is sharply lower than the 116.92 billion yuan in April and marked the smallest tally since August.\par \par \uc2 The dramatic slowdown in capital inflows came after the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  engineered a campaign to weaken the yuan's value earlier this year in a bid to discourage incessant bets on a rising Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan lost 3.2% against the U.S. dollar between Feb. 18 and Apr. 30, the period when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  guided the yuan lower to squeeze out speculators. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since stabilized and rebounded a tad, with a fall of 2.5% versus the greenback since the beginning of this year.\par \par \uc2 However, with the Chinese economy showing encouraging signs of a recovery since last month, questions are resurfacing about whether the yuan may resume an upward trend, which may lure speculators back into the country.\par \par \uc2 "It's likely that hot money inflows will come back, but it's unlikely to be excessive simply because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase the volatility of the yuan," said Larry Hu, economist with \uc2 Macquarie Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But even if the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  succeeds in deterring speculators with an increasingly unpredictable exchange rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to come up with other means to offset the negative impact of decreased capital inflows, which has been a main source of money market, Mr. Hu said.\par \par \uc2 With economic rebalancing still high on Beijing's agenda, it is unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will introduce any broad-based monetary policy easing, said Mr. Guan. "Instead, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will use targeted credit easing measures, such as lower reserve requirements for select banks," he added.\par \par \uc2 Pumping more cash into the financial system via the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's weekly money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and boosting bank lending quotas are also options to increase the supply of credit, Mr. Hu said.\par \par \uc2 Liyan Qi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 09-07-14 0333GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140709ea7900035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
