\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-China move to guide yuan higher puts  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market in spin\b0\par\par\uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2014\par 02:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story from Friday with no changes to text)               \par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney and Saikat Chatterjee               \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI/HONG KONG, June 13 (Reuters) - The one thing \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared clear about when it guided the yuan stronger against the dollar earlier this week was that the depreciation it orchestrated earlier this year had run its course.               \par \par \uc2 But the signal raised so many questions about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's motives that analysts became filled with doubt whether the message was even as simple as that.               \par \par \uc2 Over three trading days, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the midpoint for the daily trading range - the exchange rate is allowed to fluctuate 2 percent either side - to make the yuan 0.42 percent stronger.               \par \par \uc2 It was the sharpest short-term adjustment of the mid-point since October 2012, and the mid-point's 14-day moving average showed a rise for the first time since January, when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  surprised markets by putting the yuan into depreciation mode.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts were left wondering: why now?               \par \par \uc2 One plausible explanation was that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  had become concerned that prolonged depreciation might lead to destabilising capital outflows, particularly as Chinese firms and investors would try to keep money offshore, rather than bring it back and park it in their own weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Or, analysts argued, it was the unexpected strength of China's exports in May that persuaded the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  that the weak yuan had done its job. It had lost 3.4 percent against the dollar between January and April, and now it should be allowed to strengthen.               \par \par \uc2 Yuan markets have reacted hesitantly, at first ignoring the higher benchmarks set by \uc2 PBOC\uc2  and then catching up. The value of the offshore exchange rate has gone up 0.8 percent against the dollar since June 5.               \par \par \uc2 "The stronger than expected yuan fixings caught the markets off guard," said the executive director of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge fund in Hong Kong.               \par \par \uc2 "Many were expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain on the weaker side especially as economic growth has seen steady signs of decelerating despite some pockets of optimism. The strong fixings were also at a time when the general dollar trend was subdued which left the markets even more clueless," he said.               \par \par \uc2 The hedge fund was looking at buying the offshore yuan on the view that the weakness so far had been overdone, he said.               \par \par \uc2 Analysts however struggled to explain the sudden change in trend.                              \par \par \uc2 OFFICIAL AMBIVALENCE               \par \par \uc2 One banker noted the Chinese authorities were struggling to get loans to flow into productive investments despite a banking system awash with cash. Rampant speculation in property and financial products also needed to be contained.               \par \par \uc2 So, while trying to manage the domestic liquidity situation, the banker presumed the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  wanted to eliminate another complication, namely capital outflows stemming from a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Another investment bank felt \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was merely trying to deflect allegations of conducting a mercantilist exchange rte policy before its usual strategic and economic dialogue with the United States in July.               \par \par \uc2 At the other end of the spectrum were several market players that simply didn't buy into the notion that the yuan's trend had conclusively turned.               \par \par \uc2 Carl Huttenlocher, chief investment officer of Myriad Asset Management, told a conference in Hong Kong on Thursday that he expected further depreciation, arguing that it would support Chinese exports and while it might stoke inflation that would have the benefit of effectively lessening \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's debt burden.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the aim of stopping one-way bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or to keep dealing rooms guessing about its policy intent.               \par \par \uc2 There is ambivalence, however, within \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities over the pros and cons of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the yuan.               \par \par \uc2 In a webcast on Thursday, officials from the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) made a rare admission, saying that the costs of accumulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have exceeded the income they produced.               \par \par \uc2 The SAFE officials also said they were ready to use the exchange rate to suppress hot money inflows in future, implying that further weakening of the yuan remained a possibility.               \par \par \uc2 Moreover, while dollar buying to suppress the yuan meant adding to China's already massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, but it also put more yuan into circulation, expanding the base money supply.               \par \par \uc2 M2 broad money supply has expanded by a whopping 117 trillion yuan, five times that of 10 years ago.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-6-16T01:35:27.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140616ea6g000jj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
