\par\par \b \uc2 *S&P Affirms China 'AA-/A-1+' Rtgs; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2014\par 09:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12 Jun 2014 03:29 ET  Press Release: S&P Affirms China 'AA-/A-1+' Rtgs; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par  \par OVERVIEW \par      -- China's robust growth prospects, strong net external asset position, \par and relatively healthy fiscal profile are key supporting factors for the \par government's creditworthiness. \par      -- These strengths offset weaknesses related to China's low middle-income \par economy, limited transparency, moderate contingent liabilities, and extensive \par government involvement in economic activities. \par      -- We are affirming our 'AA-' long-term and 'A-1+' short-term sovereign \par credit rating on China. We are also affirming our 'cnAAA/cnA-1+' Greater China \par regional scale ratings. \par      -- The stable outlook reflects our expectation that reform towards a more \par market-driven economy and enhanced governance could help China sustain robust \par GDP growth while curbing credit growth over next two to three years. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par On June 12, 2014, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services affirmed the 'AA-' \par long-term and 'A-1+' short-term sovereign credit rating on the People's \par Republic of China. The outlook on the long-term rating is stable. In line with \par this, we are affirming our 'cnAAA/cnA-1+' Greater China regional scale \par ratings. Our transfer and convertibility (T&C) assessment is 'AA-'. \par  \par RATIONALE \par The ratings on China reflect what we consider to be the country's continued \par robust economic growth potential, its strong external liquidity and balance \par sheet, and the government's healthy overall fiscal position. We weigh these \par strengths against weaknesses related to China's relatively low levels of \par prosperity and limited transparency in terms of some policies and economic \par data, notably on local government finances. An economic policy framework that \par is still evolving to suit China's largely market-based economy is an \par additional sovereign credit weakness. \par  \par We expect China to gradually move towards a more market-driven economy with \par enhanced governance, as guided by the central government's reform blueprint in \par November 2013. This shift would, in our view, be characterized by a more \par effective approach to prevent and prosecute corruption, heightened fiscal \par transparency and management, further deregulation, and smarter regulation. \par Assuming China makes significant progress in these areas over the period in \par our base-line scenario, we expect the economy to continue its robust \par performance over 2014-2017 and maintain its large external creditor position. \par That's despite a shrinking working age population, a greater economic base, \par and growing environmental concerns. \par  \par In such context, we project that China's per capita real GDP will grow 6.6% in \par 2014-2017 on average--faster than that of countries with similar income \par levels. Such robust growth would mitigate the constraint over China's \par sovereign creditworthiness stemming from its low-middle income level. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard \par & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  estimates China's per capita GDP in 2013 at about US$6,700. However, \par the robust growth would only benefit sovereign creditworthiness if it is \par supported by market-oriented reforms--as we anticipate in our base-line \par scenario--rather than by fast credit expansion. \par  \par Continued fast credit growth would increase the risk of a sharp correction in \par the financial system, partly through accommodating ineffective investments, \par which is already significant in the country in our view. At 45% of GDP, China \par has the second-highest investment ratio of all 129 sovereigns that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & \par Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  rates (see "\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2 ", Choose all countries and \par click on the "Economic Data" tab). We believe that such an elevated rate is \par unsustainable. In our view, China's inevitable recalibration towards a growth \par model with greater reliance on private-consumption will be challenging for \par macroeconomic management. \par  \par We expect China to maintain a strong net external asset position over the next \par three years. We estimate the country's narrow net external asset position \par stood at 129% of current account receipts in 2013, excluding equity and \par non-bank private sector foreign assets. Kuwait and Saudi Arabia were the only \par rated sovereigns to surpass that figure. We expect China's asset position to \par gradually decline to 104% by 2017. The country's net external creditor \par position remains underpinned by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's very large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par reserves. We project the reserves will rise moderately towards 2017 from about \par US$4 trillion in 2013. \par  \par Deepening market-oriented reforms could improve China's productivity, \par mitigating the pressure of rising labour costs on its export competitiveness. \par China's domestic savings are likely to remain higher than its elevated \par investment levels over the next three years. This is likely to result in \par continued moderate current account surpluses of around 2% of GDP, backed by \par what we expect to be improvements in productivity. The increasingly active \par trading of the renminbi in the global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, according to \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2 , is also bolstering China's external \par financial resilience, in our view. \par  \par We expect the central government to continue to aim to provide the \par preconditions for robust economic growth. However, we see significant \par challenges for policymaking and implementation. This is partly due to limited \par transparency and the lack of some relevant information--despite many timely \par and informative statistics. In particular, there is insufficient data on home \par ownership, occupancy, and income distribution. This comes at a time of \par significant uncertainty over the outlook for the property market, high \par disparity in incomes and wealth, and a pressing need for related fiscal \par reform. \par  \par Unless transparency materially improves, the risk of "rent seeking"--or \par lobbying for preferential treatment--could remain substantial as the \par government is still extensively involved in economic activities. This \par situation may contribute to inefficient allocation of resources and could \par undermine fiscal discipline, especially at the lower levels of government. We \par also believe that the public financial data of local governments remain \par relatively non-transparent. \par  \par China will maintain a generally healthy fiscal profile over next two to three \par years, in our view. That's despite a weaker revenue performance and strong \par spending pressure. We project general government debt will increase by an \par average of 2% of GDP each year over 2014-2017. Net general government debt \par should rise modestly to 18% of GDP in 2017 from 15% in 2014. However, we \par believe China's underlying fiscal position is weaker than these indicators \par suggest. \par  \par We estimate that the government's contingent liabilities are moderate, as our \par methodology defines the term, and could surpass 30% of GDP for three main \par reasons. First, entities related to local and regional governments have \par incurred sizable debt. Second, domestic credit growth has grown strongly over \par the past five years; we estimate that domestic credit to the resident \par non-governmental sector increased to 137% of GDP in 2013 from 103% in 2008). \par Finally, and to a lesser extent, non-bank lending grew rapidly (see "Banking \par Industry Country Risk Assessment: China", published on RatingsDirect on Jan. \par 9, 2014). \par  \par China's monetary flexibility is neutral to its sovereign creditworthiness. \par Monetary policy is largely credible and effective, as reflected in low \par inflation for an emerging economy. Consumer price index inflation averaged \par 3.4% over 2007-2013, and is likely to drop below 3% annually over 2014-2017. \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has significant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  independence, in our view, \par especially regarding open-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The impact of these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on \par the economy transmits through a largely responsive interbank market and a \par sizable and expanding bond market. Nevertheless, China's managed exchange rate \par regime and high leverage in the corporate sector constrain its monetary \par flexibility. \par  \par We equalize the T&C assessment for China with the 'AA-' sovereign foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating. This reflects our opinion that the likelihood of the \par sovereign restricting access to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needed by China-based \par non-sovereign issuers to service debt is similar to the likelihood of the \par sovereign defaulting on its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  obligations. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par nature of government policies and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's perceived priority to support \par government creditworthiness underlie our opinion. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par The stable outlook is based on our expectation that China can sustain its real \par per capita GDP growth at 6%-7% over the next three years. At the same time, we \par believe the country should be able to curb credit growth and gradually shift \par its growth model away from investment towards private consumption. \par  \par We may raise the ratings if the central government's announced reforms lead to \par much greater reliance on market-based macroeconomic management tools, \par supported by better transparency, improved information availability, and a \par more flexible exchange rate. \par  \par Conversely, we may lower the ratings if reform efforts falter, reducing per \par capita real GDP growth below 4% annually. We may also downgrade China if \par policymakers resort to fast credit expansion to cushion economic shocks prior \par to having materially better governance to prevent ineffective investments. \par Such a scenario would increase the risks to stability in the financial sector \par and may trigger a sharp correction of the economy. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Sovereign Government Rating Methodology And Assumptions, June 24, 2013 \par      -- Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term Ratings For \par Corporate, Insurance, and Sovereign Issuers, May 7, 2013 \par      -- Criteria For Determining Transfer And Convertibility Assessments, May \par 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Defaults And Rating Transition Data, 2013 Update, April 18, \par 2014 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment: China, Jan. 9, 2014 \par      -- Supplementary Analysis: China (People's Republic of), Dec. 19, 2013 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Jun 2014 03:29 ET \par Press Release: S&P Affirms China 'AA-/A-1+' Rtgs; -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to \par make an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par Ratings Affirmed \par  \par China (People's Republic of ) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating                AA-/Stable/A-1+ \par  Greater China Regional Scale           cnAAA/--/cnA-1+ \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment   AA- \par  \par  Senior Unsecured                       AA- \par  Senior Unsecured                       cnAAA \par  \par Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of RatingsDirect at \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrs