\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan edges lower on dollar demand for dividend payments\b0\par\par\uc2 832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2014\par 07:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * June is peak for firms to buy dollars for dividends\par     * Big firms could need $18 bln for dividends - trader\par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  data shows less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as yuan depreciates\par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint stronger but does not point to uptrend\par \par     By Lu Jianxin and Kazunori Takada\par     SHANGHAI, June 4 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened\par marginally against the dollar on Wednesday as overseas-listed\par Chinese companies bought dollars to pay interim dividends,\par traders said.\par     Spot yuan stood at 6.2552 per dollar at midday,\par easing 0.02 percent from Tuesday's close as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 refrained from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  either way, according to\par traders.\par     June is the peak month for Chinese companies listed in\par offshore markets to buy dollars in order to make interim cash\par dividend payments to investors, traders said. Typically such\par purchases are a feature of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market through until\par mid-July.\par     Dollar demand from some of the biggest firms listed in Hong\par Kong could reach as much as $18 billion from June to early July,\par according to Huang Yi, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 China\par Guangfa Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Shanghai.\par     "That amounts to a sudden outflow of $18 billion dollars\par under the capital account," he said.\par     Huang, however, added that such an outflow could be offset\par if China's trade surplus reaches $28 billion in June, based on\par the performance in previous years.\par     Traders said they expected a rough balance of dollar supply\par and demand in June, forecasting that the yuan will mostly move\par in a narrow range of 6.23 to 6.27 during the month.\par     On Wednesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  set its official midpoint at 6.1693,\par or 0.03 percent stronger than Tuesday. The official daily\par trading band allows the exchange rate to fluctuate 2 percent\par either side of the midpoint.\par     The yuan usually follows the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's guidance to rise\par or fall, but the corporate purchases made it buck the trend on\par Wednesday, traders said. Moreover, the marginal rise in the\par midpoint was not a strong enough sign that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  wanted the\par yuan to strengthen.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has guided the yuan to depreciate by 3.2 percent\par against the dollar so far this year to deter speculators from\par betting on a non-stop yuan rise.\par     Before this year's unexpected depreciation, the yuan had\par steadily appreciated by more than 30 percent since its landmark\par revaluation in 2005.\par     In past years, when the yuan was rising, dollar supply was\par sufficient to meet demand, but the yuan's lingering weakness\par this year has persuaded firms to retain more dollars, so dollar\par demand exceeds supply at times, traders said.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data published on Tuesday showed the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  assets rose a moderate 84.6 billion yuan ($13.5\par billion) in April, less than half of the 174.1 billion yuan rise\par seen in March.\par     The increase in April, which mainly reflects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases on the yuan market, was also\par the smallest since July last year.\par     China has seen huge capital inflows and trade surpluses for\par more than a decade, forcing the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  to absorb large quantities\par of dollars to moderate the upward pressure on the yuan.\par             \par  The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par  Item                       Current    Previous  Change (pct)\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint              6.1693     6.1710      0.03\par  Spot yuan                  6.2552     6.2541      -0.02\par  Divergence from midpoint*  1.39                 \par  (pct)                                           \par  Spot change ytd                                 -3.22\par  Spot change since 2005                          32.31\par  revaluation                                     \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 1 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par     \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:\par  Instrument                     Current  Difference\par                                          from onshore\par                                          (pct)\par  Offshore spot yuan             6.2557   -0.01\par  Offshore non-deliverable       6.2555   -1.38\par  forwards                                \par  *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par     \par     >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>\par     RECENT DEVELOPMENTS\par   