\par\par \b \uc2 AUD Lifted By Investment Data, USD/JPY Weighed By Falling Yields -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2014\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] The markets had that month-end exhaustion feel overnight with the main equity bourses close to flat and the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs showing little signs of life. A bid tone in bonds sent the US 10-year yield down to a fresh 2014 low and kept USD/JPY pinned down, while in Asia, AUD outperformed after the release of 1Q capital expenditure and investment intentions data where, although the headline was weaker, the details were more upbeat. For Thursday, Nordic markets are closed for Ascension day while it's also a public holiday in Germany and France. On the data front Spain and US 1Q GDP will grab the attention. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] Austria's EUR6 billion dual-tranche issuance Wednesday marked the biggest syndication ever for the country, the \uc2 Austrian Treasury\uc2  says in press release. The issuance was made up of EUR4 billion in the 1.65% October 2024 conventional bond and EUR2 billion in the June 2020-dated floating rate note indexed to 6-month Euribor. The new floating rate note, which was issued under Austria's euro medium-term note program, is the first FRN of a high quality core euro-zone sovereign since January 2010, and the first syndicated floating rate product issued by Austria, it says. "6-year is a tenor where high quality investors do not have many outstanding issues to choose from," says Martha Oberndorfer, Managing Director of the \uc2 Austrian Treasury\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] The Chinese yuan rises versus the U.S. dollar Thursday, ending a sharp two-day losing streak triggered by month-end demand for the greenback from local importers. Month-end demand for U.S. dollars has evaporated as the spot-date, or contractual delivery date for a spot contract, today moved into the month of June; the last day for a month-end delivery of U.S. dollars was Wednesday. The drop in spot USD/CNY to 6.2474 from an intraday high of 6.2623 had earlier been attributed by one offshore trader to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which seems a less likely explanation. USD/CNY is now 6.2504 from its Wednesday close of 6.2556. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] Chinese oil and gas companies are expected to step up investments in developing unconventional fuels such as shale gas. "We estimate oil companies could spend over $80 billion in the next five years to achieve China's target of 60-100 billion cubic meters of shale gas production," Nomura says in a report. "...We sense an increasing acceptance that oil prices will continue to hover above $100 per barrel as easy-to-extract reserves deplete, despite the fragile global economic recovery." Both \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  and Sinopec have had some success in developing "tight gas" projects that should boost new technologies, it adds. Nymex WTI for July is currently quoting at $102.91/bbl. (biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 ended largely unchanged at 5183.161 in mixed trading on weak offshore leads. Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) ended up 1.4% at NZ$4.49, helped by the weaker New Zealand dollar while Pacific Edge (PEB.NZ) shed 3.4% at NZ$0.86 as investors remained downbeat about its results reported Wednesday. \uc2 Mainfreight\uc2  (MFT.NZ) ended down 0.8% at NZ$13.89 on some profit-taking after strong gains on its FY result earlier this week. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  is pleased that Beijing hasn't flinched from financial sector reform in the face of economic slowdown in China, but measures aimed at reining in shadow financing will hurt growth anyway, the bank says. "This should pay off slowly but surely," SocGen says. "However, we think that the net impact of the reforms and regulations announced so far is more likely to be negative for economic growth in the near term, given the significant contribution of shadow banking to total credit growth." China needs both more equity financing and more bond sales, SocGen says, adding that expanding local government bonds should be a priority to deepen capital markets while reducing reliance on shadow finance in one go. Total social financing, the broadest measure of credit creation that includes shadow finance, fell 25.9% to CNY1.55 trillion in April. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira has strengthened against the dollar Thursday as carry traders eyeing some policy stimulus from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week step in to buy, says Turk Economi Bankasi. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been pressured this week by Prime Minister Erdogan's strong support for lower interest rates. The \uc2 Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will publish the minutes of last week's policy meet later, along with its biannual financial stability report. Markets will comb both for any signs of further monetary easing in Turkey, but there's no presentation from the governor this time. USD/TRY is now at 2.0976. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD remains "on the backfoot since Fonterra," says Chris Hunter, corporate dealing manager at \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions. He says the fact that the Kiwi didn't get a lift along the Aussie after Australian private new capital investment intentions for fiscal 2014-15 were revised up "just shows the market isn't that keen kiwi." He says news that Fonterra cut its forecast payout to its farmer shareholders coupled with weak business confidence Wednesday "could provide the RBNZ with more excuses to be dovish," and the market may pull back on its expectations for rate hikes over the next 12 months. "A couple of minor data releases are combining to change the markets mind about the Kiwi. It just adds some straw to the camel's back and the straws are starting to add up," he says. The pair is at 0.8489 and Hunter says it has support at 0.8470 and resistance at 0.8500. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT [Dow Jones] India's still-high inflation and the potential for rising U.S. interest rates imply a weaker rupee against the U.S. dollar, says Edward Teather, India economist with \uc2 UBS\uc2 , in a note. He expects the USD/INR at 62 at end-December and at 65 at end-2015. However, if the strong mandate for the new government results in strong capital inflows, the USD/INR could be at 58 through end-December, the note says. "A move beyond 58 may take INR into uncharted territory on a real effective exchange rate basis. However, strong capital flows can create such a possibility anyway, in the interim," the note says. The USD/INR is trading at 58.85 vs 58.93 at last close. (nupur.acharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] Weak iron ore prices have sparked stronger interest for imports of iron ore from Indian steel producers. \uc2 JSW Steel\uc2 , one of India's largest steel producers, is set to import additional higher grade ore to offset shortfalls in domestic supply, says a report by ANZ research. India used to be the world's third-largest exporter of iron ore two years ago, but some domestic steel-makers have turned to imports of the raw material as domestic output has slumped due to mining bans and red tape. Earlier this month, international iron ore prices sank below $100 per metric ton after nearly two years due to weak demand from China, the world's largest importer. Iron ore prices fell by 1.3% to close at $96.8/metric ton on Wednesday.(biman.mukherji@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end 0.1% lower at 9109.00, weighed by profit-taking after the index closed at a 40-month high Wednesday, says Yong-cheng International Securities Investment Consultant analyst William Chen. "But investor sentiment remains bullish as foreign investors are still standing on the buy-side in both spot and futures markets," he says, and adds, trading volume remaining above NT$100 billion and positive signals on technical charts also bode well for the market. He expects the index to trade between 9100 and 9200 Friday. Tech heavyweight TSMC (2330.TW) falls 1.6% to NT$122.00 and \uc2 Largan Precision\uc2  (3008.TW) drops 1.5% to NT$1985.00. But Fubon Financial (2881.TW) gains 0.8% at NT$43.10 and Cathay Financial (2882.TW) is up 0.2% at NT$46.00. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] After hitting a 3-month low of 32.77 to the U.S. dollar, Thailand's baht has recouped some losses and currently trades at around 32.73. A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader based in Singapore says market participants suspect the \uc2 Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been selling U.S. dollars around the 32.75-32.77 level to limit the baht's fall Thursday. The Thai baht is experiencing its fourth consecutive loss against the greenback in as many days as the military tightens its grip on the country's administration. USD/THB is now 32.73 from its Wednesday close of 32.68. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 29, 2014 02:56 ET (06:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140529ea5t000uy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\pics