\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Not Far From Equilibrium, Says Former PBOC Adviser -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2014\par 05:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] A former adviser to \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says the country should fully float the yuan since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is trading not far from its equilibrium level. Yu Yongding, a former member of the monetary policy committee of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says in an interview with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 , "Why should China be afraid of floating? I don't think that there would be a large appreciation if the PBOC halted its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." Yu says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs to get its "own house in order" before fully opening up the capital account. "If you have a flexible exchange rate and then liberalize the capital account, the sequencing would be right and the process would be much smoother." (jacky.wong@wsj.com; Twitter: @jackycwong)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot iron ore price weakness, particularly against a resilient Australian dollar, sees the chance of an Australian interest rate cut equalling the chance of a hike in the next 18 months, Deutsche Bank Chief Economist Adam Boyton says. "In our view we aren't yet at the point where we'd include a rate cut in our forecasts off the back of the current iron ore price. Given, however, the post-Budget weakness in sentiment and the current weakness in iron ore, we remain comfortable with our 'low for long' cash rate view." Spot iron ore rose 1% to US$98.50 yesterday after hitting a 20-month low of US$97.50 this week. Australia's biggest export is down about 26% this year, while the Reserve Bank's Australian dollar trade weighted index is up about 2.6%. (david.rogers@wsj.com; Twitter: @DRWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 1.6% at 14267.18 midday as the dollar continues to strengthen helping exporters and financial shares following preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index data showing a rise in May. The PMI number is still in the contraction zone "but at least it's heading in a positive direction. There has been a minor knock-on effect," an equity trading director at a foreign brokerage says. \uc2 Suzuki Motor\uc2  (7269.TO) is up 4.3% at Y2,945 after a Nomura Securities upgrade to Buy. \uc2 Kansai Electric Power\uc2  (9503.TO) is off 3.7% to Y905 after a court ruled against restarting one of its nuclear reactors. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] Some economists are pushing back their expectations for a further shot of easing by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  struck an upbeat tone on the country's prospects. "We have changed our forecast for the timing of additional monetary easing from July at the earliest to October," write Shuichi Obata and Tomo Kinoshita of Nomura Securities. Junya Tanase, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , also says the probability of the BOJ taking additional easing measures in July has "slightly decreased," although the company still expects action in that month. Aside from the timing, speculation for further easing is likely here to stay, as few economists believe that the bank can achieve its target of 2% inflation by next spring with its current policy measures. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com )\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY is steady as investors seek more cues on China's economic outlook. The USD/CNY is last at 6.2340 compared with Wednesday's close of 6.2337. "Investors are closely looking at what measures China will take to prevent declining house prices from derailing its growth," a Beijing-based trader at a local bank says. "It's too early to reach the conclusion that China's slowing economic growth has bottomed out," ANZ economist Zhou Hao who expects the USD/CNY to consolidate between 6.2300 and 6.2400. The preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing PMI rose to a five-month high in May thanks to government spending on public works, economists say on Thursday. (rose.yu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash manufacturing PMI results indicate positive momentum on exports going forward, but there are still a lot of signs of slowing domestic demand, especially weakness in the property market, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Ding Shuang says. "I don't think this reflects the effects of the mini-stimulus measures," he says, adding the PBOC only announced relaxed policies on mortgages in the middle of April. Beijing is likely to continue to come up with measures planned at the beginning of the year to further stabilize growth, but a RRR cut is unlikely in the near term, he says. The preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing PMI rose to a five-month high of 49.7 in May, from a final reading of 48.1 in April. (Mark.Magnier@wsj.com )\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan's industrial output likely rose for the third straight month in April, supported by rising demand for the island's electronic components due to launches of new tech products, economists say. A Wall Street Journal survey of eight economists tips a 3.75% year-over-year rise in April following a 3.05% rise in March. "Restocking demand ahead of China's Labor Day holiday [in early May] likely boosted the island's manufacturing output last month," Yuanta Polaris Research Institute economist Lai Yi-sin says, and adds output of steel and petrochemical products also likely recovered last month after routine maintenance. The production subindex of Taiwan's official PMI hit 66.8 in April, the highest in over one year. That also likely signals faster growth momentum. The data will be published Friday 0800 GMT. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] China's manufacturing sector looks set to benefit from further government spending on infrastructure projects, says CIMB economist Zhang Fan, who predicts the PMI will continue to rise. Local governments "still want to keep economic growth at a certain level," he says, and the central government may quietly make some money available for them to do that. Still, policymakers have to maintain a careful balancing act, continuing growth whist eliminating excess capacity in sectors like metals and chemicals. The government will likely stick to fiscal support and avoid monetary easing for now, Mr. Zhang thinks. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures were supported by an improvement in monthly Chinese manufacturing data this morning. The preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, rose to 49.7 in May, compared with a final reading of 48.1 in April, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said. Oil benchmarks were in negative territory in early morning trade. Nymex WTI is now flat at $104.07/bbl; Brent crude is up 2 cents at $110.57/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] Increased lending by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  suggests it will loosen monetary policy in 2Q, mainly due to the correction in the property market, according to Nomura. It highlights news of a RMB100 billion loan to \uc2 China Development Bank\uc2  in April, for use in shanty town renovation. "We see this as an important policy easing measure that suggests the overall monetary policy stance has indeed eased," says Nomura chief China Economist Zhiwei Zhang. He expects China's GDP to recover to 7.5% by Q414, but warns that growth is likely to slow again in 2015 to 6.8% as "easing measures are likely to push inflation higher next year, constraining the scope for further easing." (david.rogers@wsj.com; Twitter: @DRWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are up early, after manufacturing data for May showed an improvement. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.7% at 2038.23, tipped to face immediate resistance at the 2050 level. The preliminary HSBC China manufacturing PMI rose to 49.7 in May, compared with a final reading of 48.1 in April. The reading is the highest in five months, though it still indicates contraction. "The previous issue dragging the stock market regarding new shares have been digested since more details became available, and the market can more easily focus on other economic and corporate fundamentals in its rebound," says Simon Wang, an analyst at \uc2 Guoyuan Securities\uc2 . "The question remains how sustainable the rebound would be," he adds. Among actives, \uc2 China Vanke Co\uc2 . (000002.SZ) is up 2.1% at CNY7.90 and \uc2 Citic Securities\uc2  (600030.SH) rises 0.9% to CNY11.37. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.8% at 1035.79. (esther.fung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD jumps after \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash China manufacturing PMI for May came in at a five-month high of 49.7 compared with April's final 48.1. The pair is at 0.8578 as the data gave risk appetite a lift on the world growth story, says Stuart Ive, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at OM Financial. Ive says it has resistance around 0.8600. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 21, 2014 23:18 ET (03:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140522ea5m000mq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
