\par\par \b \uc2 China Banks Are "Dormant Volcano", Watch Rate Reform: Nomura -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2014\par 04:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] Whether China's economy crumbles into a Japan-style bust in the years ahead will hinge upon the consequences of lifting of a cap on deposit rates, says Nomura. China's loan growth is outstripping GDP much as in Japan in the late 1980s, and is similarly leveraged on property as collateral. "For China, deposit rate deregulation could be the first trigger point for a credit cycle though, and similar to what happened in Japan, this could be a prolonged process over the next several years." Still, China has the policy levers to address a severe credit crunch and prevent its economy from turning Japanese, Nomura adds. Banks should maintain profit growth of around 8%-10% over the next three years, Nomura says, though its worst case scenario paints earnings falling as much as 42% on average and ROEs bottoming out at 3%. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. and Europe stock declines and higher \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields overnight may have less to do with fears over slow euro-zone GDP growth than with asset managers who are changing their investment allocations through their hedge funds. That's the theory offered by Yunosuke Ikeda, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy and global market research of Nomura Securities in Tokyo, in a morning note. He says that applications to cancel asset management contracts were due May 15, 45 days ahead of the industry wide June 30 hedge fund book closing. As evidence, he points to bonds in Italy and Spain rapidly falling after a recent surge. But, Ikeda acknowledged, "it is risky to read the theme in the market" only on the basis of the market reaction. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei continues to sink, now down 1.7% at 14091.97 midday as the dollar finds little traction (USD/JPY now 101.50). "With a steadily improving jobs picture in the U.S., the low interest rates situation is really confounding--they should be higher. It almost seems anomalous," says Lorne Steinberg, CEO at Montreal-based Lorne Steinberg Wealth Management, which has $160 million in assets under management--one-third of which are in Japan equities. "As rates fall, so does the dollar, which is bad for Japan exporters. But with stock prices looking more attractive as the market get cheaper, we may end up putting some cash to use soon." Japan Display (6740.TO) is down 10% at Y528 after a disappointing earnings forecast. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] As India announces federal election results Friday, the number of seats that are won by India's main opposition Bharatiya Janata Party could determine which way India's benchmark 30-share S&P BSE Sensex is headed in the coming weeks. Sensex has hit lifetime highs in recent days, on expectations that a government led by BJP's candidate Narendra Modi, seen as a pro-reform leader, will come to power. Sensex has jumped 21% since Mr. Modi's prime ministerial candidature was announced last fall. (shefali.anand@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : shefalianand)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR has reached interim technical support at 11,400--the base of the daily Ichimoku Cloud--and may stall for now. The slide began Wednesday when USD/IDR entered the daily Bollinger downtrend channel. Continuation of the bearish signal could activate the weekly Bollinger downtrend channel and thus exacerbate bearish potential for the greenback. While the U.S. dollar outlook has dimmed due to declining \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, the rupiah has been shining due to optimism for the coalition led by the PDI-P. The market expects a strong win for the coalition in the upcoming presidential elections scheduled for July 9. USD/IDR is now 11,410 from its last close of 11,445. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is largely flat as a large bank is aggressively buying the greenback in spite of its broad weakness overseas. The USD/CNY is at 6.2303, down slightly from Thursday's close of 6.2306. The USD/CNY opens lower at 6.2270 due to disappointing U.S. industrial data. "A few minutes later after the market opened, one of the four largest banks bought the dollar significantly, pushing it bounce back to CNY6.2300," says a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank. "We don't know whether there is any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the USD/CNY central parity at 6.1628, down from 6.1640 Thursday. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Conflicting forces are keeping USD/THB trapped inside the daily Ichimoku Cloud consolidation zone spanning 32.33-32.54. While the possibility of prolonged political uncertainty in Thailand has been hurting the baht, the U.S. dollar has its share of troubles. The consistent decline of \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields makes the dollar unattractive to investors, thus weighing on USD/Asia in general. While the Thai political impasse drags on, fresh violence at protests in Bangkok -- which has resulted in several casualties -- is also keeping the baht under pressure. USD/THB is now 32.45 from its Thursday close of 32.48. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Southeast Asian shares follow the wider region lower early Friday, with the Philippines PSEi losing 0.4% and Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index down 0.2%. Malaysia's KLCI is 0.1% lower while Indonesia's JSX is 0.1% higher as the only gainer. The week's trading has mostly been driven by earnings and corporate news, while South China Sea tensions and political developments in Indonesia and Thailand have also influenced index movements. Indonesia has been the week's strongest performer in Southeast Asia, trading up 2% as sentiment gains from political developments that look likely to avoid the necessity of a presidential election runoff. As Citi points out, inflows have been positive, with US$180 million of net purchases in Indonesian markets from foreigners in the week ending Wednesday. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are steady as investors take a breather following Thursday's drop, say analysts. The Shanghai Composite Index is flat at 2024.59 and is tipped to "stay above 2000 before a restart of the IPO market which is expected to take place in coming weeks", says \uc2 Soochow Securities\uc2  analyst Deng Wenyuan. Defense companies gain following anti-Chinese riots in Vietnam; \uc2 Zhonghang Heibao\uc2  (600760.SH) adds 3.6% to CNY6.85 and Jiangxi Hongdu Aviation Industry (600316.SH) rises 2.5% to CNY16.20. Computer and media companies drop due to over stretched valuations; Shanghai Amarsoft Information & Technology (300380.SZ) loses 4.1% to CNY29.31 and Beijing Hualubaina Film & TV (300291.SZ) slides 3.8% to CNY24.18. The Shenzhen Composite Index falls 0.5% to 1017.08. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] The Intercontinental Exchange Dollar Index is trading in subdued fashion Friday after retreating sharply from a five-week high of 80.338 overnight as U.S. 10-year Treasury yields tumbled to six-and-a-half month lows. The short-term technical picture for the USD Index has a negative tinge after a bearish shooting-star candlestick pattern was completed Thursday, and the slow stochastic measure has turned bearish at the overbought level--suggesting risks of further losses near-term. Further downside in the spot Dollar Index may encounter support at the 55-day moving average, coming in now at 79.823, and then at Monday's low of 79.784. The spot Dollar Index was recently at 79.995. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] China bears who are long on bad-debt buyer China Cinda (1359.HK) have a "misplaced sense of security," says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , starting coverage of the stock at sell. "We believe the company is significantly more exposed than banks to China's long-term slowing economy," analysts say. The company was formed 15 years ago to buy bad debt from China's state-owned banks but over the years has become more of a lender itself. Its traditional distressed assets business accounted for just 4% of assets last year, Deutsche notes, while restructuring accounted for more than a quarter of assets. The latter is a sub-prime lending business, Deutsche adds, with nearly two-thirds extended to Chinese property developers at the center of recent market concerns. China Cinda shares down 4.9% at HK$3.72; its still trading 4.5% above its December IPO price. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares fall 0.7% to 8818.24 early Friday, tracking declines overnight on Wall Street and as riot damage to Taiwanese factories in Vietnam weighs on textile and traditional manufacturing sectors, says Capital Securities assistant vice president Diana Wu. "It's still unclear how much damage the factories have suffered and when they can return to production," she says. Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) falls 1.0% to NT$75.80, a day after its steel plant in Vietnam was trashed by rioters. Tech heavyweights also fall, with TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.4% at NT$121.50 and Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.1% at NT$88.50. (eva.dou@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 15, 2014 22:52 ET (02:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140516ea5g000mp\par }\