\par\par \b \uc2 FXCM's 1Q Mixed; April Retail Activity Slumps -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2014\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 6:45 EDT - As FXCM posts mixed 1Q results (a better-than-feared revenue decline but EPS plunging even more than thought), the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage's April results show the environment the company finds itself in. Retail customers' trading volume tumbled 32% on a dollar basis last month, though the institutional side logged 1% growth and made up 43% of FXCM's total. Institutions has been a focus for the company so it's not so reliant on what could be a fickle mom-and-pop investor. The slack trading environment was evident in investment banks' recent 1Q reports, and FXCM CEO Drew Niv says, "Volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is currently at lows only seen twice in the past 20 years." Shares, inactive premarket, are down 21% this year, including 8.3% this week to hit 11-month lows. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 1042 GMT [Dow Jones] The impressive jump in China's soybean imports in April is unlikely to be sustained, says Capital Economics. The numbers may have been distorted by shipments from Brazil arriving earlier this year than they did in 2013 when there were transport problems, it says. Also, the heavy inflow is creating a glut in China, which may provoke a wave of cancellations of shipments in coming months. Additionally, it says, China is to start selling soybeans from its stockpiles next week, further dampening demand. April Soybean import volumes soared 40.7% on-year to 6.5 million metric tons, Customs data released Thursday showed. (isabella.steger@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1041 GMT [Dow Jones] "Draghi will talk dovish, but won't pull the easing trigger today," says RBS credit strategist Alberto Gallo. Low inflation and a rising euro are piling pressure on the ECB to ease monetary conditions further, but the ECB will likely keep rates on hold while trying to talk the euro lower, Gallo says. The small bounce in inflation last week buys the ECB more time before it needs to act, he says. The implications for credit investors? The ECB's easing bias, subdued inflation and attempts to fix the region's financial system mean euro credit will remain relatively insulated from a potential rates shock compared with other global fixed income markets, Gallo says. (ben.edwards@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1040 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will likely avoid a rate cut for the rest of the year despite revising down its GDP growth forecast, as the current account deficit remains relatively high and the economy is still growing strongly, DBS Bank says in a research note. "We probably need to see a significant fall in GDP growth momentum or a narrowing of the annual current account deficit to 2% of GDP" before making the case for a rate cut, writes economist Gundy Cahyadi. He reiterated a forecast for 5.8% GDP growth and a current account deficit of at least 2.5% of GDP this year. Indonesia kept its benchmark interest rate at 7.5% for the sixth straight month Thursday, saying domestic demand remains robust and investment is picking up. (ben.otto@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1040 GMT [Dow Jones] Today's downside breaks in EUR/NOK and USD/NOK have been maintained since \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2 's policy decision and more near-term NOK outperformance is expected by \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . The bank targets 8.1250 for EUR/NOK which now trades at 8.18 from the day's 8.2250 highs, and 5.7550 for USD/NOK from its current level of 5.87.gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1038 GMT [Dow Jones]--With EUR/USD trading above 1.39, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says the ECB President Mario Draghi has little choice but to explain whether the ECB intends to react to euro strength, and if so how. At the April meeting concerns about a strong euro were voiced in the prepared statement, and \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says if the ECB does not want to drive the rate higher it will at least have to maintain the same degree of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it used last time around. EUR/USD is now at 1.3945, and the ECB press conference begins at 1230 GMT. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1034 GMT [Dow Jones] Latest official data show that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been sterilizing its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. According to data released by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought 174.2 billion yuan ($27.9 billion) worth of dollars in March to hold down the value of the Chinese yuan. By comparison, the PBOC bought, on average, 230 billion yuan worth of dollars a month last year to keep the yuan from appreciating too fast, according to an analysis by \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2 . The latest data suggest that "the PBOC may not have sterilized its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more powerful," said \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  analyst Claudio Piron. (lingling.wei@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1031 GMT [Dow Jones]--Better than expected export numbers out of China should not be overestimated. "The trade data shows improvement this month, but the overall growth story in China is still subdued," RBS's strategists write. "April PMI data reported last week declined to 48.1. CASS, the Chinese government think tank, lowered its GDP forecast to 7.4% for 2014. Credit quality for the Chinese banks is also deteriorating following rapid credit growth over past years. To hedge against China slowdown risk, we remain underweight iTraxx Australia," they write. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP closed at a fresh five-month low on Thursday, helped by USD inflows from offshore banks. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is at 44.19, down from late Wednesday's 44.22 level and its lowest close since mid-December. A local bank trader said offshore banks appear to be taking advantage of potential gain in swap points in the non-deliverable forward market. Volume is moderate. Traders expect the USD/PHP to move within the 44.00-44.50 trading range in the coming days. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1024 GMT [Dow Jones] China's local government financing vehicles raised a record volume of funds from issuing bonds in April, spurred by government efforts to use infrastructure construction to support the economy, one of China's domestic bond rating agencies said in a note. China Chengxin International Credit Rating said the increase was also driven by companies needing fresh funds to roll over existing debts that they otherwise would struggle to repay. In April, local government financing vehicles raised more than 460 billion yuan ($74.2 billion), up 8% from a year earlier, and up 27% from March, according to China Chengxin. (Dinny.McMahon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1022 GMT [Dow Jones]--New types of bank instruments could see investors making the same mistakes made before the financial crisis, says \uc2 Kames Capital\uc2 's head of fixed income David Roberts. Instruments include hybrid securities, additional tier-one debt and contingent capital notes. "In some cases these instruments act much like equities, performing well under strong market conditions and entailing significant downside risk when it is not. Under 2008/9 conditions, many of these instruments would be written-off with little probability of recovery any money," says Mr. Roberts. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian gold prices may decline to a range of 24,500 rupees to 26,500 rupees per 10 grams by the end of the current financial year on expectations of a fall in international gold prices and muted domestic demand on the possible continuation of the current import restrictions, says India Ratings and Research in a report. Spot gold prices in India are currently trading at about 29,600 rupees. The rating agency predicts international gold prices to be in the $1,150 to $1,250 an ounce range during the financial year due to gradual winding-up of its monetary easing policy in the U.S. and recovery in the U.S. and Eurozone economy. (debi.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 08, 2014 06:45 ET (10:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140508ea58001o7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
