\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares ride Wall St gains, Europe seen up too\b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2014\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks notch up modest gains, holidays crimp trade\par \par \uc2 * Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  locked in tight ranges, gold firm on safe-haven bid\par \par \uc2 * Data diary mostly empty, Australia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds rates\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  yuan firms, talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps\par \par \uc2 By Wayne Cole and Vidya Ranganathan\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, May 6 (Reuters) - Asian share markets shuffled higher on Tuesday after promising U.S. economic news helped Wall Street to a firmer finish, though activity was again light with Tokyo still on holiday.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets in India and China led the way with modest gains, while MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan added 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 European stocks were also seen inching higher, reversing the previous session's dip.\par \par \uc2 Financial spreadbetters expected Britain's FTSE 100 to open 5 to 7 points higher, or up 0.12 percent, Germany's DAX to open around 15 points higher, or up 0.16 percent, and France's CAC 40 to open 7 to 8 points higher, or up 0.18 percent.\par \par \uc2 S&P futures also pointed to a slightly firmer open.\par \par \uc2 The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting was the only notable economic event in Asia, and the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) kept the cash rate at a record low of 2.5 percent, just as markets had priced in.\par \par \uc2 Australia's main stock index trimmed gains while the Australian dollar firmed a quarter of a U.S. cent after the RBA announced its decision, and said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was high historically, but refrained from calling for it to fall.\par \par \uc2 "The RBA is trying to be desperately neutral," said Annette Beacher, head of Asia-Pacific research at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "For every positive, they included a negative. They are trying to retreat to the sidelines and the Aussie dollar doesn't know what to do."\par \par \uc2 Asian markets in Seoul and Hong Kong were also off on Tuesday to celebrate the Buddha's birthday.\par \par \uc2 Despite the holiday-thinned trading, stock markets did get a fillip after the Institute for Supply Management's U.S. services sector index rose to 55.2 in April, the fastest pace in eight months and easily topping forecasts. A reading above 50 indicates expansion.\par \par \uc2 The data added to evidence that the U.S. economy is emerging from a particularly harsh winter-induced slowdown and provided a welcome offset to worries about China.\par \par \uc2 The CSI300 index of the largest Shanghai and Shenzhen A-share listings was up 0.4 percent, while the Shanghai Composite Index edged 0.3 percent higher to 2,036.83 points, although gains were limited by weakness in the property sector as investors braced for any signs of financial distress among developers.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. is showing signs of recovering from particularly slow momentum in Q1, driven to a significant extent by adverse weather effects, and the euro area remains on a stable, gradual upward trajectory," noted analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In Japan, the sales tax hike frontloading and payback, which began last month, were largely in line with expectations."\par \par \uc2 TIGHT \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  RANGES\par \par \uc2 The better U.S. news helped Wall Street recover early losses. The Dow closed up 0.11 percent, while the S&P 500 gained 0.19 percent and the Nasdaq 0.34 percent.\par \par \uc2 Bonds took the opposite tack, with Treasuries surrendering some of the gains made last week. Yields on 10-year paper lurched up to 2.61 percent, having been as low as 2.57 percent.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  auctions three-, 10- and 30-year debt this week which will be a useful litmus test of investor demand.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, trading was subdued as holidays in London and Tokyo sapped liquidity. The dollar index was steady at 79.499, having drifted between 79.433 and 79.527 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The euro stood at $1.3882, having hugged a tight $1.3864-$1.3887 range. Against the yen, the greenback was little changed at 102.02 as was the euro at 141.62.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan hit its strongest point in two weeks, following talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it touched an 18-month low last week. Spot yuan hit its strongest level since April 22 in early trade, changing hands at 6.2330 at midday.\par \par \uc2 In commodities, gold traded near its highest in three weeks at $1,308.9 an ounce, its safe-haven appeal boosted somewhat by the simmering tensions in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Pro-Russian rebels shot down a Ukrainian helicopter in fierce fighting near the eastern town of Slaviansk on Monday, and Kiev drafted police special forces to the southwestern port of Odessa to halt a feared spread of rebellion.\par \par \uc2 Loss of control of Odessa would be a huge economic and political blow for Ukraine, a country the size of France that borders several NATO countries and aspires to join the military alliance, a primary source of concern for the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices extended their recent decline. Brent crude for June delivery was off 8 cents at $107.64 a barrel, while U.S. crude eased 2 cents to $99.46. (Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2  & Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-6T07:09:31.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140506ea56007hj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
