\par\par \b \uc2 USD/TWD Lower; 30.180 Support Tipped -Trader -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2014\par 05:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD falls to a one-week low owing to exporter sales near the end of the month, a local trader says. The pair is at 30.200 compared with 30.275 traded before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday which lifted it to 30.285 at the close of onshore trading, traders say. The weaker USD/KRW and rising local stocks are also weighing down the USD/TWD, a trader says "but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to step in unless the local pair falls below 30.100." The pair will find initial support at 30.180 for the session, he says. The Taiex is up 0.5%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Bell Potter has cut Australian banks to Hold from Buy, neutralizing a 2 1/2 year bullish call on the sector, after 3 of the 4 major banks hit Bell Potter's top-down share price targets, which were based on a fully franked dividend-yield target of 5%. "I don't expect the 5.00% fully-franked prospective yield to be bid down any further," Bell Potter's managing director, Charlie Aitken, says. "Cash rates at 2.50%, with upside risks, and grossed-up major bank yields at 7.14% suggests an equity yield risk premium of under 5.00% versus cash and I would be very surprised if that got bid down any further from here." Major banks are currently down 0.5%-1.3% after early gains. (david.rogers@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @DRWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY consolidates with upside bias as investors remain bearish about the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to guide it higher. The pair is at 6.2530, unchanged from Monday's close, but its trading levels are mostly higher than Monday's. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the fixing at 6.1556 vs 6.1565 Monday, the fifth straight session it pushes the yuan higher via the reference rate. "The yuan may hover around the current level in the rest of the session as every time the investors sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the big state banks would offer some support," says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.2485/6.2505 from 6.2510/6.2530 late Monday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher in the morning led by port operators after Premier Li Keqiang urged the invigoration of businesses along the Yangtze River. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.2% at 2008.07, and is expected to consolidate around 2000 later. The smaller Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.5% at 1012.52. But analysts say any rebound will likely be short lived. "The rise is unlikely to reverse the downward trend," says Deng Wenyuan, an analyst at \uc2 Soochow Securities\uc2 . Due to a lack of fresh fund inflows, the rise in port stocks would only lead to falls in other sectors. "Broad confidence remains fragile," he says, adding investors are still concerned about the impact of a resumption in initial public offerings although the securities regulator has repeatedly vowed to control the pace of new offerings. Among actives, Shanghai Port (600018.SH) rises 0.7% to CNY4.46, Chongqing Gangjiu (600729.SH) gains 2.3% to CNY10.24 and Wuhu Port (600575.SH) adds 0.6% to CNY3.65. Baby formula maker Beingmate (002570.SZ) falls by the 10% daily limit to CNY23.11 after reporting a 36% fall in first quarter net profit. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR is well-supported at 11,520 where the base of the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone coincides with the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel. As long as this technical floor holds, the U.S. dollar is more likely to rise than fall. The immediate target for USD/IDR on the daily chart is the top of the Bollinger uptrend channel at 11,650. The main driver for greenback strength ahead will be the U.S. Federal Reserve's FOMC meeting that concludes Wednesday, and the U.S nonfarm payrolls report due Friday. Affirmation of the improving U.S. hiring environment - which will likely lead to an earlier lifting of the base U.S. lending rate - will drive the dollar higher. USD/IDR is now 11,590 versus its Monday close of 11,575. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are steady in early Asian trade as markets weigh new Western sanctions against Russia and potentially higher Libyan oil supply. The U.S. and Europe imposed new sanctions on Russian officials and business entities, including the chief of the oil and gas giant OAO Rosneft, Igor Sechin. But analysts said the sanctions were mild and fell significantly short expectations. "The second Libyan port previously blockaded by rebels, the terminal at Zueitina, moved a step closer to resuming exports after a force majeure was lifted," ANZ says. "The port has a capacity of 70,000 barrels a day, and combined with the opening of the terminal at Hariga a fortnight ago, 180,000 barrels of Libyan oil will return to export markets," the bank adds. Weekly oil inventory data from the U.S. API is due later today with expectations of further increase in U.S. oil stocks. Nymex WTI crude is up 2 cents at $100.86/bbl, Brent is up 11 cents at $108.23/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR appears unable to break higher and enter the Bollinger uptrend channel after twice in the last two sessions failing to close above 3.2710. This indicates the dollar's rally has run out of steam and may hence consolidate lower for now. Waiting at 3.2540 is the 20-day Bollinger mid support which will likely prop up the dollar on dips. As long as the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield stays above 2.70% - implying that investors expect the U.S. Federal Reserve to announce another cut in bond purchases after the FOMC meeting on Wednesday - the dollar is likely to remain buoyant. USD/MYR is now 3.2700 versus its Monday close of 3.2645. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Samsung Electronics Co\uc2 . (005930.SE) shares are down 1.7% at KRW1,365,000 in early Asian trade Tuesday after reporting a 5.9% increase in first quarter net-profit at 7.57 trillion won from 7.15 trillion won a year ago. Although performance at its key mobile unit beat market expectations, the company attributed the results partly to "net positive impact from adjustments to reserve items," without elaborating, which analysts don't expect to be a sustainable contributor to mobile earnings. Instead, many in the market expect mobile profit margins to decline towards the end of the year as price competition intensifies among smartphone makers while sales growth continues to slow. Samsung reported an operating profit margin of 19.8% for its mobile unit in the first quarter, largely unchanged from a year ago. HMC Investment Securities analyst Greg Roh says these margins could fall to as low as 13.5% in the fourth quarter. (min-jeong.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Given a data-light day in Asia and with financial markets in Japan closed for Showa Day public holiday, Tuesday's trading session is likely to be a rather quiet, UOB says. The bank thinks recent sideways consolidation for the USD/SGD pair appears incomplete and further range-trading is likely to continue Tuesday amid a dearth of market moving news and data. UOB expects USD/SGD to trade in a 1.2545-1.2585 range short-term. Spot USD/SGD was recently quoted at 1.2576-81 in the interbank market compared with 1.2565-67 in late New York trade Monday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] The parity rate for USD/CNY may have peaked or is close to finding a top, DBS says. Between Jan. 14 and April 22, the parity rate rose 1.1% from 6.0930 to 6.1610, and the official trading band was doubled to plus/minus 2% around parity on March 15, the bank says. DBS notes that back in 2012, the parity rate rose 1.3% from 6.2670 on May 2 to 6.3490 on Aug. 16, and China widened the band to plus/minus 1% around parity in April 2012. The higher parity rates in both episodes should be viewed in the context of higher year ago levels, DBS reiterates. "Put simply, China has not reversed into a weaker exchange rate stance, but is allowing the yuan to consolidate the gains garnered in 2H13." \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate this morning at 6.1556, lower than Monday's fixing of 6.1565. Spot USD/CNY was recently at 6.2538, compared with Monday's close of 6.2530; the offshore spot USD/CNH was at 6.2546. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW dropped back into the Bollinger downtrend channel as of its Monday close, lending a bearish bias to the U.S. dollar this week. USD/KRW opened lower Tuesday following overnight greenback weakness, and may now head for the downtrend channel's base at 1,028. Strong capital inflows have been helping the South Korea won to strengthen despite broadly bullish views on the U.S. unit ahead of the U.S. nonfarm payrolls report on Friday, said a trader. The U.S. dollar has been firm against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of the elevated U.S. 10-year Treasury yield--which reveals that investors expect the U.S. Federal Reserve may turn more hawkish on improving jobs data. USD/KRW is now 1,032.3 versus its Monday close of 1,035.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY's upside bias remains in play, though weakness in the U.S. dollar index overnight resulted in a lower USD/CNY daily benchmark rate of 6.1556 versus 6.2565 the day before. Traders are still wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan strength at bay. The continuation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - despite verbal warnings from the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  department that China should keep on its track for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform - will likely encourage traders to bet on further yuan weakness to come. The yuan hit a new 16-month low of 6.2583 on Monday. USD/CNY is now 6.2535 versus its Monday close of 6.2530. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 28, 2014 23:40 ET (03:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140429ea4t0008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
