\par\par \b \uc2 China's Slowdown Likely to Spur Stimulus, Not Reforms\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis and Mark Magnier   \par \uc2 1,074 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2014\par 03:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--China's slowdown in growth, widely expected to continue through the second quarter at least, will boost pressure on Beijing to dial up stimulus measures and dial back structural reforms, economists argued.\par \par \uc2 Over the past week, China's premier and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief underscored their focus on growth, telling business leaders at a conference in southern Hainan island that they would use monetary policy or "slightly bigger adjustment" measures if growth slips below acceptable levels. Already, Beijing has announced plans to build more railways and cut some taxes. China's critics also argue that in recent months Beijing has devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help out troubled exporters.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, China reported that growth fell to 7.4%, an 18-month low, from 7.7% in the fourth quarter, with little sign that China will manage much faster growth in the near term. Indeed some economists argued that economic activity was slower than China reported. According to Capital Economics, a London research firm, which looks at measures of electricity, shipping and real estate a, China's economic activity slowed in the first quarter to around 6%, year-over-year, compared with 6.3% in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 "Growth is lower than the official estimates," said \uc2 Qinwei Wang\uc2 , a Capital Economics economist, but he says the slowdown "wasn't as bad as people feared; it isn't a hard landing."\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  analyst \uc2 Tao Wang\uc2  says that the growth slowdown means that difficult reforms will be put off indefinitely. "The principle of reform sequencing is simply 'the easiest ones first,' "Ms. Wang wrote in an analyst note.\par \par \uc2 But, Ms. Wang said, more difficult efforts, including restructuring state-owned enterprises, land reform and a nationwide property tax "will progress more slowly."\par \par \uc2 Stimulus measures in coming weeks will likely involve a deepening of some of the same sort of targeted spending measures that were recently rolled out, including stepped-up investments in transportation, urban renewal and alternate energy projects, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "They'll probably do a bit more of the same," said \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist M.K. Tang, adding that he expects second-quarter growth to roughly match first-quarter results, a cause for concern in official circles. "I don't think they feel comfortable to move ahead with reforms."\par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect there will be a reserve-requirement reduction to give banks more money to lend. On Wednesday, The State Council announced that it was cutting the reserve requirement ratio for county-level rural commercial banks that "meet certain standards" without clarifying what the standards refer to.\par \par \uc2 The government could spur growth with a more accommodative monetary policy, which would help private companies facing a slowing economy, weak demand in global markets and high interest rates, analysts said. But it will more likely continue its long-standing reliance on investment even as government spending becomes less effective, they added.\par \par \uc2 "They have a penchant to use fiscal policy," said ANZ economist Li-Gang Liu. The mini-stimulus program announced in early April "will very likely run out of steam by the third or fourth quarter," he added. "It's a case of diminishing returns."\par \par \uc2 Slower growth may also make it unlikely for Beijing to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate much any time soon. On Tuesday, the U.S. slammed \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and allowing the yuan to fall 2.5% against the dollar in the past two months. A semiannual Treasury report called the decline in the yuan "unprecedented" and pressed Beijing to disclose its market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more regularly.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday urged patience. Beijing will continue to "perfect the managed floating exchange rate system," said Foreign Ministry spokeswoman Hua Chunying in a regular briefing. "We hope the U.S. side can correctly understand and properly handle trade issues related to the exchange rate."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were prompted by an attempt to change market psychology. For the past several years the yuan has risen steadily in value, which has prompted speculators to bet on its continued appreciation and increased pressure for further yuan strengthening.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think that competitiveness concerns were the key driver of the policy-induced depreciation," said \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  economist Louis Kuijs, a former \uc2 World Bank\uc2  China economist. "But the longer China needs to wait for convincing export growth and organic growth, the more likely it is that keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down is a tempting policy measure to try to instill some life in exports and manufacturing."\par \par \uc2 Wang Xueqin, export manager with Jiaxin Silk, a Shenzhen-listed company based in Zhejiang Province, said exports are barely holding even with the exchange-rate depreciation. Tougher competition has forced the company to innovate, visit clients more often and spend more time at overseas trade fairs. "Our main focus is to safeguard orders and maintain market share, not worry about margins," she said.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, China released a number of data that pointed to continued economic weakness.\par \par \uc2 Most countries measure their GDP data on a quarter-by-quarter basis, annualized and adjusted for seasonal variations. China doesn't provide all that data, so market analysts make their own estimates. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimated that on a quarter-to quarter basis, China's economy slowed more sharply, to 5.4% annually in the first quarter from 8.4% in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 Housing sales in the first quarter fell 7.7% to 1.11 trillion yuan ($178 billion) compared with a year earlier, as lending limits and falling prices in some Chinese cities weakened housing demand. Growth in property investment and land sales have slowed, while construction starts have also declined by 25% in the first three months this year.\par \par \uc2 Industrial production grew 8.8% in March, compared with 8.6% year-over-year growth in January and February, which were combined to try to reduce the distortions related to the Lunar New Year holiday, \uc2 China's National Bureau of Statistics\uc2  said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Retail sales, meanwhile, posted 12.2% year-over-year growth for March, a modest increase over the 11.8% year-over-year rise seen in January and February, as an anticorruption and --extravagance campaign continued to discourage spending.\par \par \uc2 Kersten Zhang, Fanfan Wang, Lilian Lin and Chuin-Wei Yap\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 17-04-14 0132GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140417ea4h0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
