\par\par \b \uc2 US Slams China's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Moves -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 1,031 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2014\par 03:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 4/16/14)\par \par \uc2 By William Mauldin\par \par \uc2 The U.S. blasted China for its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, calling the decline in the yuan "unprecedented" and pressing Beijing to disclose its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets more regularly.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department, in its semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  released Tuesday, said the weakening of the yuan would "raise particularly serious concerns" if it signals a retreat from Beijing's publicly stated policy of scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to let market forces play a bigger role.\par \par \uc2 The report stopped short of labeling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator, a move U.S. administrations have avoided for the past two decades.\par \par \uc2 But the warning marked the Obama administration's latest move to push Chinese officials to avoid slipping from their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commitments despite weakening growth in the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 American manufacturers and some lawmakers have complained that China's artificially weak yuan gives the country an advantage in trade with the U.S. In recent years, Beijing had allowed the yuan to appreciate, before shifting toward a recent bout of depreciation this year.\par \par \uc2 The shift since the beginning of the year has been "quite unusual," said \uc2 Nicholas Lardy\uc2 , a senior fellow at the Peterson Institute for International Economics and an expert in the Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials had previously touted their efforts to persuade China to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate and want to make sure they aren't ignoring the recent weakness. Still, many observers believe the yuan's weakness is a temporary move to deter speculators and that U.S. warnings are unlikely to have a policy impact in China, which now faces a potentially slower rate of economic growth and smaller trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 "Complaints from the U.S. are going to increasingly fall on deaf ears, given the appreciation in past few years and the adjustment in China's current account," said David Loevinger, a former \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  official who is now an analyst at investment firm \uc2 TCW Group Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Chinese Embassy in Washington didn't immediately respond to a request for comment. \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2 , vice governor of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , told a U.S. audience last week that the movements of the yuan in February and March were normal and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "float in both ways" rather than the one-way appreciation seen in recent years.\par \par \uc2 A broad range of economic indicators -- from trade to consumer spending to industrial production -- have showed a slowing Chinese economy, which has reduced demand for the yuan.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials disagree with the idea that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at equilibrium.\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese authorities have been unwilling to allow an appreciation large enough to bring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to market equilibrium, opting instead for a gradual adjustment which has now been partially reversed," the Treasury Departing said in the report. "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should disclose \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to increase the credibility of its monetary policy framework and to promote exchange-rate and financial-market transparency."\par \par \uc2 The report also softened slightly its tone toward some policies in Europe. Six months ago, the Treasury employed unusually sharp language in criticizing Germany's economic policies and blamed the "anemic pace of domestic demand growth" for dragging down its neighbors and the rest of the global economy. This month's report registered the same concerns but in less strident language, highlighting low inflation in Europe that has "reinforced deflationary pressure on the periphery."\par \par \uc2 As with Europe, the U.S. said it is "imperative" for Japan to focus on an increase in domestic demand. A spokesman for the Japanese Embassy in Washington said he couldn't comment on the report on Tuesday evening. He directed inquires to \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo, which didn't immediately respond to emails.\par \par \uc2 The report said South Korea should limit won \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exceptional circumstances. The South Korean Embassy also didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The latest report underscored the continued clash between major economies almost five years into a weak global recovery.\par \par \uc2 The U.S.'s singling out of China comes as emerging markets have complained that the Federal Reserve's bond-purchasing program, known as quantitative easing, initially put downward pressure on the dollar and sent a flood of capital their way.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. sees economic growth returning following the financial crisis, the Fed is slowly reducing its quantitative easing, generating more complaints.\par \par \uc2 "The tough words in the report signal an escalation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions among the major advanced and emerging market economies," said Cornell University economist \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2 , a former head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's China division. "The U.S. has started pulling back on quantitative easing but other countries including China and Japan are continuing to rely on monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation to buffer short-term growth."\par \par \uc2 Facing slower economic growth, Chinese officials have discussed the importance of shifting away from an emphasis on export growth to boosting consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 Beijing is expected to disclose its latest economic growth figures overnight.\par \par \uc2 "Both countries are struggling to promote growth, and I think both countries wouldn't mind if a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped boost growth," \uc2 TCW Group\uc2 's Mr. Loevinger said. "And not everybody can have a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 In mid-March \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widened the band within which it allows the yuan to trade, after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to push down the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since then the yuan has continued to weaken, leaving trading partners uncertain about China's monetary intentions.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have indicated the deprecation is designed to avoid allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators to make a one-way bet on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "China has repeatedly said the movement of exchange rates should be increasingly two way, and I think that's what they've been trying to accomplish over recent months," said Mr. Lardy of the Peterson Institute.\par \par \uc2 While China has adjusted its level of disclosure in recent years, U.S. officials want \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to provide some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data on a higher-frequency basis," Mr. Lardy said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Eric Morath contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-04-14 0156GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140416ea4g0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
