\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China c.bank says to free up deposit rates when time is right\b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2014\par 14:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, comment)\par \par \uc2 BOAO, China, April 10 (Reuters) - China will choose a proper time to liberalise bank interest rates and gradually reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to give the yuan more freedom to move, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "We will choose a proper time to widen the floating range of deposit rates," Zhou told reporters and delegates during the annual Boao forum.\par \par \uc2 Zhou said last month deposit rates were likely to be liberalised in one to two years, but government economists and policy advisers told Reuters they believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was treading cautiously as economic growth slow.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to introduce a deposit insurance scheme before liberalising deposit rates to protect savers in case a freed-up market leads to major turbulence for smaller banks.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the controls on deposit rates to be lifted gradually. The current ceiling on deposit rates is 110 percent of the benchmark set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Zhou reaffirmed that the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would gradually exit from regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to give the yuan more breathing space.\par \par \uc2 "The renminbi (yuan) floating range has been expanded to 2 percent from 1 percent, the next step will be moving in this direction," he said without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  loosened its grip on the yuan by doubling its daily trading band, adding teeth to a promise it would allow market forces to play a greater role in the economy.\par \par \uc2 China will further encourage mainland firms to issue yuan bonds in Hong Kong and support Hong Kong firms to issue panda bonds on the mainland, Zhou said. (Reporting by \uc2 Aileen Wang\uc2  in Boao and Kevin Yao in Beijing; Editing by Robert Birsel)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-13T12:53:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140410ea4a00kg8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
