\par\par \b \uc2 China in No Hurry to Halt Yuan Slide Despite New U.S. Pressure-3-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei and Bob Davis   \par \uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2014\par 18:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--China is in no hurry to halt a recent slide in the yuan despite new U.S. pressure to get Beijing to strengthen the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Interviews with a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, bankers and analysts suggest Beijing won't move in the near term to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening. The yuan has fallen 2.3% this year, making the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the worst-performing in Asia against the dollar, according to \uc2 HSBC Holdings PLC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The drop prompted a senior official at the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  on Monday to warn that Washington is looking closely at whether Beijing's efforts to devalue the yuan represent a shift in policy that could start a round of competitive devaluations. It would "raise serious concerns," the Treasury official said, if China is backing away from making the yuan a more market-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Beijing considers the drop part of its effort to drive out short-term speculators who see the yuan's strengthening as a sure bet, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official and others. That makes Beijing unlikely to act unless the yuan, also known as the renminbi, moves too sharply.\par \par \uc2 "Part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's job is to maintain stability in the renminbi's exchange rate," said an official at the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Barring a sharp decline that could destabilize the country's economy, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make it higher,"\par \par \uc2 The drop also reflects investor concern about a further slowdown in the Chinese economy, say analysts and investors. It comes after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month doubled the range in which the yuan is permitted to trade on a daily basis, which has exacerbated the slide spurred by Beijing.\par \par \uc2 "Market participants have taken this as a cue to sell yuan as they became increasingly concerned over recent signs of slowing economic growth," said \uc2 Zhang Ming\uc2 , a senior economist at the \uc2 Chinese Academy of Social Science\uc2 , a government think tank.\par \par \uc2 The yuan strengthened slightly Tuesday, closing in Beijing at 6.1968 yuan per dollar, compared with 6.2123 on Friday. China's markets were closed Monday for a public holiday.\par \par \uc2 Longer term, investors and analysts widely believe China will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resume its rise against the dollar, adding to a more-than-33% strengthening since 2005. They cite expected capital inflows, the potential for an improving economy later in the year and concern about producing a backlash in Washington that could cause congressional action to penalize China.\par \par \uc2 "I do not believe that the Chinese government has changed its policy of a gradually appreciating yuan, so I would expect an appreciating bias into the year-end," said Suanjin Tan, a Singapore-based fixed-income portfolio manager at \uc2 BlackRock Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But its current stance is likely to make the issue a lingering source of contention between Beijing and Washington. The U.S. and other Western countries have long urged China to let the yuan rise at a faster pace. A weaker yuan, they have said, gives Chinese exporters an unfair edge over their foreign competitors.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's thinking have argued that depreciation is needed to drive out short-term speculators who expect steady appreciation and have used different methods to buy yuan, putting even more pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 An influx of speculative capital last year--driven in part by investors betting on yuan-denominated assets--complicated China's efforts to manage the economy, contributing to property bubbles and injecting excess cash into the financial system. One sign of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the yuan's rise: \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves grew nearly $510 billion last year, to $3.8 trillion, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars with yuan and tried to slow the appreciation of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese authorities will remain cautious in the near term regarding any potential resurgence in speculative appetite for the yuan," said Paul Mackel, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 China hopes to convince investors that the yuan could just as easily weaken as strengthen, giving them pause when they consider piling on bullish bets that could add upward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Greater two-way fluctuation, they maintain, is necessary before China can give the market a bigger role in determining the yuan's value--a move the government must make to fulfill its ambitions that the yuan become an important global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials also have argued in the past year that the yuan is approaching its fair-market value, meaning that investors shouldn't expect it to increase in value much more. As evidence, they point to the country's shrinking trade surpluses.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late February to drive down the yuan's value against the dollar. It guided its parity rate--the daily rate at which the yuan is allowed to trade 2% either way--lower against the dollar. It also instructed banks to purchase the dollar, according to traders and people familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com and Bob Davis at bob.davis@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 08-04-14 1645GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140408ea48000b4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
