\par\par \b \uc2 NZD/USD Higher Late, Supported By Local Data -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2014\par 06:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is trading higher, benefiting from solid 1Q business confidence in New Zealand and an unwinding of AUD/NZD positions, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bank Senior Strategist Imre Speizer. The NZD/USD is at 0.8629 late afternoon in New Zealand after news business confidence held firm in the first quarter and remains at its highest level since mid-1994. In contrast, the Aussie lost some ground after news business conditions remained soft in March across the Tasman, although both the AUD/USD and the NZD/USD remained well supported over the session with the Kiwi outperforming. Speizer says the NZD/USD has some initial support at 0.8573 and resistance at 0.8700. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] Stocks on Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index catch a break Tuesday, heading for their best session in a week with a 0.9% gain to 22574.30 as of midday. A relief rally for beat-up mainland shares drives gains; \uc2 Great Wall Motor\uc2  (2333.HK), which this month launches a long-awaited--and previously delayed--H8 sport utility model, is up 6.2% at HK$41.45. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Jobs Index suggests Australia has passed a cyclical low point in employment growth, but the labor market isn't expected to match population growth this year, resulting in unemployment rising to 6.5% in 4Q 2014, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  senior economist Justin Smirk says. The index fell to 48.6 in March from 48.8 in February, remaining below the long-run average of 50. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  sees employment growth of 1.3% in 2014 vs 0.7% in 2013. March employment data are due Thursday, with the market expecting a 6.1% unemployment rate and a 10,000 fall in employment. (david.rogers@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @DRWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0357 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares rise further by the midday break, with banks leading gains amid hopes that Beijing may shift its focus to pumping up economic growth from economic restructuring after a slowdown, analysts say. The Shanghai Composite Index rises 1.3% to 2086.39 and BOC International (China) investment adviser Jacky Zhang tips the index to trade in a 2050-2100 band. Investors are likely ditching small-capitalization stocks, such as tech shares, while rushing into blue-chips with stable dividends and earnings growth he says. Among actively traded stocks, coal miners gain on hopes that Beijing would eliminate more outdated capacity; \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) adds 2.6% to CNY14.37 and Yanzhou Coal Mining (600188.SH) advances 2.4% to CNY6.53. Banks rise for the second straight session on expectations that regulators may soon release guidelines about the issuance of preferred stocks; Industrial Bank (601166.SH) is up 4.3% to CNY9.99 and China Minsheng Banking (600016.SH) is 2.8% higher at CNY7.99. The Shenzhen Composite Index rises 0.6% to 1074.17. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are higher, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, says a local trader. The bond market is muted after demonstrators against a trade accord with China said late Monday they will end their weeks-long occupation of the legislature by Thursday evening, while above-view CPI and trade data on Monday also fail to move the market. "The local bond market basically shrugged off domestic political and economic factors and just focused on U.S. Treasurys," the trader says, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to change its monetary policy unless inflationary pressure increases substantially. The most-traded five-year bond yield falls to 1.0918% from 1.0945% late Monday; the trader tips a 1.0800%-1.0950% band for the session. The 10-year bond yield is at 1.5750% vs 1.5800%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls as the central parity was set lower to track the fall in overnight dollar, but the pair stays in a narrow range as traders are cueless about the yuan's future direction. The pair is at 6.2064 vs 6.2123 late Friday (China's market was closed Monday due to public holiday), after trading between 6.2048 vs 6.2080. The central parity is set at 6.1527 vs 6.1557. A Beijing-based local bank trader says the pair may trade between 6.2000 and 6.2100 in the rest of the day as traders await March trade data due Thursday. The market is also waiting Beijing's response after the U.S. said it is looking closely at whether Beijing's efforts to devalue the yuan represents a shift in policy that could start a round of competitive devaluations. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT [Dow Jones] Global equity markets may be in for an "extended pause", according Australian fund manager Perpetual. "High valuations in momentum stocks is making it very hard for the markets to continue their rally from 2012 and 2013, with most bourses down in 2014 as investors sell overvalued sectors and companies and buy cheaper equities or less expensive government bonds," Perpetual's head of investment market research, Matt Sherwood says. "Record margins and corporate earnings have been the driving force behind a two-year share market rally, which has been vastly larger than the rise in earnings. However, with profit margins at peak levels and now decreasing as the U.S. economy improves and wages growth accelerates, it is clear that margins cannot carry the market higher and that the sole hope for the side therefore is an improving economy." He feels global economic strength this year will be "marginal and likely to be back-loaded in the second half of the year", investors may have to wait for earnings to catch up to share prices. (david.rogers@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @DRWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher, lifted by short-covering by foreign investors after \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called trading rooms at some banks to check their USD sales, say two local traders. The pair is at 30.255, higher than 30.222 traded before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, which lifted it to 30.320 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. The traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called them about an hour before the close on Monday to suggest they not make USD sales of over US$2 million and also called earlier this morning to ask for the names of the clients of their USD sales, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is monitoring those behind the USD sales to discourage \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a bit nervous as the greenback approached NT$30.200, which can be seen a major support level for the USD/TWD," a trader says. They tip the pair to trade in 30.250-30.280 band for the session. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] The yuan is slightly stronger Tuesday as spot USD/CNY slips back down toward 6.2000 after a bout of overnight U.S. dollar weakness. Now at 6.2065 versus the U.S. unit, the yuan is 0.2% stronger than its Friday close. Chinese markets were closed Monday. The drop in the U.S. dollar index triggered a markdown in the PBOC's daily USD/CNY benchmark rate to 6.1527 from 6.1557, which then set the tone for spot USD/CNY. But with the daily Bollinger uptrend channel still in effect as long as spot USD/CNY stays above 6.1910, yuan weakness in the near term may persist. A \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  official has said that it is monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yuan, which contradicts the authorities' claims of wanting to liberalize its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei remains down 1.1% at 14,641.61 midday on follow-through selling after Monday's 1.7% fall and the dollar's dive under Y103 (now Y102.85). "Overseas investors are dumping internet-related stocks, largely in reaction to selloffs in their overseas peers overnight," says an equity trading director at a foreign brokerage, noting some big-ticket sales from pension funds seeking to rotate their stock holdings. The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is set to announce its monetary policy decision some time during the lunch break. "A move to buy more REITs or ETFs might be welcomed by the market, but the BOJ would have to show the data that fueled the rationale for more asset purchases, which it just doesn't have right now," he adds. All 33 subindexes remain down, with Internet names underperforming; \uc2 Yahoo Japan\uc2  (4689.TO) is off 2.3% at Y484, SoftBank (9984.TO) is down 3.3% at Y7,309, Rakuten (4755.TO) is off 2.4% at Y1,317. Social networker mixi (2121.TO) is off 2.6% at Y6,040, while Gree (3632.TO) is down 2.9% at Y1,046. Volume is light at just 885 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's February industrial production index likely grew 6.4% year over year, according to a median estimate of 13 economists in a poll by The Wall Street Journal. This compares with a 3.7% expansion in January. Economists say rise is probably a result of seasonal factors as production resumed after the Lunar New Year holiday a month earlier. "Last month's manufacturing production print was notably soft and we look to this month's print to clarify whether this softness was due to Lunar New Year distortions or less modest electronics demand," ANZ says adding a lower base of comparison may provide a technical boost to February's data. The official data is due Thursday 0401 GMT. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0251 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD has slipped into the daily Bollinger downtrend channel following a slide in the U.S. dollar index overnight. Confirmation of entry into the downtrend channel--contingent on a Tuesday close below 30.310--would suggest more U.S. dollar weakness ahead. However, the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone that spans 30.050-30.320 may delay further downside for the dollar. Traders are also cautious of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too rapidly. USD/TWD is now 30.255 from its Monday close of 30.320. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 08, 2014 00:44 ET (04:44 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140408ea48000er\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
