\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan slips while c.bank appears to signal temporary ceiling\b0\par\par\uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2014\par 07:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint appears to signal ceiling at 6.2/dollar\par     * Traders say that level will keep punters losing money\par     * They report signs of recovering corporate dollar sales\par     * Recent round of yuan depreciation appears largely over\par \par     By Lu Jianxin and Pete Sweeney\par     SHANGHAI, April 3 (Reuters) - China's yuan edged lower\par against the dollar on Thursday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixed a\par weaker official midpoint, helping end a  rebound in the Chinese\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the previous two days.\par     The yuan briefly firmed beyond 6.2 per dollar on Tuesday and\par Wednesday, but the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) weak midpoint\par on Thursday appears to signal that it wants to make the level a\par ceiling for the yuan for now, traders said.\par     Spot yuan stood at 6.2068 per dollar at midday,\par dropping 0.02 percent from Wednesday's close, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par fixed its midpoint at 6.1520, down 0.04 percent from the\par previous trading day.\par     The market believes speculators built up heavy long\par positions based on the view the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would not weaken below\par 6.2, but those positions were smashed when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  engineered a\par round of yuan depreciation in February and March. \par     The yuan depreciated a net 2.54 percent in February and\par March, its biggest two-month loss since China established the\par domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in 1994. Analysts said Beijing\par took advantage of the country's seasonally weak foreign trade in\par the first quarter of the year to strike at speculators.\par     But traders reported increased corporate dollar sales this\par week, a sign that China's foreign trade may have started to\par recover, adding dollar supply to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par     As such, they said they believed this round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -engineered yuan deprecation may be largely over.\par     Traders have widely expected China's foreign trade to\par recover in the second quarter, creating a large trade surplus.\par With dollars plentiful in the domestic market, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  will\par have to pay heavily to force yuan depreciation by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par buy dollars.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also posted a slew of weaker-than-expected\par economic data for the first quarter of this year, sparking\par worries over the health of the world's second-largest economy. \par     On Wednesday, China acted for the first time this year to\par steady its stumbling economy by cutting taxes for small firms\par and announcing plans to speed up the construction of railway\par lines. \par     Despite a few cases of PBOC-engineered yuan depreciation,\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has generally staged one-way appreciation and has\par risen more than 33 percent since its landmark revaluation in\par 2005, supported by factors such as strong capital flows into the\par country, including those from trade surpluses.\par               \par  The onshore spot yuan market at a glance:    \par  Item                          Current    Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint                 6.1520     6.1493    -0.04%\par  Spot yuan                     6.2068     6.2056    -0.02%\par  Divergence from midpoint*     0.89%                \par  Spot change ytd                                    -2.46%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation                 33.35%\par  \par   *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET       \par The offshore yuan market at a glance: \par  Instrument                       Current    Difference from\par                                              onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan          *    6.2079     -0.02%\par  Offshore non-deliverable         6.2251     -1.17%\par  forwards               **                   \par  *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par .     \par     >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>\par     RECENT DEVELOPMENTS\par      - New quotas rekindle debate over dim sum market's future\par  \par      - Flood of offshore yuan bonds may spark higher yields\par  \par     - China investors face bumpy ride as reform speculation\par intensifies \par     - CHINA MONEY - \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  preparing market for more yuan\par volatility \par         \par     KEY DATA POINTS\par     - Yuan is positive outlier compared with plunging emerging\par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/cyx46v"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/cyx46v}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - Hot money tracker: Hot money inflows slowed to a trickle\par in December 2013 GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/saz74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/saz74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - China's trade surpluses mainly driven by weak imports\par rather than strong exports. GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/qav68s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/qav68s}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - Corporate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  behavior reflects yuan appreciation\par expectations. GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/tyx74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/tyx74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - Despite relatively stable dollar/yuan exchange rate, the\par yuan is appreciating on a trade-weighted basis. GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/sed74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/sed74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par      \par    >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  \par \par  (Editing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacqueline Wong\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-6T05:11:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140403ea4300797\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
