\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar's Moment Is Here-2-\b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 09:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 3/27/14)\par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 It is hard to imagine a clearer set of signals for a stronger dollar than those of the past month. Yet the greenback's charts look . . . meh. That seems to be because the world's most powerful central banks have conditioned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to trust actions over words.\par \par \uc2 Despite the Federal Reserve bringing forward the timetable for U.S. monetary tightening, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  laying the groundwork for easier euro-zone policy and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  driving the yuan to 12-month lows, The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index is down slightly from its level at the end of February. That index, which is based on a basket of 16 major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is telling us that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are adopting a show-me-the-money posture. Too often they have seen the Fed return to bond buying and the ECB find excuses to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 From a big-picture perspective, it is tempting to argue that this is the dollar's moment -- or at least it should be -- against the euro.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, ECB President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  repeated his famous "whatever it takes" line to describe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to prevent falling prices, while Deutsche Bundesbank President \uc2 Jens Weidmann\uc2  -- until now, a potent source of resistance to looser ECB policy -- toyed with the ideas of negative interest rates and bond buying.\par \par \uc2 Could there be a more stark contrast to the comments from Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  last Wednesday, when she hinted that U.S. rate increases could come sooner than previously thought?\par \par \uc2 Other factors are dollar-positive, too -- and not just in relation to the euro. Most important, the U.S. current-account deficit hit its lowest level in 14 years last quarter. Over time, a narrower current-account deficit should correlate with a stronger dollar as it takes pressure off the U.S. to finance its debts and means the exchange rate won't need to adjust lower to attract inflows.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, policy in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has meaningfully changed. With some heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit hard against speculators that had bet on a perpetually rising yuan. This was so effective that when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later widened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band, a chastened market dutifully took the yuan even lower.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan means a loss of competitiveness for emerging-market manufacturers that compete. That should translate into competitive exchange-rate depreciations in Asia and elsewhere, all of which feeds into a stronger dollar in general.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, with the possible exception of the U.K., the U.S. is exhibiting the healthiest growth outlook among the world's advanced economies.\par \par \uc2 And yet, this perceived outperformance remains a relative concept. There are still millions of Americans in need of work. It is merely that the outlook for an otherwise lackluster U.S. economy looks better than almost everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps that is what makes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders reluctant to go all in. After years of false starts, they know that U.S. economic numbers can sour again and that when that happens the Fed can easily crank up the printing presses again.\par \par \uc2 Action, not words -- that is what the market wants to see.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 27-03-14 0803GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140327ea3r0007b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
