\par\par \b \uc2 All Signs Point to a Stronger Dollar, Except the Dollar Itself -- Horizons\b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par By Michael J. Casey \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's hard to imagine a clearer set of signals for a stronger dollar than those of the past month. Yet the greenback's charts look... meh.\f28 \par \par \uc2 That seems to be because the world's most powerful central banks have conditioned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to trust actions over words.\par \par \uc2 Despite the Federal Reserve bringing forward the timetable for U.S. monetary tightening, the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  laying the groundwork for easier euro-zone policy, and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  driving the yuan to 12-month lows, The Wall Street Journal dollar index is down slightly from its level at the end of February. There's nothing in that index, which is based on a basket of 16 major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to suggest the dollar is breaking free of a long-lasting sluggish run versus the rest of the world.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are adopting a show-me-the-money posture, as are those who set prices in short-term interest rate markets. After years in which the Fed has shown itself willing to crank up its bond-buying at the first sign of economic softness while the ECB has trotted out every excuse not to follow suit, they're unwilling to trust that these latest hints will be backed by a meaningful rebalancing in international monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  strategist Kit Juckes wrote Wednesday that the euro/dollar exchange rate "will start to move meaningfully lower when, but only when, the U.S. rates market starts to price in Fed hikes." He doesn't see that happening "much earlier" than the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 From a big-picture perspective, though, it is tempting to argue that this is the dollar's moment -- or at least it should be versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, ECB officials showed unusual eagerness to act against the ever-mounting threat of deflation in the euro zone, a scourge that can only be fought with looser monetary policy and, by extension, a weaker euro. ECB President Mario Draghi repeated his famous "whatever it takes" line to describe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to prevent falling prices, while Deutsche Bundesbank President Jens Weidmann--until now, a potent source of resistance to looser ECB policy--toyed with the ideas of negative interest rates and "quantitative easing," or bond-buying.\par \par \uc2 Could there be a more stark contrast to the comments from Fed Chairwoman Janet Yellen last Wednesday, when she hinted that U.S. rate increases could come more quickly than previously thought?\par \par \uc2 Other factors are also dollar-positive--and not just in relation to Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most important, the U.S. current account deficit got down to its lowest level in 14 years last quarter, largely because of shale-oil and gas-extraction techniques, which are consistently reducing American dependence on foreign energy imports. Over time, a narrower current-account deficit should correlate with a stronger dollar as it takes pressure off the U.S. to finance its debts and means the exchange rate won't need to adjust lower to attract inflows.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, policy in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has meaningfully changed. With some heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit hard against speculators that had bet on a perpetually rising yuan. This was so effective that when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later widened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band, a now chastened market dutifully took the yuan even lower.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan makes sense. The once-gangbusters Chinese economy is slowing. But it means a loss of competitiveness for emerging-market manufacturers that compete with China's giant exporting machine. That should translate into competitive exchange-rate depreciations in Asia and elsewhere, all of which feeds into a stronger dollar in general.\par \par \uc2 What's more, with the possible exception of the U.K., the U.S. is exhibiting the healthiest growth outlook among the world's advanced economies.\par \par \uc2 And yet, this perceived outperformance remains a relative concept. There are still millions of Americans in need of work and no guarantee that the U.S. recovery will continue. It is merely that the expected outlook for an otherwise lackluster U.S. economy looks better than almost everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps that's what makes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders reluctant to go all in. After years of false starts, they know that U.S. economic numbers can sour again and that when that happens the Fed can easily crank up the printing presses again.\par \par \uc2 Action, not words--that's what the market wants to see.\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 26, 2014 12:40 ET (16:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140326ea3q002w2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
