\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Bourses Seen Higher on China, Ukraine Hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 3,814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen higher, bunds and gilts mixed; euro, oil lower, spot gold up\par \par \uc2 -G-7 Agrees to Exclude Russia, Boost Sanctions\par \par \uc2 -Canada PM: G7 Eyeing Sanctions On Russia Energy Sector\par \par \uc2 -Russian Capital Flight Surges in First Quarter\par \par \uc2 -German Defense Minister Rejects Ukraine Joining NATO -Report\par \par \uc2 -SEC in New Probe of Securitized Deals -- Sources\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: French business sentiment; German Ifo index; U.K. inflation data, distributive trades; U.S. housing data, Treasury Secretary Lew testifies\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & U.S./ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Investors seem cautiously hopeful on Ukraine and China with European stock markets likely to open higher, and bunds and gilts mixed.\par \par \uc2 The heads of state of the world's largest advanced economies moved Monday to isolate Russia, severing a key link between Moscow and the Western world after nearly two decades.\par \par \uc2 The leaders of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2 , or G-7, nations effectively disbanded the larger G-8 by excluding Russia until it changes course in Ukraine. They also agreed to exert punitive sanctions on Russia's energy, banking, finance and arms industries unless Moscow begins to dial back plans to absorb the region of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 But the G-7 leaders, who met in emergency session, didn't specify how far they would go to isolate President Vladimir Putin and hammer Russia's economy.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets were mostly down Tuesday as traders booked profits after the previous day's rally, while Wall Street provided another negative lead in response to anaemic U.S. and Chinese manufacturing data. Traders hoped Beijing would soon announce monetary easing measures.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were nudging higher, with Treasurys little changed, as the euro and oil eased lower, while spot gold inched higher.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European stock markets are likely to start higher on hopes that China will introduce stimulus to counter the slowdown in its economy.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the opening Tuesday, City Index has called the FTSE up 27 at 6547, the DAX up 40 at 9229 and the CAC up 23 at 4299.\par \par \uc2 Economists at Reorient Financial Markets in Hong Kong, however, warned investors Tuesday against expecting an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Chinese government. "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new leaders have proved on several occasions that they will not be nervously diverted from their course of action by minor setbacks," the economists wrote.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were slightly higher Tuesday, after stocks declined as a selloff in the biotechnology sector persisted for a second straight session while investors also focused on negative economic data out of China and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Sellers continued to single out the once hot sector, which was among the top performers in last year's banner rally, as investors and analysts say concerns have mounted that many companies in the sector had become too expensive.\par \par \uc2 The catalyst behind the recent slide emerged Friday with a congressional complaint over the high price of a \uc2 Gilead Sciences\uc2 ' hepatitis C drug. Although the complaint singled out one company, investors and analysts say the decline across the sector reflects broader concerns about valuations across the sector. "Sentiment is waning the most we've seen in the last [two to three] years," analysts at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  wrote.\par \par \uc2 While U.S. companies sit on a mountain of cash, investors are getting weary of stock buybacks and instead are looking for CEOs to invest to boost sales or to buy competitors for growth and cutting costs, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 On the data front, investors will look for U.S. housing figures Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "New home sales have (been) rebounding since the soft patch in the third quarter of last year," Handelsbanken noted in a note to investors ahead of the U.S. data. "At present, new home sales are growing at a lower pace than residential investment as construction of apartment houses is increasing more than construction of single-family homes," it noted.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro nudged lower in Asia as investors continued to respond to weak data and geopolitical impact from the Ukraine situation.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of further sanctions could drag the euro down, said Stuart Ive, senior client advisor at OM Financial in Wellington. "Anything along those lines may see the euro dip down, mainly because central Europe receives the majority of its oil supplies from Russia and the euro zone has very strong economic links with Russia. Any sanctions will weigh on the euro."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, "The worse data gets in China, the closer you get to the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  responding by stimulating the economy," said Brian Daingerfield, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Securities. "A positive response from the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  might help the Aussie Dollar."\par \par \uc2 While investors also will watch Japanese share prices as a trading cue, some are somewhat worried about the G7-Russia situation. Therefore, "it's unlikely we will see traders will be in a fully risk-on mood and buy the dollar against the yen toward Y103," said a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds may start mixed Tuesday ahead another round of German Ifo surveys that are likely to confirm bullish business sentiment, even if down marginally on the February levels on some heightened Russia risk.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Deutsche Bundesbank\uc2 , in its latest assessment, grew strongly in the first quarter but the tempo could slow in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., February consumer price index was expected up 1.7% on the year versus up 1.9% in January. Falling inflation further below the Bank of England's 2% target will reinforce the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's message that interest rates will stay low for a while yet, notwithstanding a booming economy.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bond prices were little changed Tuesday, after markets bounced back Monday as some investors adjusted their positions on concerns over the Federal Reserve's next move on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The market recovered after some investors were seen stepping up buying longer-dated Treasury debt, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "There're a number of people, who were mispositioned along the curve last week betting on a more dovish Fed, trying to reverse their positions," said Thomas L. di Galoma, head of fixed income rates at ED&F Man Capital. "The perception is that the Fed will end its QE (quantitative easing) program soon and restructure the front-end of the Treasury market."\par \par \uc2 The latest round of buying was also partly spurred by the weakness in equity markets, as well as news headlines saying that Russian forces had stormed a Ukrainian navy ship in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, prices of government bonds were lower amid news that foreign investors have trimmed their JGB holdings.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures were lower in Asia with Nymex WTI futures extending prior losses. Brent crude also slipped from its previous close. "The disruption in supply that was expected due to the Russian situation, is not happening. Prices will rise if there are some strong sanctions against Russia," said Gnanasekhar Thiagarajan, director of Commtrendz Risk Management. The sanctions so far are insufficient to disrupt supplies, he said. "Overall, the trend is bearish. The U.S., which is one of the largest consumers, has huge stockpiles. Its shale inventories are also rising," he said.\par \par \uc2 Nymex WTI for May delivery was at $99.30/bbl, down 30 cents and May Brent crude was at $106.48/bbl, down 33 cents.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Gold edged higher in Asia on modest dip-buying after prices fell 1.9% in the spot market on Monday. While safe-haven appeal in gold has dimmed as tensions in Ukraine show signs of easing, expectations of higher interest rates in the U.S. have further dented gold's demand. The prospect of interest rates in the U.S. rising soon may have "dampened" the inflation disaster scenario for some gold bulls with regard to the after-effects of the U.S. ultra-easy money policy, Scott Schuberg, chief executive of Rivkin Securities Pty Ltd, said in a report. Gold will be "much better assessed using technical analysis than attempting to justify the effect of changing fundamental factors," he said.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,313.46/oz, up $4.70.\par \par \uc2 Base metals were likely to face pressure as investors could take a cautious view following downbeat economic data from China on Monday. Expectations of stimulus measures by the Chinese government helped to limit the downside.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month copper was little changed at $6,473.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 25 Mar 2014 01:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\par \par \uc2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\par \par \uc2 Australia's New Hope Sees Little Hope for Coal Price Rebound\par \par \uc2 China's Harbin Bank Prices US$1.1 Bln HK IPO Near Low End Of Price Range\par \par \uc2 Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Not Considering Rate Cuts -- Deputy Governor\par \par \uc2 SEC Is Probing Dealings by Banks and Companies in Loan Securities\par \par \uc2 Pension Plans Brace for a One-Two Punch\par \par \uc2 Huawei's Role in Internet Traffic Grows\par \par \uc2 Other Top Stories:\par \par \uc2 BOJ: Japan Firms' Cash Holdings Up 6.4% on Year at End-Dec\par \par \uc2 Japanese companies' cash holdings increased again over the final quarter of last year, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  data showed Tuesday, even as Prime Minister Shinzo Abe pushed firms to put their huge piles of money to more productive use.\par \par \uc2 Australian Prime Minister: Search to Resume as Soon as Possible\par \par \uc2 Australia's Prime Minister Tony Abbott said Tuesday that a search would resume "as soon as possible" for potential debris from \uc2 Malaysia Airlines\uc2  Flight 370 in the southern Indian Ocean, after poor weather grounded aircrews earlier Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Japan Govt Suga: Pledged up to Y150 Billion in Aid to Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Japan's chief government spokesman said Tuesday that Tokyo had pledged financial aid to Ukraine at the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  summit in The Hague.\par \par \uc2 Mexico, Panama Finish Free-Trade Talks\par \par \uc2 Mexico and Panama have finished negotiating a free-trade agreement, which the two governments expect to go into effect once it is approved by their respective legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Mexican Judge Orders House Arrest of Oceanografia CEO\par \par \uc2 Mexican authorities said Monday that a federal judge has ordered a 40-day house arrest against the chief executive of an oil-services firm at the center of an alleged multimillion-dollar fraud against the Mexican unit of \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Overhaul of NSA Data Collection Takes Shape\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the House Intelligence Committee are set to unveil Tuesday a proposal overhauling the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 's phone-records program by requiring searches to be conducted at phone companies instead of having the NSA compile the records.\par \par \uc2 S&P Downgrades Brazil Credit Rating, Citing Weak Growth\par \par \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  on Monday cut the credit rating on Brazil's long term bonds to one notch above junk, citing deteriorating government accounts and rising debt, the latest rebuke for a once-highflying economy now struggling to hold on to the investor enthusiasm it inspired just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 25 Mar 2014 01:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: Market Prices, Data Calendar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA    16276.69  -26.08 -0.16% \par Nasdaq   4226.39  -50.40 -1.18% \par S&P 500  1857.44   -9.08 -0.49% \par FTSE 100 6520.39  -36.78 -0.56% \par DAX      9188.77 -154.17 -1.65% \par CAC40    4276.34  -58.94 -1.36% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future   16213.00 +11.00 +0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 3612.50  +1.50  0.0% \par S&P Future    1851.10  +1.70 +0.1% \par As of 0430 GMT \par  \par Nikkei       14493.11 +17.81 +0.12% \par Hang Seng    21820.64 -25.81 -0.12% \par S&P/ASX 200   5332.60 -14.30 -0.27% \par MumbaiSensex 21997.99 -57.49 -0.26% \par Kospi         1944.62  -0.93 -0.05% \par ShanghaiComp  2075.25  +8.97 +0.43% \par Taipei        8670.30 +64.92 +0.75% \par As of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY 102.23-32 +0.03% \par Range   102.29 102.16 \par EUR/USD 1.3833-36 -0.04% \par Range   1.3839 1.3832 \par GBP/USD 1.6496-500 +0.00% \par Range   1.6502 1.6490 \par USD/CHF 0.8805-14 +0.02% \par Range   0.8811 0.8806 \par AUD/USD 0.9140-44 +0.10% \par Range   0.9157 0.9124 \par EUR/JPY 141.46-50 -0.01% \par Range   141.51 141.34 \par EUR/GBP 0.8385-87 -0.03% \par Range   0.8391 0.8384 \par EUR/CHF 1.2184-88 -0.03% \par Range   1.2192 1.2182 \par As of 0350 GMT vs. NY close \par  \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 7.500%/7.900% \par Day Earlier: 7.500%/8.000% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract 6.000%/6.300% \par Day Ea