\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Fall on Wall Street Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 03:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Asian stocks traded broadly lower early Tuesday, keeping step with losses on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 fell 0.3% to 14370.17, also dented by a firmer yen. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last trading around Yen102.19 versus Yen102.25 late Monday in New York. South Korea's Kospi fell 0.4% to 1937.63 while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 fell 0.6% to 5314.50.\par \par \uc2 Stocks on Wall Street slumped overnight amid a selloff in biotechnology shares, hitting the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite the hardest. The Nasdaq fell 1.2% to 4226.39 while the S&P 500 declined 0.5% to 1857.44.\par \par \uc2 Closer to home, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  data released Tuesday showed Prime Minster Shinzo Abe is having trouble getting Japanese corporations to put their large cash piles to work in the economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and deposits held by private-sector companies excluding financial firms rose 6.4% year-over-year to Yen222 trillion ($2.17 trillion) as of the end of December, following a 5.8% rise in the previous three month period, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Investors in the region will be closely watching Chinese stocks when they begin trading later Tuesday as expectations rise for China to introduce new measures to tackle slowing growth. Hong Kong's Hang Seng China Enterprises Index rallied 2.8% Monday despite worse-than-expected Chinese manufacturing data. Stocks tied to the country's so-called "old economy"--such as oil and bank stocks--were the strongest gainers.\par \par \uc2 Economists at Reorient Financial Markets in Hong Kong however warned investors Tuesday against expecting an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Chinese government. "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new leaders have proved on several occasions that they will not be nervously diverted from their course of action by minor setbacks," the economists wrote.\par \par \uc2 Write to Mia Lamar at mia.lamar@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 24, 2014 22:45 ET (02:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140325ea3p000eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
