\par\par \b \uc2 UK Morning Briefing: FTSE Seen Higher On China Stimulus Hope\b0\par\par\uc2 1,151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Snapshot: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FTSE seen higher; sterling little changed; gilts seen mixed; spot gold higher, oil lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 -G-7 Agrees to Exclude Russia, Boost Sanctions\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Siemens\uc2 , \uc2 Associated British Ports\uc2  in Wind Turbine Investment\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Anglo American\uc2  Halts \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Los Bronces Copper Mine, Cites Actions of Protesters -Reuters\par \par \uc2 Watch for: house prices, inflation \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Shares: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London markets were likely to start higher on hopes that China will introduce stimulus to counter the slowdown in its economy. Ahead of the opening Tuesday, City Index has called the FTSE up 27 at 6547.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists at Reorient Financial Markets in Hong Kong, however, warned investors Tuesday against expecting an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Chinese government. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's new leaders have proved on several occasions that they will not be nervously diverted from their course of action by minor setbacks," the economists wrote.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the Ukraine situation, the leaders of the G-7 nations effectively disbanded the larger G-8 by excluding Russia until it changes course in Ukraine. They also agreed to exert punitive sanctions on Russia's energy, banking, finance and arms industries unless Moscow begins to dial back plans to absorb the region of Crimea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the G-7 leaders, who met in emergency session, didn't specify how far they would go to isolate President Vladimir Putin and hammer Russia's economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Locally, "all eyes remain fixed on CPI with the release of core CPI in the U.K.," noted IG strategist Evan Lucas. "With Mark Carney categorically turning the rate cut switch off, signs of inflation will see the GBP higher against most major pairs." \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling was little changed in Asia and was likely to hold up well even if inflation declines further, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank said the markets were unlikely to roll back Bank of England rate-hike expectations in response to the expected soft CPI data. BNP noted that long-sterling positioning has been substantially reduced. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  pegged risks for sterling to the upside heading into the second quarter this year. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Gilts: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gilts may start mixed ahead of more inflation data. February consumer price index was expected up 1.7% on the year versus up 1.9% in January. Falling inflation further below the BOE's 2% target will reinforce the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's message that interest rates will stay low for a while yet, notwithstanding a booming economy. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Energy: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures were lower in Asia with Nymex WTI futures extending prior losses. Brent crude also slipped from its previous close. "The disruption in supply that was expected due to the Russian situation, is not happening. Prices will rise if there are some strong sanctions against Russia," said Gnanasekhar Thiagarajan, director of Commtrendz Risk Management. The sanctions so far are insufficient to disrupt supplies, he said. "Overall, the trend is bearish. U.S. which is one of the largest consumers, has huge stockpiles. Its shale inventories are also rising," he said. Nymex WTI for May delivery was at $99.30/bbl, down 30 cents and May Brent crude was at $106.48/bbl, down 33 cents. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Metals: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold edged higher in Asia on modest dip-buying after prices fell 1.9% in the spot market on Monday. While safe-haven appeal in gold has dimmed as tensions in Ukraine show signs of easing, expectations of higher interest rates in the U.S. have further dented gold's demand. The prospect of interest rates in the U.S. rising soon may have "dampened" the inflation disaster scenario for some gold bulls with regard to the after-effects of the U.S. ultra-easy money policy, Scott Schuberg, chief executive of Rivkin Securities Pty Ltd, said in a report. Gold will be "much better assessed using technical analysis than attempting to justify the effect of changing fundamental factors," he said. Spot gold was at $1,313.46/oz, up $4.70.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals were likely to face pressure as investors could take a cautious view following downbeat economic data from China on Monday. Expectations of stimulus measures by the Chinese government helped to limit the downside. LME 3-month copper was little changed at $6,473. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Prices: \par DJIA      16276.69 -26.08 -0.16% \par Nasdaq     4226.39 -50.40 -1.18% \par S&P 500     1857.44 -9.08 -0.49% \par FTSE 100   6520.39 -36.78 -0.56% \par FTSE 250 16019.41 -140.70 -0.87% \par FTSE AIM \par All-Share   845.66 -10.67 -1.25% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future   16213.00 +11.00 +0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 3612.50 +1.50   0.0% \par S&P Future    1851.10 +1.70  +0.1% \par As of 0430 GMT \par  \par Nikkei     14493.11 +17.81 +0.12% \par S&P/ASX 200 5332.60 -14.30 -0.27% \par ShanghaiComp 2075.25 +8.97 +0.43% \par As of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY  102.23-32 +0.03% \par Range 102.29 102.16 \par EUR/USD  1.3833-36 -0.04% \par Range 1.3839 1.3832 \par GBP/USD 1.6496-500 +0.00% \par 1.6502 1.649