\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: A Slowing China Hopes For Stimulus -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 12:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey, Alen Mattich and Michael Arnold\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: Russia's annexation of Crimea has advanced with its takeover of Ukrainian military bases. Meanwhile, purchasing managers' surveys were released for China and the major euro-zone economies. China's reports confirmed recent signs of contraction while the European reports signaled that expansion remains under way.\par \par \uc2 Investors seem to be shrugging off Russia's takeover of Crimea. European equity markets opened moderately weaker, possibly taking a signal from the U.S. market's late decline on Friday, notwithstanding purchasing managers' surveys signaling continued recovery across the euro zone. Asian markets, meanwhile, seemed to take a weak Chinese PMI reading to suggest the Beijing's authorities will make moves to stimulate the economy back to growth. (AM)\par \par \uc2 CRIMEA: Russian troops took control of a Ukrainian naval base in Crimea, the third attack on Ukrainian bases in the province during the past two days.\par \par \uc2 Russia continues to consolidate its control of Crimea following its decision to annex the territory. Some pundits have speculated that Crimea will be the first stage in a Russian attempt to consolidate control of the whole northern Black Sea coast. So far, the West has limited itself to largely symbolic measures in support of Ukraine's territorial rights. What's not clear is how far the Western governments would allow Russian expansionism to go or what they might be able to do to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (AM)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The March \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash purchasing managers' survey PMI fell to an eight-month low of 48.1 in March from 48.5 in February.\par \par \uc2 Any figure below 50 indicates that activity is contracting, and the latest \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI survey adds to mounting evidence China's economy is slowing to start the year. The March data was seen as particularly important because it's free of distortions from the long Lunar New Year holiday that make January-February data suspect. The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  survey is generally a few points lower than the official China PMI, which remained --only just --above the boom/bust line of 50 last month. The weakness in China's economy is leading to speculation that authorities will step in with stimulus spending or cuts in reserve requirement ratios, in other words a loosening of monetary policy to get the economy going again --as it's done routinely during recent years. Some think stimulus measures have already been started. The weakness will test the leadership's seriousness about rebalancing China's economy, rather than pursuing high growth at all costs. (MA)\par \par \uc2 THAILAND: February industrial confidence dropped to 85.7 in Thailand from 86.9, a 56-month low and below the neutral reading of 100.\par \par \uc2 The Federation of Thai Industries\uc2  sentiment survey made for gloomy reading. Separate data out Monday showed Thai auto production fell Thailand's 24.3% in February from a year earlier, though it rose 6.67% on-month. The ongoing political crisis is devastating Thailand's economy, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again lowering its growth forecast for the year and cutting interest rates earlier this month, even as its peers elsewhere in Asia are preparing to tighten policy. (MA)\par \par \uc2 FRANCE: March preliminary "flash" purchasing managers' surveys: services was 51.4 vs. forecast of 47.5; manufacturing was 51.9 vs. forecast of 49.6.\par \par \uc2 French preliminary purchasing managers' surveys registered surprising gains in both manufacturing and services. PMIs above 50 imply growth, so that's a ray of sunshine in what's been a gloomy economy. The manufacturing component registered growth for the first time since July 2011. (AM)\par \par \uc2 GERMANY: March preliminary "flash" purchasing managers' surveys: services was 54.0 vs. forecast of 55.5; manufacturing was 53.8 vs. forecast of 54.5.\par \par \uc2 German purchasing managers where marginally less ebullient than expected, perhaps unsurprisingly given the uncertainty thrown up by the Crimean crisis. The \uc2 German government\uc2  has taken a strong stance against Russia but the economy is dependent on Russian gas supplies. (AM)\par \par \uc2 EURO ZONE: March preliminary "flash" purchasing managers' surveys: composite was 53.2 vs. 53.3 forecast; manufacturing was 53.0 vs. 52.9 forecast; services was 52.4 vs. 52.6 forecast.\par \par \uc2 The euro zone's preliminary PMIs were positive and almost exactly in line with market expectations, notwithstanding the Crimean crisis and central Europe's reliance on Russian fuel supplies, the survey results suggest solid growth. One source of concern, however, is that both input and output prices signal deflationary pressures. (AM)\par \par \uc2 Coming Up:\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 9 a.m. EDT. Federal Reserve Governor Jeremy Stein speaks at crowdfunding conference\par \par \uc2 After the stir caused by Fed Chairman Janet Yellen's comments last week, which led investors to bring forward their projections for when the Fed will start to raise interest rates, there will be added attention on members of the Federal Open Market Committee, including Mr Stein, in the hope of clarification. They might be disappointed with this event, however: a crowdfunding-themed conference may not be the right setting for this Fed governor to get into the nuances of monetary policy. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 9 a.m. EDT. Markit Economics preliminary "flash" manufacturing PMI. [Final February PMI was 57.1.]\par \par \uc2 This indicator very much plays second fiddle to the Institute of Supply Management's monthly gauge of manufacturing activity. But because it has shown itself to be fairly much in line with ISM's trends -- last month, like ISM, it showed a healthy increase from January's readout -- the flash indicator's capacity to give investors an early read on how the manufacturing sector as a whole is doing could start to make it a useful indicator. (MC)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL: 9:30 a.m. EDT. (10:30 a.m., Sao Paulo.) February Balance of Payments. [Previous current account was deficit -$11.59 billion, or 3.67% of GDP.]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's current account deficit hit a record monthly high last month, suggesting that the weakening in the real over the past year has not done enough to improve the country's external imbalances. So long as the deficit is above 3%, it will be viewed as a vulnerability for Brazil because it forewarns of future challenges paying its foreign debts. And with the real having strengthened in February, which makes its exports more expensive and so tends to put further pressure on the trade balances, the prospects for a narrowing in the deficit last month aren't great. (MC) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 24, 2014 07:57 ET (11:57 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140324ea3o0019l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
