\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Falls Farther, but Ride May Be Nearly Over\b0\par\par\uc2 902 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 10:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan fell to its lowest level against the U.S. dollar in more than a year, as signs of tighter U.S. monetary policy benefited Beijing's recent campaign to deter speculative capital inflows by weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Growing pessimism about the country's slowing economy and fresh evidence of distress in its property sector and entire financial system have compounded the effects of the government campaign. But with hot-money inflows showing signs of slowing, analysts say the authorities--whose overarching emphasis is always financial stability--are unlikely to let the yuan fall much farther in the near term.\par \par \uc2 It has fallen every day since China widened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily trading range over the weekend, losing 1.3% of its value against the dollar--increasing the loss for 2014 to 2.8%, nearly wiping off the entire 2013 gain of 2.9%. In Shanghai trading Thursday morning, the yuan fell to an intraday low of 6.2334 to the dollar--its lowest level since late February 2013.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has powerful weapons to dictate the yuan's day-to-day movements, analysts say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness is largely a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to guide it lower using the so-called central parity, the reference rate it sets daily. The yuan is allowed to move up or down from this setting by 2%, a trading band the PBOC doubled from 1% over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC set the central parity at 6.1460 yuan to the dollar Thursday, much higher than Wednesday's 6.1351. The higher the setting, the weaker the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "The PBOC would be happy to introduce uncertainty about the yuan's direction so as to deter speculative capital inflows. They want to make sure it's no longer a one-way bet," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, a senior economist and strategist at \uc2 Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The PBOC's effort in recent weeks to use a weaker yuan to squeeze out speculators betting on its rise already shows signs of success. Net foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial institutions, a proxy for inflows of foreign capital, fell sharply to a 128.25 billion yuan ($20.75 billion) in February, from 437.37 billion yuan in January.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say Beijing's yuan weakening is meant also to spur the world's second-largest economy by boosting sagging exports. Weaker-than-expected indicators in recent weeks--from industrial production to fixed-asset investment to foreign trade--have led major investment banks to cut projections for this year's growth in China's gross domestic product. Bank of AmericaMerrill Lynch's outlook is now to 7.2%, down from 7.6%; \uc2 UBS\uc2 's is 7.5%, down from 7.8%.\par \par \uc2 Cracks are also showing up in China's creaky financial system and debt-ladden corporate sector. A small property developer on the east coast said earlier this week that it is unable to repay almost $400 million worth of bank loans, news that followed the first default on a domestically issued bond by a Chinese company, a solar components maker in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "The drumbeat of negative news is certainly weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it has already depreciated a lot. I think further downside is very limited," said Mr. Kowalczyk, adding that Beijing's latest policy rhetoric suggests the authorities may step up government spending to spur growth.\par \par \uc2 After a routine policy meeting, China's cabinet released a statement Wednesday stressing the need to keep economic growth within the "reasonable range" and "quickly roll out" measures to increase domestic consumption and stabilize growth.\par \par \uc2 "In the long run, the yuan is still likely to appreciate because China still runs a large trade surplus," said Mr. Kowalczyk, adding that he expects the yuan to rise to 6.0000 against the dollar by the year-end.\par \par \uc2 Nomura economist Zhiwei Zhang said the government's remarks reflect "increasing concerns over growth among the leaders." That, he added, reinforces expectations of more policy easing in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 While the widening of the yuan's trading band and increased volatility in the market have triggered hopes for a much more liberal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from the PBOC, some analysts think the recent bout of yuan weakening will likely be transitory.\par \par \uc2 "The PBOC's recent relaxed grip on the fixing has made people excited, but once things go to the extreme--say, if the yuan falls by its daily limit continuously--I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will definitely step in to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Liu Dongliang, senior analyst at \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Merchants Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the top of the weekend statement announcing the widening of the trading band, Mr. Liu noted, the PBOC stuck with its long-standing definition of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime as a "managed floating exchange-rate system."\par \par \uc2 "The interesting thing about Chinese policy statements is that you really need to pay attention to the first few sentences," he said\par \par \uc2 Offering a similar view, Mr. Kowalczyk said it will likely take at least two years for the PBOC to substantially relax its control on the reference exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Shen Hong and Wynne Wang\par \par \uc2 Write to Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com and Wynne Wang at wynne.wang@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 20, 2014 05:24 ET (09:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140320ea3k000rm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
