\par\par \b \uc2 Fed's Isolationism Means Calm Markets Mask Deeper Global Problems -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 18:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 If Janet Yellen has glanced at bond yields or stock indexes during the Federal Open Market Committee meeting over the past two days, the new Fed Chairwoman has likely been satisfied.\par \par \uc2 But the calm in markets before today's 2 p.m. FOMC announcement and Mrs. Yellen's inaugural press conference is disguising a bigger problem. Some unsettling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions are poised to return to the international economy and that's in part due to the myopic, isolationist policies of the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 China is devaluing the yuan. It is now down 2.5% from its January peak versus the dollar and at its lowest level in 11 months. Even before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decline, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had added some $250 billion in foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in 2013. Those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  inherently hurt emerging markets, whose exporters compete with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by making their products comparatively more expensive in dollars than Chinese products. So, with the finances of emerging markets stabilizing after the turn-of-the- year's minicrisis, many will follow this lead, weakening their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and accumulating dollars.\par \par \uc2 Expect those whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stubbornly held up during the recent episodes to move first: South Korea, Mexico, Indonesia and Poland, for example. But others will follow. It all points to a cycle of competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation and a perpetuation of artificially low yields on \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds, the assets into which these countries' newly acquired greenbacks must flow.\par \par \uc2 Why do we care? Because this entrenches a codependent relationship between savings-rich, export-oriented developing nations and a borrowing-dependent, consumer-driven America. It is the same relationship that created the conditions behind the 2008 blowup in the world financial system.\par \par \uc2 We probably won't see a similar crisis. China's trade surplus has shrunk and America's exports have grown, which played out in Wednesday's news of a shrinking current-account deficit. Meanwhile, U.S. household debt has eased and more tightly regulated banks can't as easily turn rivers of cheap foreign credit into a crazy mortgage-derivatives machine. But having the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  soak up most of these foreign central banks' ever-growing rainy-day funds is unhealthy. It distorts the ability of financial markets to price capital and it puts off the return of a market-driven economy in which investment risks are fairly priced.\par \par \uc2 I am not talking about a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war per se--at least not according to the definition of the 1930s, when countries' competitive depreciations led to war, first a trade war then a more violent form of combat. But when each country is pursuing its own competitive exchange-rate policies, it creates friction and perpetuates bad, market-distorting policies. It also means that multilateral institutions such as the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  are sidelined in the face of an unpredictable free-for-all in domestic policy making.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is king in this world. That isn't because everyone loves it, but because the reserve-accumulating central banks are starved of safe-haven alternatives. So, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  gets to keep borrowing cheaply without an incentive to improve its fiscal management. And because foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buyers are holding down long-end Treasury yields while the Fed anchors short-term rates at zero, we end up with a "flat yield curve" in bond markets. Medium-term, that is good news for stocks, because their returns look attractive in relative terms and it helps big companies that use capital markets to raise money. But it creates few incentives for U.S. banks to borrow short and lend long, which means smaller and midsized firms can't easily tap their traditional source of credit. The end result is less investment and less hiring--a perpetuation of the U.S. economy's six-year dilemma, in other words.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials tend to blame all this on foreign central banks. If only they would let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate, we'd all be OK, they say. By their reckoning, the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and heavy bond purchases by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and other central banks are a bigger sin than the Fed's "quantitative easing" bond-buying, which are ostensibly focused entirely on domestic conditions and exchange-rate neutral.\par \par \uc2 But this view ignores the reality that the Fed is unquestionably the world's lender of last resort. When the FOMC made not even mention at its last January meeting of the turmoil in emerging markets, it reinforced the perspective of those countries' policy makers that they are on their own. As such, they pursue the only insurance policy they know: buying dollars.\par \par \uc2 That might help give U.S. markets a feel-good buzz. But it isn't good for global stability in the long run.\par \par \uc2 -- Follow Michael J. Casey on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @mikejcasey. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field