\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-WRAPUP 2-China's yuan dips in widened band, but scope for big swings seen limited\b0\par\par\uc2 991 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Weakness expected to persist in near term\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to end one-way bets on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation\par \par \uc2 * Sharp decline unlikely given sizeable surplus, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves\par \par \uc2 * Cbank likely to keep tight grip on mkt until nerves settle\par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI/HONG KONG, March 17 (Reuters) - China's yuan eased against the dollar on Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doubled the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily trading band as part of its commitment to let markets play a greater role in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Yet the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved in a relatively narrow range reflecting market views that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  will seek to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings at a time when markets fret over China's cooling growth and the quality of corporate debt.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , with the help of major state-owned banks, will for certain tighten the grip on yuan's value in coming days and weeks to prevent what it sees excessive volatility," said a dealer at a European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 In the longer run, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move in a broader range in a sign of its confidence that it can keep speculators at bay and that the economy was mature enough to handle greater uncertainty about the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "Over time, the widening will pave the way for the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to gradually lessen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in daily trading and will help \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reforms to make the yuan fully convertible eventually."\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  doubled the yuan's daily trading range, so that it can now rise or fall 2 percent around the daily mid-point rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at 6.15 to the dollar, just 0.29 percent to the weaker side of the official mid-point rate. It briefly fell to an intraday low of 6.1642, 0.2 percent weaker than Friday's close.\par \par \uc2 Since the start of this year the yuan has lost 1.8 percent against the dollar, largely as a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts, reversing much of last year's near 3 percent rise as Beijing sought to change the perception the yuan was a safe one-way appreciation bet.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's efforts to clamp down on such trades combined with concerns over China's economic health are expected to keep the yuan on the back foot in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 LITTLE UPSIDE\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, a Chinese company became the first to default on a corporate bond, and concerns about economic growth were highlighted by a dramatic 18 percent fall in exports in February and sluggish manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 "Given China's recent relatively weak export performance, we see little upside for the yuan this coming year," said Tao Wang, an economist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of International Settlements\uc2  data underscore that point showing the yuan at a record high against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China's trading partners adjusted for inflation.\par \par \uc2 Yuan non-deliverable forwards, that reflect bets on future \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  performance, are implying a nearly 1 percent drop over the next 12 months compared with half a percent fall in December.\par \par \uc2 But China will be careful to temper expectations of more yuan weakness to prevent capital outflows at a time when the economy is facing headwinds.\par \par \uc2 A series of weak economic surveys made some economists predict that Beijing will struggle to meet its 7.5 percent growth target this year, which it itself would be already the weakest in 24 years.\par \par \uc2 China's new leadership has signalled greater tolerance for more pedestrian growth as the necessary price for its intended rebalancing away from rapid investment- and export-driven expansion towards more sustained development.\par \par \uc2 Giving markets more leeway in setting the exchange rate is part of a broad reform plan that also includes freeing up interest rates, allowing private investment in protected sectors and cutting red tape. But Beijing has also made clear it was ready to act if the economy lost traction too quickly to preserve economic and social stability.\par \par \uc2 RENMINBI TO RISE\par \par \uc2 However, in the longer run, the yuan could still find support in China's sizeable current account surplus and its massive $3.8 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve war chest.\par \par \uc2 That also chimes in with Beijing's efforts to boost the yuan's use in international trade. In recent months, it has opened new offshore yuan hubs in London, Singapore and Taiwan and relaxed restrictions for investors seeking yuan assets.\par \par \uc2 More than a sixth of China's trade is now denominated in the yuan compared to less than 1 percent four years ago and analysts expect this share to double over the next two to three years.\par \par \uc2 A band widening is also seen as a precursor to a fully floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which would be a big step towards the yuan, also referred to as renminbi, becoming an international reserve asset.\par \par \uc2 "Any short term weakness that may occur would provide investors a good opportunity to reallocate away from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of more indebted countries in favour of the Renminbi," said Andy Seaman, fund manager at London-based fixed income fund Stratton Street Capital.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mid-point level fixed 0.04 percent firmer than Friday's level and Monday's opening trades suggested it was Beijing's intention to keep markets guessing about where yuan would be heading.\par \par \uc2 "These rates are the first signs that the band widening may not point to any certain direction of yuan depreciation or appreciation," said a dealer at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting that heightened uncertainty, one-month implied dollar/yuan volatilities traded in the offshore market, perhaps the clearest indicator of how volatile the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to be over the next month, hit a record before easing slightly.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-20T09:05:22.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140317ea3h0040i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
