\par\par \b \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Pulls the Trigger on Yuan Band Widening -- WSJ Blog-2-\b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 04:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled the trigger on something the market already knew it was aiming at -- widening the trading band for the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  said it would allow the yuan to move 2% above or below a rate it sets every day for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar, up from 1% now, in a move that will mean the yuan will behave a little more like major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The policy move, which takes effect Monday, still lets the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  keep its thumb on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  scale, knowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't move more than 2% away from where it wants it on any given day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely seen as preparing the market for just such a move when it let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slip repeatedly last month. Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to surprise more than a few people with the timing of its announcement -- just before dinner hour on Saturday night. One analyst referred to this as "\uc2 PBOC\uc2  style" with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing that it still could shake things up just a little when it wanted.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , which last expanded the band to 1% from 0.5% in April of 2012, said that this was part of its effort to make the exchange rate more market-based, and that it would continue to push ahead with this effort. It also said this did not mean either a big depreciation -- or appreciation for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- in the future and it added that Chinese companies were already better prepared to deal with somewhat greater yuan fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 It also said it would withdraw from routine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Analysts generally applauded the move -- which had been already been touched on in Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  's work report delivered at the opening of the annual session of parliament this month.\par \par \uc2 Following are some of comments from analysts (edited for style and length):\par \par \uc2 -----\par \par \uc2 "This was a good time to widen the trading band. It came at a time when there was a rather large division in the views on the direction of the yuan and came against a background of slowing capital inflows," wrote Bank of Communications economists in a note to clients. "This can effectively avoid a big appreciation or depreciation of the yuan's exchange rate immediately following the move."\par \par \uc2 They added that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably push ahead with the introduction of new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related products, relax some restrictions on fund movements under the capital account and it might raise its ceiling on domestic bank deposit interest rates this year.\par \par \uc2 (Editor's explanatory note: Domestic bank deposit rates are currently capped at 1.1 times a benchmark rate set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Liberalized interest rates and relaxed capital account restrictions are seen as key parts of a package of inter-related financial reforms).\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 "Today's band widening strengthens the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's signal that the one-way bet on the yuan is over and we should expect more yuan/dollar volatility going forward," \uc2 Lu Ting\uc2 , economist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  won' stop here, but further band widening is of little meaning. A much more important and meaningful reform is to change the rule on setting the daily fixing of yuan/dollar. As an intermediate step, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  could peg the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighted by the importance of (\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's) trading partners."\par \par \uc2 ------\par \par \uc2 "If the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  really withdraws from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in the near term there would be more volatility," said Ma Xiaoping, economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "They will probably withdraw gradually."\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has been signaling that it was moving in this direction. In the future there will be a more flexible exchange rate based on market supply and demand."\par \par \uc2 -----\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to spur businesses and financial institutions to prepared for a more flexible exchange rate," said Louis Kuijs of RBS.\par \par \uc2 "They want to let this volatility happen so people will up their game. They want volatility but it's going to be a bit contrived, generated by policy not by the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 "It might not lead to big changes on a day-to-day level, but it signals they are serious about moving toward more flexibility."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 "The move was as we anticipated though the margin of the band widening was less than we had expected," said Lu Zhengwei of Industrial Bank in a comment on his \uc2 Sina Weibo\uc2  account. "We had expected 2.5%-3.0% and this shows that the authorities are being cautious."\par \par \uc2 The economist also said: "This also shows the yuan's recent depreciation is over and we will now see two-way movement in the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 --Liyan Qi, Richard Silk and William Kazer\par \par \uc2 More at The Wall Street Journal's China Real Time Report blog, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime/}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 17-03-14 0323GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140317ea3h0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
