\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia Rebounds on Poor China Exports -- Asia Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 1,861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 01:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.1030-6.1300   6.1000-6.1331 \par USD/TWD   30.270-30.420   30.210-30.500 \par USD/KRW    1,064-1,068     1,060-1,070 \par USD/SGD   1.2647-1.2677   1.2617-1.2700 \par USD/MYR   3.2670-3.2880   3.2570-3.3000 \par USD/THB    32.23-32.44     32.20-32.47 \par USD/PHP    44.53-44.84     44.46-45.00 \par USD/IDR   11,360-11,500   11,200-11,750 \par USD/INR    61.00-61.27     60.83-61.50 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY--consolidation higher. USD/CNY may rise slightly due to mild risk aversion triggered by the weak exports growth data published on Saturday, and also due to possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan below 6.1000 to the U.S. unit. Chinese exports fell 18.1% from a year ago, far below expectations for a 5.0% increase; distortions due to the Lunar New Year holidays affecting exports volume. On the other hand, imports rose significantly to push China into its biggest trade deficit since Feb. 2012 - a sign that China's shift toward consumption-driven economic growth may be taking root. \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot USD/CNY market late last week as the round-figure barrier at 6.1000 approached. The suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the PBOC spooked USD/CNY much higher to close at 6.1285 from its trough of 6.1000. Expectations of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could keep spot USD/CNY buoyant this week. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/CNY is at 6.1030 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1000 (psychological support), before 6.0800 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 6.1300 (psychological resistance), then at 6.1331 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 6.1500 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD--consolidation. USD/TWD is likely to remain locked in a narrow range of 30.270-30.420 as the daily chart shows a lack of momentum for the Taiwan dollar. The upbeat U.S. nonfarm payrolls on Friday has given the benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury yield a boost, which could add to dollar bullishness and thereby push USD/TWD toward the top of the suggested trading range. If USD/TWD ends the day above 30.420 and thereby inside the Bollinger uptrend channel, the bullish chart indicator suggests the dollar could soon rise toward 30.570 where the top of the channel caps. Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the Taiwan dollar from gaining too much is likely one of the main reasons for the Taiwan dollar's stability. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 30.270 (20-day Bollinger mid support), then at 30.210 (daily Ichimoku Cloud support), before 30.000 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.420 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 30.500 (psychological resistance), before 30.570 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW--consolidation with downside bias. USD/KRW has risen out of the Bollinger downtrend channel for now, but remains well-capped by the Ichimoku Cloud resistance at 1,068 which ought to keep dollar strength in check. The U.S. dollar is slightly stronger this week after an upbeat U.S. nonfarm payrolls report that pushed benchmark U.S. bond yields higher on Friday. USD/KRW is hence trading with a supportive tone and may linger within its currently elevated range of 1,064-1,068. The weak start for South Korean stocks, which are down 0.5% possibly due to weak China exports data over the weekend, may weigh on the won. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,064 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,060 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,056 (weekly Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,068 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone and 20-day Bollinger mid resistance), then at 1,070 (psychological resistance), before 1,072 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD--consolidation. USD/SGD has rebounded back into daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone and could linger within a range of 1.2647-1.2733 for now. The U.S. dollar jumped late Friday after an upbeat U.S. nonfarm payrolls report that pushed benchmark U.S. bond yields higher. Weaker-than-expected China exports data on Saturday could have hurt global investor sentiment and thereby led to broad U.S. dollar-buying. Unless USD/SGD breaks the 1.2647-1.2733 Ichimoku Cloud range, momentum for a sustained move in either direction may be lacking. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2647 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone), then at 1.2617 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1.2600 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2677 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2700 (psychological resistance), before 1.2733 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR--consolidation higher. USD/MYR is rising on mild risk aversion triggered by weak China exports and an upbeat U.S. jobs report which increased demand for the U.S. dollar. USD/MYR has broken out of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel and may be headed for the 20-day Bollinger mid line at 3.2880. Unless USD/MYR manages to close Monday below 3.2670, the likelihood of ringgit strength in the near term will be reduced. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2670 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 3.2570 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud support), before 3.2480 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 3.2880 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 3.3000 (psychological resistance), before 3.3110 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB--downside bias. Despite a broad turnaround higher for the U.S. dollar, USD/THB will retain its bearish technical bias as long as it remains under 32.44 and thereby below the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone. The U.S. dollar is slightly stronger this week after an upbeat U.S. nonfarm payrolls report that pushed benchmark U.S. bond yields higher on Friday. Coupled with weak Chinese exports data on Saturday, a mild risk aversion in Asia is weakening the baht slightly. International investors appear mostly unfazed by the political mess in Thailand, believing that the country's fundamentals are still robust and that the politics will sort itself out in the medium term as long as the Thai army stays out of the fray. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.23 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 32.20 (psychological support), before 32.00 (major psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.44 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance), then at 32.47 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 32.50 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP--consolidation higher. USD/PHP may rise back into the daily Ichimoku Cloud support which - was capping the dollar at 44.46 - as a mild risk aversion descends over Asia. The much poorer-than-expected Chinese exports data on Saturday is likely to depress investor sentiment for Asian assets. Upbeat U.S. nonfarm payrolls on Friday pushed the benchmark \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields higher - which pulled the greenback along as well. USD/PHP could hence re-enter the stalemate between 44.53-44.84, caught between the entrance to the Bollinger downtrend channel and the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance. Looking ahead, the Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep interest rates low at its March policy meeting as inflation in the Philippines tapers off, rising just 4.1% on year in February compared with 4.2% in January. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.53 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 44.46 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud), before 44.00 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.84 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance), then at 45.00 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR--consolidation. While the rupiah is still a favored Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, some weakness may be in store in the early part of this week due to disappointing Chinese exports data on Saturday and the possibility of further tapering of U.S. quantitative easing after strong jobs data on Friday. USD/IDR may hover between 11,360-11,500 for now as the dollar makes a mild rebound across Asia. The rupiah continues to lead the way for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gaining as much as 6.1% this year, helped by persistent inflows from foreign investors fueling demand for high-yielding rupiah-denominated assets. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 11,360 (4-month low), then at 11,200 (psychological support), before 11,000 (round-figure trading barrier). Immediate resistance is at 11,500 (psychological resistance), then at 11,750 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 11,800 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR--downside bias. USD/INR could pullback slightly from its recent sharp break lower as signs of risk aversion appear. The dollar is broadly higher in Asia after weak Chinese export data over the weekend and upbeat U.S. jobs data that refreshed the possibility of further reduction in U.S. Federal Reserve bond-buying. USD/INR may consolidate for now between 61.00-61.67 as investors take a step back from recent heady gains for the rupee. The rupee has rallied due to an improving current account deficit and the announcement of general elections to be held April 7. As election fever heats up, expectations are high for the popular opposition party - Bharatiya Janata Party - that touts itself as anti-corruption and is more business-friendly - to win. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 61.00 (psychological support), then at 60.83 (December low), before 60.20 (base of weekly Ichimoku Cloud support and weekly Bollinger mid line). Immediate resistance is likely at 61.27 (daily lower Bollinger band standard deviation 2), then at 61.50 (psychological\par \par \uc2 9 Mar 2014 20:58 EDT  USD/Asia Rebounds on Poor China Exports -- Asia -2-\par \par \uc2 resistance), before 61.67 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 09, 2014 20:58 ET (00:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN