\par\par \b \uc2 China's Shift on Yuan Exchange Fixes One Problem but Creates New Ones -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 02:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 For all the excitement over China's moves to weaken the yuan and open it up to market forces, it's worth noting that the slide since Jan. 1 that took it to its weakest level in five months against the dollar on Wednesday was worth all of 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 Compare that with drops of 2.8% for the Turkish lira, 8.6% for the Russian ruble and 6.3% for the Chilean peso, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a country from which China buys a lot of copper.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's decline is hardly drastic stuff. In fact, it's reminder of how gradual the government's approach to market liberalization will be. And it's still an open question whether it can continue to manage that process without causing a significant financial disruption.\par \par \uc2 Despite the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deliberate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which were first reported Wednesday in The Wall Street Journal, the yuan is still the best performing emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year, according to analysis by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  showing that it was up 8.3% on a trade-and inflation-adjusted basis in the 12 months to January 31. Pushed out to a two-and-a-half-year period, this measure of the yuan's trade-weighted real exchange rate was up 20%. Trade-weighted real exchange rates measure a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's valuation against a basket of various trading partners' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that's weighted according to the size of the trade relationship and also incorporate the effect that inflation in making a country's exports more expensive.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is that while the global economy has slowed and China's main emerging-market competitors' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have weakened, its producers have had to sell their products overseas at considerably higher prices. That's hardly something that's in this export-dependent country's best interests. China is trying to migrate from a less export-dependent model to a consumer-led one. But it can't afford to hit its export sector too hard in the process, lest that exacerbate the risk of bankruptcies and job losses.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that trade interests have not been the main driver of the exchange rate. Blame that on capital flows, led by speculators. Indeed, one of the most striking elements of the PBOC's move to curtail the yuan's appreciation -- made in apparent preparation for widening the trading band within which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely against the dollar -- is that it implicitly acknowledges that capital flows are happening despite the government's restrictions on such money movements. The incoming money ends up fueling an underground lending industry that has in turn helped sustain bubble-like prices in many Chinese real-estate markets.\par \par \uc2 Until now, the PBOC's policies had encouraged the upward momentum in the yuan, frustrating Chinese exporters and leaving authorities worried about overvalued property markets. For three years after it abandoned a rigid fix to the dollar in June 2010, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gradually increased the midpoint around which the yuan traded, essentially engineering a creeping appreciation. Whether they did it to appease criticism from the U.S. or to wean the economy off export dependence, the moves created the ingrained expectation that the yuan was always going to rise.\par \par \uc2 The result was that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  became a speculator's dream. Not only did it deliver strong returns, but the appreciation also occurred in a very stable, orderly way. "It was a one-way bet with no volatility," said \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  global emerging markets economist Alberto Ades.\par \par \uc2 So, while the market response to these moves reflects the continued influence of Chinese policy makers over the yuan's valuation, it doesn't necessarily mean their actions achieve desired results. Speculators interpreted those actions in ways that were misaligned with the government's objectives.\par \par \uc2 What will happen when the trading band is widened and the yuan allowed to float more freely? If investors now assume that the one-way bet is off, it's not hard to imagine the fund flows abruptly reversing, producing an outflow that could choke off financial liquidity by a magnitude more than the PBOC desires. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be careful what it wishes for.\par \par \uc2 There's a long way to go before the yuan can be declared a fully free-floating, internationalized and convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But even before we get there the authorities' dance with markets as they undergo a new experiment in financial openness could have profound effects on both the Chinese economy and that of the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 -- Follow Michael J. Casey on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @mikejcasey. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's Real Time Economics blog,  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/ }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 27, 2014 20:35 ET (01:35 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140228ea2s000jc\par }