\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Weakens Further as China Banks Buy Dollars-3-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei and Richard Silk   \par \uc2 1,036 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 16:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pushing reforms to make the yuan more responsive to market forces with an unusual tactic: more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up its week-and-a-half-long effort to weaken the yuan by instructing big state-owned banks to buy dollars aggressively in the mainland market, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at both Chinese and foreign banks. The move caught the market by surprise and caused the yuan to register its biggest one-day fall since its revaluation in 2005, when China dropped a decadelong peg to the dollar. The yuan ended at 6.1450 per dollar Friday, down 1.5% since the beginning of the year and its lowest level in 10 months.\par \par \uc2 The volumes of dollar purchased by the Chinese banks were larger than expected, the traders said. "Nobody dares to trade against the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 . Nobody dares to buy the yuan heavily," said a Shanghai-based trader at a large foreign bank. Officials at the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  and the country's top four state-owned banks declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Though the drop in value is small by the fluctuations of many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is a massive shift for the tightly controlled yuan. The increased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to people familiar with the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's thinking, is part of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preparations to introduce more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets a daily benchmark for the yuan, also known as the renminbi, and allows investors to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value 1% in either direction from that set rate. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said recently that it plans to widen the trading range for the yuan in 2014 in an orderly manner. But with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  having steadily appreciated for years despite a few blips, speculators have been pouring money into China in expectation of continued gains. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to drive down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value to introduce wider two-way trading swings, put speculators off balance and relieve pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the people close to the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some analysts think the yuan has further to fall, though few view it as anything more than a temporary departure from a longer-term trend of steady appreciation, given that China's economy is still growing, though more slowly.\par \par \uc2 The yuan "faces further near-term downward pressure before a real two-way expectation is established," said analyst Jian Chang at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Still Ms. Chang said she maintains her forecast for the yuan to reach 5.95 per dollar by the end of this year, citing China's continued trade surpluses and capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 The persistent rise in the yuan in the past year has led to a large amount of speculative capital inflows, some of it circumventing cross-border controls. UBS AG estimates that so-called hot-money inflows amounted to more than $150 billion in 2013. That is out of $244 billion of total capital inflows last year, according to the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator. Data suggests the trend accelerated in January, as banks bought a net $76.3 billion from their clients in January, a one-year high, according to the regulator.\par \par \uc2 The influx has complicated Beijing's efforts to manage the economy, contributing to soaring property prices and injecting excess cash into the financial system.\par \par \uc2 China has a $3.8 trillion war chest of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which it could deploy to bolster the yuan, though \uc2 PBOC\uc2  officials have said in the past the bank would avoid routine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to make the yuan a more market-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan in theory helps exporters, lowering the price for their goods internationally when they are struggling to compete with cheaper rivals in countries including Vietnam and Bangladesh. But the people with knowledge of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking said the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not about lending support to the export sector, pointing to the yuan's long track record of appreciation. The yuan has strengthened more than 30% since its revaluation in 2005 and gained against the dollar every year since 2010, including a 2.9% rise in 2013 when \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some emerging markets tumbled.\par \par \uc2 "There is no reason to backtrack on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms," one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts inside and outside China agree, believing the exporters have lost a battle to keep the yuan's value low. "The exporter lobby has lost," said Damien Ma, an analyst at the Paulson Institute in Chicago, a think tank founded by former U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Hank Paulson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some exporters said that after years of rising in value, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to depreciate further and stay there to help their businesses.\par \par \uc2 "I was glad to hear about it, but I'm not going to go out and open a bottle of champagne," said Dave Hon, chief executive of Dahon Bikes, a U.S. company that makes folding bikes at factories in China's southern Guangdong province and mostly sells them to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hon said only sustained depreciation of the yuan would help companies like his. Like other companies that deal with multiple \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Hon said Dahon Bikes also has hedging contracts in place to limit the impact of the yuan's short-term volatility.\par \par \uc2 Uwe Hutzler, the German general manager at China-registered Heshan Bestway Leather, another manufacturer in Guangdong, was skeptical that the yuan's weakness would last. "The upward trend goes on," he said.\par \par \uc2 China has long been accused by critics in the U.S. and Europe of keeping the yuan weak to boost Chinese exports.\par \par \uc2 "The latest move by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  is yet another clear-cut example of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and the need for my legislation to stop it," said Rep. Tim Murphy (R., Pa.), who has sponsored a House bill targeting China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices.\par \par \uc2 Rose Yu, Ben Leubsdorf and William Kazer contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com and Richard Silk at richard.silk@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 28-02-14 1533GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140228ea2s000c6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
