\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Ends at 8-Month Low, Slides Nearly One Percent Earlier-2-\b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 11:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.1214               6.1224 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.1450    +0.39%     6.1284 \par             High         6.1808    +0.97% \par             Low          6.1275    +0.10% \par   SHANGHAI--China's yuan ended Friday at an eight-month low against the U.S. dollar on speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is stepping up efforts to push it lower. \par   On the over-the-counter market, the dollar ended at CNY6.1450, the highest closing level since June 27 when it settled at CNY6.1490. The dollar hit a 10-month high of CNY6.1808 in the morning, at which the Chinese yuan was 0.9% lower against the greenback from Thursday's closing of CNY6.1284, the biggest single-day drop since the 2005 revaluation. \par   The especially large move took the market by surprise, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  had set a slightly stronger reference rate for the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before trading started. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.1214, down from Thursday's 6.1224. \par   Traders said the slump was highly likely as a result of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-owned banks--buying up the U.S. dollar and selling the yuan. \par   "I suspect that's because of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's window guidance. It's taking aim at carry trade," said Liu Dongliang, a senior analyst at the financial markets department of \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Merchants Bank Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par   Speculative investors usually borrow the low-yielding dollar and convert it into high-yielding yuan, also with the hope of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains from yuan appreciation. "If the yuan drops sharply, these investors will incur huge losses," said Mr. Liu. \par    Another senior trader at a Shanghai-based bank also pointed to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central  bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , saying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's state-run four largest banks were aggressively buying the dollar. "We believe the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  is behind them," he said. "It wants to convince global investors that it's serious about deterring yuan appreciation expectations." \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Official data released this week indicate strong capital inflows continued in January. Banks bought a net $76.3 billion from their clients in January, a one-year high, according to data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Administration of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a unit of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  overseeing foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The SAFE said in a statement Wednesday that China continues to face massive capital inflows. "Domestic and overseas market participants continued their trading strategy of buying the yuan while short foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's moves to restrain the yuan come just before the start next week of the National People's Congress meeting, which could produce further liberalization of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan has risen over 30% since that 2005 revaluation, which ended decadelong peg, but since hitting a record high of CNY6.04 to the dollar last month, it has lost roughly 2%. The larger the number of yuan to the dollar, the weaker the yuan is.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.1570/6.1595 from 6.1440/6.1480 late Thursday, implying a 0.2% fall by the yuan over the next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Rose Yu at rose.yu@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 28-02-14 1011GMT\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140228ea2s0008n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
