\par\par \b \uc2 China PMI Data To Provide Cues to Copper -- Commtrendz -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 06:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0534 GMT [Dow Jones] China's manufacturing PMI data this weekend is likely to drive base metal prices and there is a risk the data could drag prices lower, says Gnanasekar Thiagarajan, director at Commtrendz Research. Weak economic data from China and a drop in the Chinese yuan, which has coincided with concerns about tighter credit availability to real estate developers in the country, has also weighed on investor sentiment toward base metals. China is the world's top consumer of base metals. However, LME copper inventories are "not comfortable" and lower copper prices could encourage expansion of projects and bargain buying, Thiagarajan say. LME 3-month copper is at $7,007.75/ton, down $17.25 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] The renewed interest in gold as a safe-haven asset is stemming from emerging market volatilities and geo-political risk in Ukraine and is likely to contribute to volatility in the market, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  says in a report. "The main bright spot for gold is likely to be ongoing demand from Asia, particularly if India's import restrictions are removed sooner than expected," the bank says. It adds that concerns about looming inflation pressures and/or economic instability, could prompt additional safe-haven demand in the region to preserve wealth. The bank expects that gold prices "will hold up in the near term," before gradually declining as uncertainty dissipates. NAB expects gold prices to moderate to $1,170/oz by the end of 2014 and further soften to $1,060/oz at the end of 2015. However, potential risks to the outlook include a faltering in U.S. economic growth, an escalation of geopolitical tensions and emerging market volatility. Spot gold is at $1,330.01/oz, down $1.79 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0507 GMT [Dow Jones] A mild contagion effect is taking place in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the onshore Chinese yuan falls the most in 9 years. The South Korean won, the Singapore dollar and the Malaysian ringgit, are all weaker after starting the day on a strong note. The fall of the yuan does have a small impact on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are centrally managed via a relative value \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket in which the yuan carries a significant weighting. But more likely, these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are displaying a kneejerk reaction as losses mount for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors who are heavily leveraged in long-yuan positions. These investors are likely to trim other long-Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions to reduce the risk of steeper losses in the event of contagion around the region. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0459 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore yuan traders are watching with anxiety Friday's steep rise of the onshore USD/CNY, anticipating that the offshore yuan traded in Hong Kong could soon follow suit. One trader said clients are passing on marked-up USD/CNH prices, implying that they were buyers looking for a cheaper price to buy the U.S. dollar. A couple of offshore traders say the 'pain threshold' in the USD/CNH market is the 6.1500 level above which there are large option barriers that, if triggered, could spark a massive dollar-short covering. In the onshore market, likely market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has manipulated the yuan to a 10-month low in the biggest single day move since 2005. The CNH exchange rate has dropped to 6.1320 against the dollar from its Thursday close of 6.0990 but has so far been spared the panic buying that was seen in its onshore counterpart USD/CNY. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] China's slowdown has spooked investors, but \uc2 Stephen Roach\uc2 , former chairman of \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  Asia, said one should worry about the U.S. instead. Roach argued in an online article that the China's slower growth is nothing apocalyptic, but a "welcome development" as the country rebalances to a steadier consumer-led economy. Unlike other deficit-prone emerging economies, China runs a current account surplus and has outsized \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as a backstop for financial contagion. "The problem... is not with China," he said, "but with the world -- and the United States, in particular -- which is not prepared for the slower growth that China's successful rebalancing implies." Roach said the U.S., which developed a "co-dependency" with China out of a marriage of convenience over the past decades, is now facing two choices: stays under-saved and suffers higher inflation and rising rates or moves away from excess consumption and invests in people and infrastructure. A tough one. (jacky.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] January and February was a tough period for investors, says Nomura. "That whiplash pain in your neck at least means you (and your portfolio) are still alive," the research house says, pointing out that most equity markets and regions are now back within a percent or two of their January starting points. On the bright side, says Nomura, "investors appear to have already shrugged off the weeks of U.S. data disappointment as merely a temporary weather effect." However, it warns that while the U.S. economy looks solid enough to pick up and propel global growth, that assumption is well priced in to markets, making it likely that equities will just move sideways for a few weeks, although first-quarter 2014 earnings will be "unavoidably clunky." (jake.watts@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong stocks start flagging in early trading before a sharp depreciation in the Chinese yuan socks investors, with mainland Chinese companies taking it on the chin. Hang Seng goes negative early on, down 0.3% to 22,760.81 at the midday break. Selling accelerates after a snap drop in the Chinese yuan, dropping 0.9% against the US dollar to 6.1808 just before noon in the largest intraday drop since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's revaluation in 2005. The Hang Seng China Enterprises Index, which incorporates mainland Chinese companies and is also known as the H-share gauge, sheds 0.82% to 9,875.71. Yesterday's improbably strong rally, coming at a time of widespread bearish sentiment on Chinese equities, was expected to run into trouble anyway. The 23,000 mark was expected to create a "strong technical resistance" for the Hang Seng Index, says GuocoCapital. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] Outflows from emerging Asia stock and bond funds picked up in the week ended Feb. 26, driven by a more-than-doubling of investor cash leaving stock funds, according to EPFR data. Investors pulled a total of US$2.1 billion overall, jumping from US$1 billion in the prior week. Stock fund outflows accounted for a full 85% of the losses, at US$1.7 billion. A report last week indicating Chinese factory activity at a seven-month low has revived investor fears on fallout from the country's economic slowdown. Funds focused on the country's stocks lost US$711 million over the week. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones ]The NZD/USD has continued stronger as local data continues to attract investors into the NZD, while a number of NZD-denominated bond issues this week are likely to be supporting, says ASB Head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Institutional Sales Tim Kelleher. "There has been massive demand for New Zealand dollar assets this week; the data has been good and we're two weeks out from a (likely) RBNZ hike so it's on people's radars." The pair is at 0.8390 vs 0.8369 this morning. Kelleher says resistance around 0.8425-0.8450 remains key while support comes in at 0.8325 to 0.8350. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com), @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] The yuan accelerates loss and is having its biggest intraday drop since China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform started in 2005. The USD/CNY touched 6.1808 before noon, hitting a 10-month high, and lifted 0.87% vs 6.1275 when the market opened this morning. Though the USD is paring gains slightly now to below 6.1700. Traders attributed the yuan's drop to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via big Chinese banks, which keep snapping up the USD and selling yuan, though the PBOC set the guidance for a slightly stronger yuan earlier this morning. The central parity for the USD/CNY was fixed at 6.1214 vs Thursday's 6.1224. "There are large buyers buying the dollar onshore, and the offshore market is forced to follow," says a Hong Kong-based senior trader at a Chinese bank. In the offshore market, the yuan, or the CNH, also tumbles after opening a bit stronger than Thursday close. The USD/CNH hits above 6.13, while it was below 6.10 at market open, and is now trading at 6.1253. (fiona.law@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0337 GMT [Dow Jones] China's manufacturing purchasing managers' index probably stayed just above the dividing line that separates expansion from contraction in February, according to economists' forecasts. The median estimate of 11 economists surveyed by Dow Jones was 50.1, just above the cut-off of 50. A preliminary reading of the HSBC China manufacturing PMI, a competing index, dropped to 48.3 in February. "The weakness in manufacturing partly reflects the welcome slowdown in credit and investment growth which began in the middle of last year," says Julian Evans-Pritchard, an economist at Capital Economics. That is positive for the long-term health of the economy, he says, but will weigh on the manufacturing sector this year. The PMI is due out at 0100 on Saturday. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 28 Feb 2014 00:34 EDT  China PMI Data To Provide Cues to Copper -- -2-\par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down fractionally at 14,920.65 at the midday break. The dollar began to fall under Y102 late in the morning session, and is now down sharply (at Y101.83), dragging Nikkei futures lower with it. The lead March contract is now at 14,890, possibly auguring a lower afternoon session open for the cash index. "The lack of transparency with the Ukraine political crisis and other factors are translating into a lower \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields and a weaker dollar," says Daiwa Securities chief technical strategist Eiji Kinouchi. "This is certain to continue to have a dampening effect on Japan stocks if it persists." 25/33 subindexes are lower, but machinery and precision instruments are holding their ground; Nabtesco (6268.TO) is up 1.7% at Y2,585 and \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) is up 0.9% at Y1,870, benefiting from better-than-expected Japan industrial production (+4.0% on-month in January, as reported before the market open). Overall trading volume looks light, however, at just 954 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 28, 2014 00:34 ET (05:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140228ea2s001ky\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
