\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Near Flat; Dollar Fall Under Y102 May Trigger Weakness -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2014\par 04:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down fractionally at 14,920.65 at the midday break. The dollar began to fall under Y102 late in the morning session, and is now down sharply (at Y101.83), dragging Nikkei futures lower with it. The lead March contract is now at 14,890, possibly auguring a lower afternoon session open for the cash index. "The lack of transparency with the Ukraine political crisis and other factors are translating into a lower \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields and a weaker dollar," says Daiwa Securities chief technical strategist Eiji Kinouchi. "This is certain to continue to have a dampening effect on Japan stocks if it persists." 25/33 subindexes are lower, but machinery and precision instruments are holding their ground; Nabtesco (6268.TO) is up 1.7% at Y2,585 and \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) is up 0.9% at Y1,870, benefiting from better-than-expected Japan industrial production (+4.0% on-month in January, as reported before the market open). Overall trading volume looks light, however, at just 954 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Southeast Asian stock markets open higher early Friday, heading for another gain on the last trading day in February following assurance from U.S. Federal Reserve head Janet Yellen that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's wind-back of its bond-buying program it not on a set course. Yellen's comments sent U.S. stocks to a fresh record. Among the most-changed stock markets in Southeast Asia is Indonesia's JSX, which gains 0.5% to 4591.9 while the Philippines' PSEi is also 0.5% higher, last at 6383.4. Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have performed well this month, led by a 5% appreciation of Indonesia's rupiah against the U.S. dollar. Malaysia's ringgit has added 2%. "Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also recovered nicely in February," DBS says in a note, with the rupiah "the poster boy of the recovery in emerging Asia, where trade and current account balances improved meaningfully on the back of better exports." (jake.watts@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan falls sharply again, after two days of relative calm, to hit a new 9-month trough of 6.1630 to the U.S. dollar. The move was likely triggered by another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to flush out speculative long-yuan positions. The yuan's fall accelerated as bank clients' stop-loss orders below 6.1500 were activated to cut losses for punters who are still holding on to the yuan. The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since last week been tightening the screws on speculators who have been betting on the yuan to appreciate sharply once the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fully liberalized. The recent attempt to weaken the yuan has resulted in a 2.0% drop in its value since Feb. 19 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began; last year, the yuan rallied 3.0%. The PBOC's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been classified by most market participants as a move to introduce greater two-way volatility for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the daily trading band is widened from its current 1.0% range. Spot USD/CNY is now 6.1530 from its Thursday close of 6.1284. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY extends gains in the morning, hitting a fresh seven-month high, on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/CNY is at 6.1384, up from Thursday close of 6.1284; earlier in the session it hit 6.1390, the highest since July 26 when it touched 6.1396. "Dollar buying is very strong. It's certain that some of them comes from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says a Guangzhou-based senior trader at a foreign bank. By doing so, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to prevent excessive speculative trading on the yuan, he says, noting that the SAFE, China's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -regulator, has made it clear recently that two-way movements will become the norm as the yuan has approached its equilibrium level. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is below the 102, as selling from Japanese exporters to lock in their earnings overseas has weighed on the pair, dealers say. "Some exporters which had taken a wait-and-see stance in the hope of the dollar's rally above 102.50 are selling dollars amid an absence of such a chance," says a dealer at a Japanese bank. Still, the downside remains solid at 101.50 as importers are constantly buying on dips. Trading is driven by commercial flows amid a lack of fresh cues, they say. The pair is at 101.91 after it earlier rose as high as 102.20. (tatsuo.ito@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD has been consolidating within a small range between the base of the Ichimoku Cloud support at 1.2625 and the 20-day Bollinger mid-resistance, which caps at 1.2653. But a breakthrough may come if USD/SGD ends the day below the Cloud - a bearish signal for the greenback. The market may turn more negative on the U.S. dollar as traders take a cue from dovish comments from the U.S. Federal Reserve chief Janet Yellen suggesting a pause in the withdrawal of Fed bond-buying is possible. A daily close below the daily Ichimoku Cloud could send USD/SGD to the weekly support at 1.2502. USD/SGD is now 1.2651 from its Thursday close of 1.2637. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR has slipped lower into the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone and may consolidate between a range of 3.2630-3.2820 in the days ahead. Overnight weakness in the U.S. dollar triggered by dovish remarks from U.S. Federal Reserve head Janet Yellen was likely the reason for USD/MYR sliding at Friday's open. Now the daily Bollinger downtrend channel may weigh on USD/MYR and take it toward the base of the Cloud support zone at 3.2500. USD/MYR is now at 3.2750 from its Thursday close of 3.2820. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW opens lower at 1,066.0 versus its last close of 1,068.8 and breaks down into the daily Bollinger band channel. A Friday close below 1,065 could indicate more gains for the won next week. Overnight, dovish comments from the U.S. Federal Reserve chief Janet Yellen pressured the dollar slightly lower. Ms. Yellen said the Fed might consider a pause in withdrawing bond buying measures if U.S. economic data continues to be weak. If this rhetoric is echoed by more members of the Fed, there could be a sustained slide for the dollar soon. South Korea's January industrial output data released earlier showed a monthly gain of 0.1% versus expectations for a 0.8% decline; on-year, industrial output fell 3.8%, worse than the 2.0% drop forecasted. USD/KRW is now 1,065.0 from its Thursday close of 1,068.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold is in a tight range after U.S. Federal Reserve Chair Janet Yellen's comments to the Senate Committee that did not indicate any change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plans about continuing tapering its monthly bond-buying program. "We remain bullish so long as gold holds above $1,307/oz support level, which is not only the last significant low, but also the 50% retracement of the late 2013 downtrend," Scotiabank says in a report. It tips resistance at $1,345/oz, a recent high. In silver, Scotiabank says a drop below support at $21.23/oz and closing below this level will be "concerning." It tips resistance at $22.17/oz. Spot gold is at $1,332.56/oz, up 76 cents and silver is at $21.28/oz, up 2 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] The offshore Chinese yuan strengthened further overnight to 6.0964 against the U.S. dollar as sentiment for the renminbi showed further signs of stabilization, notes Mitul Kotecha, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB. "Whether USD/CNH drops below its 200-day moving average around 6.0945 will be important to determine its trend over coming days," Kotecha says. If spot USD/CNH holds above the 200-day moving average, then further downside risks for CNH are likely, Kotecha predicts. Spot USD/CNH was recently at 6.1005, well below the near-seven-month high of 6.1309 hit on Wednesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says the flash euro-zone inflation in February (data due at 1000 GMT Friday) is expected to have slowed to a yearly rate of +0.7%, matching the October lows, and would finalize market expectations for easing action from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its policy meeting next week. The French bank says Thursday's data showing lower-than-expected Germany February preliminary CPI of +0.5% on-month (versus forecast +0.6%) reinforced its inflation forecast for the euro zone. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  expects the ECB to cut its main refinancing rate from a record low of 0.25% next week, with a possible move to a negative interest rate on the deposit facility (last at zero %). The bank says it is staying short euro against the U.S. dollar. Spot EUR/USD was recently at 1.3704. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down 0.1% at 14,903.85 after a fractionally higher start then dipping into negative territory. U.S. Federal Reserve Chair Janet Yellen's testimony before the Senate was generally positive for overnight Wall Street bourses, but nevertheless allowed the dollar to weaken (now Y102.04). "The market is naturally nervous about the Fed remaining steadfast about taking the training wheels off the wobbling economy," says CLSA equity strategist Nicholas Smith, noting Yellen's acknowledgement of signs of softness in the economy. "Frankly, I don't think she'll take her foot off the brakes unless the data give her a very strong reason to, however." Chris McGuire, CEO/CIO at Phalanx Capital Management, a Chicago-based hedge fund, says: "Strength in U.S. stocks has not tailed off, and while the dollar has crept back up, as long as it can hold around the Y102 mark, there shouldn't be too many worries from a Japan corporate earnings or confidence perspective. We are bullish on the market heading into March." 23/33 subindexes are lower, with exporters down; \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) is off 0.8% at Y3,670, while real estate shares continue to fall after sharply underperforming Thursday; \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) is down 2.0% at Y4,059. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 27 Feb 2014 22:26 EDT  Nikkei Near Flat; Dollar Fall Under Y102 May -2-\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 27, 2014 22:26 ET (03:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140228ea2s000q7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
