\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's attack on yuan speculators risks backfiring\b0\par\par\uc2 932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2014\par 11:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * C.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushes down yuan 1.5 pct since mid Jan, biggest slide since Greek debt crisis\par \par \uc2 * Economists doubt genuine end of yuan appreciation\par \par \uc2 * Abrupt drop squeezing speculators, especially in HK\par \par \uc2 * Talk of widening trading band imminent\par \par \uc2 * Previous widening failed to increase volatility or reduce meddling\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney and Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI/BEIJING, Feb 26 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rattled speculators this week by engineering a sudden fall in the yuan against the dollar, but economists warn that induced downside risk was no substitute for true liberalisation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes bolder steps toward allowing the market to determine the exchange rate, traders believe the correction could do little more than present speculators with a fresh buying opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has committed to letting the market determine the yuan's true value, part of a wider project to encourage international usage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rival the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's orchestrated reversal also has unleashed speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is preparing to widen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily trading band, currently set at 1 percent either side of a daily midpoint fixed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But even if the band is widened, traders doubt whether it can hold the yuan back from strengthening further, given the enduring ability of Chinese assets to attract capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 Since Jan. 13, the spot yuan has undergone an unprecedented fall of more than 1.5 percent, guided downward by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the help of major state-owned banks, which traders say were selling off yuan at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behest.\par \par \uc2 Wang Jun, senior economist at the China Centre for International Economic Exchanges (CCIEE), a well-connected think-tank in Beijing, told Reuters that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to deliver a clear message to speculators.\par \par \uc2 "It needs to tell the market, 'No more one-way rise for the yuan,' and introduce two-way fluctuations in the rate like other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have," Wang said.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator attempted to soothe markets on Wednesday afternoon, saying that the adjustment was "normal," resulting from market players independently unwinding their long yuan positions. But most participants believe this unwinding was defensive, triggered by state-owned banks' massive dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 As a relatively low-risk, high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has gained over 35 percent against the dollar since it was revaluated in 2005, the yuan remains a favorite among international investors.\par \par \uc2 The Greek debt crisis in early 2012 did provoke a brief swoon that saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lose 1.6 percent in six months, but it began to recover in July 2012 to gain as much as 5.5 percent by mid-January.\par \par \uc2 In reaction to this inexorable rally, speculators onshore and off built huge long yuan positions on assumption that the bull party would run and run.\par \par \uc2 Speculative foreign capital inflows appeared to gather pace from the fourth quarter through January, data from the State Administration for \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggested. Most economists expect the trend to continue this year, unless the yuan enters an extended decline.\par \par \uc2 GARBLED SIGNAL\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has attempted to deter yuan bulls in the past, but seldom achieved much success.\par \par \uc2 Most economists and traders still expect the yuan to appreciate between 2-3 percent this year, even given recent developments.\par \par \uc2 There is little economic justification for letting the yuan slide. Economists say depreciation would do little to help exporters, for whom the yuan's strength is a minor irritant compared with the explosive rise in labor, material, and rental costs.\par \par \uc2 GaveKal/Dragonomics research house economists Chen Long and Arthur Kroeber argued in a report titled "No, the Renminbi Is Not Tanking" that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's short-term goal is to mislead speculators into thinking the era of yuan appreciation is over, panicking them into liquidating long positions at a loss.\par \par \uc2 "Some observers have leapt to the conclusion that Beijing is spooked by slower economic growth and the recent sharp devaluations by emerging-market competitors, and now wants to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower to keep its exports competitive," they wrote. "We don't buy it."\par \par \uc2 TIMING THE MARKET\par \par \uc2 While most agree that widening the band would be a positive incremental step toward further reform, by itself it cannot introduce genuine downside risk so long as the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  and its allies at the major state-owned banks continue to meddle.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  widened the yuan's trading band from 0.5 percent to 1 percent in April 2012 the market confounded expectations by becoming less, rather than more, volatile.\par \par \uc2 That's because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to use the daily midpoint to restrain appreciation, setting it so that the spot rate remained on a tight leash.\par \par \uc2 In reaction, traders refused to do business anywhere near the midpoint and simply waited for the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to cave in and let the yuan rise again - a bet that has consistently paid off until recently.\par \par \uc2 "What the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  wants to do is inject more volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market; band widening seems to be the emerging consensus view on the simplest way to doing that," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a European Bank in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "But that is hardly going to be enough if the market believes the CNY is going to appreciate in the long term," he added. "What is needed is a more market-determined exchange rate." (Additional reporting by Lu Jianxin in SHANGHAI and Saikat Chatterjee in HONG KONG; Editing by \uc2 Simon Cameron-Moore\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-1T10:53:22.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140226ea2q007kd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
